      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>McDOUGALL'S Pewit SELF-RAISING FLOUR RAMTSRIM,D The examiner,</head>
        <p>McDOUGALL'S Pewit SELF-RAISING FLOUR RAMTSRIM,D The examiner,</p>
        <p>rALT, TAIGHTS Etll3lWivntpl MADE FOR EACH OTHER.</p>
        <p>JUSTIN McCARTHY,</p>
        <p>Author of "Dear Lady Disdain," "Red Diamonds," "Miss Misanthrope," "Maid of Athens," "Donna Quixote," "Mcnonia," ece.</p>
        <p>" Made for each other " was the general comment of the Roekshore gossips when it began to be talked about in that small seaport town or village that young Theodore Ronalds and Miss Cynthia Marston were often to be seen wandering together along the margin of the sea, and or some of the roads in its neighbourhood. The Rockshore gossips were very much pleased when anything happening in their locality gave them an opportunity of interchanging their confidential ideas. They never talked of anything or cared to think about anything going on in the outer world, and London to them Was as remote and foreign a capital as Constantinople or New York. For them, to adopt the words of Tennyson, the great world might "spin for ever down the ringing grooves of change." They let the great world spin anywhere or anyhow it would without taking account of its spinning,and the grooves of change did not send their ringing into Rockshore• ears. itockshore was a small settlement, hardly</p>
        <p>large enough to be called a town, and yet some- what too la-ge to be described as a mere village, and it was most picturesquely situated on one of the southern coasts of England. It was visited a good deal as a health resort, and during the summer and autumn months it had what might almost be called a population of strangers. "Made for each other" was not exactly the sort of comment which the relatives of Cynthia Marston and those of Theodore Ronalds were in a humour to make with regard to the occasional meetings of these young people. The greater part of the property in and around Roekshore was owned by Lady Annabel ,Marston on the one hand, and Mr. Thomas Ronalds on the other, and these names represented for Rockshore social influences which have always been coming into rivalry since modern civilisation began to grow into its present condition. Lady Annabel, for some years a widow, represented through her late husband's family and through her own the stately patrician order. Mr. Thomas Ronalds came of a thoroughly plebeian stock, and was the first of his family who had ever risen to be an owner of property. He had made a large fortune by means of some sort of business which he had started for himself and conducted to success first in one of the great provincial cities and after- wards in London. Then ho had bought large property in Itockshore and settled down there with his only son. Rockshore gossips had different theories as to what Mr. Ronalds's business had been, some raintaining that it was house-building, while others asserted that it was brick-making, and others again were of opinion that it was the retail linen trade. How- ever that might be, it was quite certain that the man himself had no pretentions to be admitted into the society of Lady Annabel Marston, and that Lady Annabel was in the habit of assuring her friends that she had never spoken to that person in her life. Lady Annabel was a woman of some culture s.nd with much generous and womanly feeling, despite her old-fashioned objections to persons who had made money in trade, and were so push- ing as to settle in her near neighbourhood. Her daughter Cynthia was at this time a beautiful and graceful young woman of twenty, who had been educated in Paris and in Germany, and seemed to have all her mother's finer qualities without any of her aristocratic prejudices. She had made the acquaintance of young Theodore Ronalds, then about twenty-two years of age, at some of the charitable bazaars and literary gather- ings got up during the summer and autumn for and by the visitors to the place. It may be admitted at once that she was much attracted by the young man's personal appearance, by his spirited manners, and his sincere love for art and literal-re and for manly sports of every kind, especially &amp;Dating and yachting. It did not occur at first to Lady Annabel as being in the least degree probable that casual meetings of this kind between her daughter and the son of a retired tradesman could possibly lead to any romantic love affair,at least on the part of Lady Annabel's well-brought-up daughter, into whose ears had been instilled from her earliest childhood the great lesson that she came of a patrician house, and must look down upon the lower orders. On the other hand, Mr. Ronslds entertained a strong resentment towards Lady Annabel, because she had always declined to take any notice whatever of him, and he had satisfied himself that for all her patrician dignity Lady Annabel's annual income was what he considered very limited, and he knew that her daughter could not bring any- thing like a large fortune to her husband. There- fore, the rulers of both the rival houses—if we may be allowed for the moment to assume a possible rivalry between the house of the patrician lady and the house of the retired tradesman—were equally hard and fast set against any love-making between the daughter and the son. All the same, the love-making went on, and, to bring our preliminaries to as prompt a close as possible, it may be mentioned as a certain fact that Cynthia Marston and Theodore Ronalds were absolutely and devotedly in love with each other.</p>
        <p>Let us hear what the lovers had to say for themselves as they walked early one morning in the lovers' month, May, by the shore of the sea. If they had not met then and there by actual appointment, we may feel quite sure that the meeting had been arranged for through the medium of love's telepathy.</p>
        <p>"My mother says," Cynthia softly murmured, "that she will take me away to Paris or the Riviera or somewhere abroad, and keep me there until I promise never to see you again, and that she will take me away from any place if you should once be seen there. She told me so last night, and I know her well enough to know that she will keep her word." "And I have told my father," said Theodore, "that I will never consent to give you up unless you want to give me up, and that I know you will never do."</p>
        <p>"Never!" said the girl, with a firmness which was all the more thrilling because a its quiet tone.</p>
        <p>"My father and f have not got on well together lately," Theodore went on to say, after he had given a glance of impassioned gratitude to her as he heard her one word. "He wants me to go into busineus, the same kind of business that was his, but I have no taste for business And no gift of business, and I know I should make a muddle of it—and in any case I hate it. I want to make my own way in life. I should love of all things to be a poet or a painter or a sculptor, but I have no genius for art of any kind. I want to be a soldier, to go to some war, and do brave deeds like those knights of old about whom we used to read, to prove myself worthy of you, Cynthia, and then to come back and claini your love as one who has done something deserve it."</p>
        <p>"Quite like another Clamle Melnotte," Cynthia replied to his ardent sperch,with a gleam of that humour in her eyes which had always been one of her charms for him. "Yo 2 are to go to the wars,Theodore,and come hack litter having made, just in a few weeks, a name and a fortune; you are to break into the wedding party, and claim me as your bride, and say you can shew more money than the personage whom my family have chosen for me. But then, you see, Theodore, the scene could not be complete in our case, for I have told my mother that I will never become the wife of any man but you, if I were to die an old maid at the age of ninety."</p>
        <p>"Your promise ig my life," Theodore gaid. "But I cannot ask you to he my wife, dearest Cynthia, until I have done something to prove that I am worthy of your confidence and your love.:" .</p>
        <p>"You have these already with the fulness of my heart." "But I must prove to you and yours," he declared, with renewed energy, "that I am worthy of you, that I can make a way for myself in life, and that you will have a strong arm to lean on when I ask you to be my Ivife. I some- times feel a mad inclination—l am speaking unite seriou.iv now. Cvnthia—Vo run away am]</p>
        <p>enlist In the Army and refuse to be bought out, and thus prove to my father that I am determined to make a career for myself, or to lose La the attempt a life which is worthless to me without you." ; "If you take such a course as that," Cynthia I said, stopping in her walk, laying her hand. upon ; his arm, and looking fixedly into his face, "you will only make my life a prolonged misery to me. Let us wait, at least for a time, and hope for better days. Let us be patient, and trust that my mother and your father may grow into a better mood and come to have loom sympathy , with the children whom they have brought into this world, whose lives they now threaten to blight for ever. Trust in me, my own Theodore, and believe that you are my own, and that I will never fail you; and I shall trust in you, and know that you will never fail me. You must choose is career, and I will help you in your choice of it: We can wait for a good whir() yet —we are both so young—and trust lo some happy ; chance or change which Heaven in its mercy may yet bring to us. : Then after a while the lovers parted. They had come to a firm understanding, mainly on Cynthia's inspiration, that they were to wait and to hope for the influence which might be ; wrought on the minds of their parents by theit • fixed resolve to remain engaged to each other; that in the meantime Theodore was to make up his mind as to the career best fitted for him, ; whether in the Army or out of it, and to pursue :it with all his might and main. Cynthia was intensely anxious that Theodore should find as soon as possible some fitting career, and should give himself to it. She clearly saw that under- neath all the romantic extravagance which some- times glorified his hopes and his language there liy a genuine strength of purpose and of iatellectual capacity which spoke highly for a C &gt;ming success in life. She was very unwilling iitdeed that he should join the Army, even though lis were to enter it as an officer, but she felt at tic same time that the mere threat of his doing sis, especially if he were to insist on serving in tie ranks, might alarm his father into consenting that he should become at once engaged to the girl he loved, and set about studying some congenial pursuit. In the meantime Cynthia made up her mind that she too must study how to occupy her time with something better than the ways of London tishionable life in the season and the local saciety of Rockshore and its neighbourhood when there was no other place that Lady Annabel cared to visit. Her lover's suggestion that his best course would be to enlist as a private soldier and strive to make an honourable career for him- self, had put into her mind an idea with regard to her own life which began to grow more and more upon her. Why should she not become a hospital nurse? Why should she not give up a life of vapid, unmeaning idleness, and adopt one which would at least offer her the means of hnding a helping hand and doing good to her fellow human creatures ? It was not likely that under the most favourable conditions her lover and she could be married just yet, and how could she spend the interval of waiting better than in devoting herself to work which would smooth the life of the suffering and make her a more helpful and a more womanly woman for the remainder of her existence? If this course did nothing more for her, it would at least give her something better to do than have to bear with her mother's expostulations and reproaches, and to listen to Lady Annabel'a frequent and very , outspoken expressions of dislike for Cynthia's lover, and contempt for that lover's father. On one point the lovers were quite resolute, and they were equally satisfied in mind and heart as to the rightfulness of their resolve. When Cynthia should reach the age that would make her legally independent of her mother, and ' mistress of her own fate they were determined to get married and fight the battle of life together. In the meanwhile each was to find some work of self-development and self-improve- ment to qualify them for reciprocal help, and to fit them for bearing a noble part in that great life-battle. Our lovers may have been very romantic and very unwise according as the world lays down its rules of practical wisdom, but they were thoroughly devoted to each other, and at the same time devoted alike to the task of making their lives well worthy of each other in the highest sense. A wiser pair of young people, according to that ordinary standard of wisdom, might have set themselves to the task of cajoling and deceiving their parents for the time is the hope that some lucky chance or other might bring the parents round to a more merciful con- sideration of the feelings of the children. But our two lovers were resolved to be frank and outspoken from the first, to tell their parents that they were pledged to each other and would never withdraw from that pledge, and then bear the consequences whatever they might be while striving their best in the interval to make themselves worthy of their mutual love and their eommon aspirations. When Theodore told Mr. Ronalds of his resolve, the father was direct and explicit in the expression of his sentiments. "Of course, you can do as youlike," he said, "seeing that you are a grown man, and are entitled to make a fool of yourself if you please, but I haven't made money in order that it may be given avyay to the daughter of a woman who would spit at you and Inc if she might. You ought to know, without my telling you, that the girl is only willing to marry you because she wants to get hold of your money, seeing that Lady Annabel is rather hard up and has been down on her luck lately, so they want to get hold of a young fellow who they believe is likely to be rich. Just tell them exactly how things stand, and see what a change there will be. Theodore could hear no more just then without losing all control over his temper, and as he could not trust himself to attempt any reply the conference broke off. Lady Annabel was equally distinct, after her own fashion. , "Go and learn to be a nurse if you like, you absurd girl," she said; "it may perhaps turn your attention away from this insane idea of marrying the son of a vulgar shopkeeper who only pretends to be in love with you because he thinks that if I could be prevailed upon to give my consent to the marriage wee might be able to introduce him and his ?tither int,s that society which these London tradesmen who make money are always yearning to get into. Go and learn nursing if you like—it will keep you out of trouble from which you could find no escape if once you had got into t." So that conference, too, came to an end. The exchange of confidences, vows, and en- couragement between the two lovers had taken place one bright morning towards the end of spring. Almost immediately after Lady Annabel and her daughter went up to Londou; and Theodore was left for a time to the corhpanion- ship of his father. The father, to do him justi( e, made himself as kindly in manner as his nature would allow him to be, and felt strong hopes that before the London season, then opening, should draw to its close and Lady ;Annabel return to Rockshore, Theodore might have wholly recovered from his absurd infatuation, or Lady Annabel might have picked up some husband for her daughter in those West-End circles which Mr. Ronald s never had the slightest ambition to enter. That the lovers found means of communi- cating with each other it is hardly necessary to say, and the heart of the elder Ronalds might perhaps have 'been touched if he could only have known that the influence of Miss Marston and nothing else had kept his re' ''ious son from carrying out his project of jct.. -g the ranks of the Army. 1</p>
        <p>Summer and autumn passed away, and in early November Lady Annabel and her daughter returned to Rockshore. Lady Annabel was coming to her seaside home in better spirits than she ' had enjoyed for some time before. She was already looking out for an especially brilliant Christmas party in Reel:shore, and had arranged that a number of London friends should be her guests, and should astonish the noel:short natives in that .gladsome season. Among the guests who had accepted her invitation was one in whom Lady Annabel's hopes were now centred. This was Lord Edmond Gervase, eldest son of a peer of old descent, to whose title and estates Lord Edmond must succeed in the ordinary course of life. Lord Edmond was a handsome, attractive, and clever young man, and he had during the London season evidently been much taken by the face, figure, and manners of Cynthia Marston. Lady Annabel had never heard Cynthia say a single word in praise of the young man, and had not ventured to offer any suggestion on the subject, but she had planned the Christmas festivities with the special purpose of having him for her guest, and thus throwing him into the society of her daughter. It was part of her Profound diplomatic scheme that the two</p>
      </div>
