      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>1908.</head>
        <p>1908.</p>
        <p>mould be brought togetner at itociishore, above all other places, because she thought that the companionship of the ginceful young patrician in that very place could nbt but impness her daughter with a sense of thb contrast between him and the son of tho vulgar tradesman who hai fastened himself on noel:shore.</p>
        <p>Lady Annabel had made up her mind that Theodore, the son, must be in reality quite ail vulgar a person as his rather,- and that it onl3 needed the shoelc of the contrast in the trades- man's own neighbourhood to cure Cynthia of her inexplicable and half-insane infatuation. B is hardly needful to say that Lady Annabel kept all these deep designs entirely to herself, for she thought she knew enough about those silly romantic girls to know that any premature hint of the kind would only arouse once more in her daughter the spirit of contradiction. But reckoned much on the gradual eirect to ". wrought on Cynthia's mind by the daily contrn. at Christmas time between the young patrician and the occasional sight of the vulgar old trades- man as he tramped forth from his hall door and took his walks abroad. Cynthia had meanwhile been devoting herstlf too the practical study of the care of the sick poor, and Lady Annabel put up with all that as passing nonsense which was likely to pass away all the sooner for being left wholly unnoticed and unreproved by parental authority.</p>
        <p>The later days or that November are likely to take an abiding place in the annals of !loci:shore. Even now the old inhabitants of the place wiD tell the passing stranger that while the previous storms of 1870 were only terrible floods and nothing more, the korms of the November which we are now describing amounted to a positive inundation. The illage of Rockshore was so situated as to front the open sea just where a grmt outstretchino barrier of rocks on either side seemed to the unskilled eye of the stranger to be a magnificent rampart or bulwark on either side protecting the inhabitaits from the effects or a sudden tempest. So, indera, these rock barrirTs might justly have been regarded if ihe winds had always been considerate enough to blow in such a manner as to direct the rush of the waves towards the outer side of either rampart. But it sometimer happened that the winds, on whose doing one can never count, would direct the main rush of Ult. tempestuous billows straight at the opening, the natural passage or hall by the walls of rook. Then it seemed as if the -Whole ea were straining its utmost to make a way for itself with all its volume straight into this opening passage, and thus to pour its inexhaustible flood over the homes of the human beings in Roekshore</p>
        <p>A poet of my younger days, who is not so well remembered now al I think he deserves to be, once described in thrilling lines a sight that might be seen "when the victor sea with a thunder shout through a breach in the rock-wall rolls." Ido not believe that poet had ever east eyes upon Roclishore, but he described a sight that might have been seen in all its devastation and terror during that November storm to which our story has now brought us. Lady Annabel's beautiful and picturesque house, with its pretty surrounding lands and gardens, stood not very far inland and on the western side of Rockshore itself. Lady Annabel and her daughter both loved the place, especially because it was so near to the sea, and had never thought of any possible danger from that familiar propinquity. That evening the sky had been especially clouded and gloomy, and the sea heaved and groaned in a manner which, to more experienced eyes, might have seemed ominous of coming danger. Cynthia had some of her favourite poor and sick to look after, and she hurried into Reekshore, not alarmed by the weather-signals of danger, but, indeed, finding a certain fascination in the awesome swelling and groaning of the sea. _ _</p>
        <p>Lady Annabel- was in the -habit of going to bed rather early, and she did not feel bound to sit up for her daughter as that young wanderer had always her own well-trained maid to accom- pany her in her wanderings. Lady Annabel was turning over the pages of a fashionable London newspaper and thinking of bringing her day to a close, when suddenly she heard a loud knocking at the hall door aud simultaneous ringing of the bell. Her flrat thought was that something must have happened to Cynthia, and, the idea became all the more alarming when she heard the voice of a man at the hail door asking eagerly whether Lady Annabel was in, and adding some words of alarm which did not quite convey their meaning to her ears. She opened the door of her sitting- room and was rapidly descending the broad flight of stairs when she saw one of her men- servants hurrying up towards her, and at the 'same moment she distinguished the figure of Theodore Ronalds standing ;n the hall. "Lady Annabel," the young man exclaimed in tones which were respectfully modulated for all his evident eraotion, "you must leave this place at once! There is a terrible sea coming, and you have not a moment to lose. You and everybody else must leave this house at once and come . with me and take shelter in our house, where I hope you will be safe enough. There is not a moment to be lost. Tell all the servants," he said, turning to the man in livery, "that they must leave the house at nnre and felleer me.</p>
        <p>TAKE NO OTHER.</p>
        <p>Beware of Substitutes ; ask for " OWBRIDGE " when buying Lung Tonic and</p>
        <p>accompanied her, "wilt go with you too, and put ourselves at your disposal. There will be plenty of work for us women in striving to help the families in the poor cottages which must by this time be overwhelmed by the flood." Lady Annabel made a sudden movenient as if she were anxious to prevent Cynthia from risking herself in such an effort, but' the quiet, resolute 1 sak of the girl dissuaded her, and immediately Theodore and his hand of volunteers, men and women, had left the house and were hastening on their way to the help of the victims from the still rising sea. Before many n.inutes had passed the news was brought to Mr. Ronalds in the presence of Lady Annabel and many others that Lady Annabel's house had been completely washed through by the waves, that most of the windows and window-frames had been dashed in, and that the grounds were for the moment a sort of sea-water lake. It was announced at the same time that the waves had broken over all the low-lying streets and houses of Rockshore, and that the long esplanade or promenade on the verge of the sea had been completely torn away and actually swallowed up by the furious billows. This esplanade had constituted for, some years the Chief pride of Rockshore. It had stretched along the whole sea-front of the littlo bay on either side of that natural barrio/7 .of rocks which has been already described. The waves were now breaking over shapeless messes of up-torn earth and over the gardens and the village streets which lay behind. That.was indeed a night of terror for the population of Rockshore. Mr. Ronalds, senior, did not go to bed that night. He said that he should have liked much to go into Rocksbore himself and lend some help, but he did not think he could be of much use at that sort of thing, don't you know, and he fancied he might be rather in the way than other- wise. He was most courteous and attentive to Lady Annabel, who was too much bewildered in mind to have any clear idea of what was going on, and was only too glad to take herself to the set of rooms which had been assigned to her for the use of herself and her maids. But she could not help seeing, even in those moments of dis- traction, that she was as handsomely quartered al if she had been in a palace, and as she declared that she would not go to bed, Mr. Ronalds told her that he would let her know immediately of every piece of news which might be sent to them from the flooded region during that eventful night. With the early morning came the cheer- ing and reviving news that the worst of the storm had spent its fury, and that, although the destruction of property was great, there had been but _few .lives Inst. Some men ..of local</p>
        <p>allowing a clear 1005-out upon the sea. l'hen Is- importance came to see Mr. lionaids as the the light of the pale moon which bad risen morning grew on, and told him and Lady they saw that the sea had broken over the double . Annabel that the loss of life among the poorer , barrier of rock, and was already flooding the cottagers might have been very great but for the lower part of the village. The flight of our little splendid services rendered by Theodore Ronalds fugitive party became more and more precipitate, and the band of volunteers under his command, until at last they reached the open gateway of and that Cynthia and the nurses accompanying the Rona.lda's house from which Mr. Ronalds and her had been the means of saving many poor a number of his servants hurried forth to meet women and children from instant death or from them. The London tradesman behaved with a death hardly less rapid caused by homeless extreme courtesy to Lady Annabel and her exposure to drenching spray and consequent daughter, and assured them that his house wns perilous chills. It was clear enough already absolUtefy at their disposal, and that they would that Theodore Ronalds and Cynthia Marston were be perfectly safe there from the fury of the still the hero and heroine of the hour. Indeed, the rising sea. With friendly clutehings of their declaration was put into uttered words on arms and other spontaneous demonstrations, of simultaneous impulse by the patrician lady and welcome, he told the nurses and the servants the London tradesman. that accommodation should be found for every one "Your son is a hero! " Lady Annabel of them until the storm had spent its rage. exclaimed. "We shall have ri rough time of it, my lady, "Your daughte-s is is heroine, my lady!" Mr. the elder Ronalds tvent on to say, "and we Anti Ronalds cleclared,with something like enthusiasm have to make a sort of camping ground of this in his tone. . "::ihe has proved herself to be house to-night, but your ladyship and Il.liois efficient-1 put my money on those who are Cynthia shall be taken good care of and made efficient." That was indeed the principal article comfortable in every way. I Del e that by noon of Mr. Ronalds's life-creed—his faith in the to-morrow or thereabouts the worst of the danger will have been got over." efficient. ferhaps at this very moment there may have been stirring in the minds of the ‘ But is my house in danger ? " dy Annabel patrician lady and the retired tradesman some asked, in a gasping voice, conviction like that which the gossips of Rock- "lf there were any people in you, house just shore had expressed so often about our hero and now, my lady, they would be in a bad way, tor a heroine—"they were made for each other." have just heard that the sea is going through The Tiochshore people went to work to restore it and all over it." as quickly as might be the destruction caused by "You have saved may mother's life," mid the storm. The reconstruction of the esplanade Cynthia, clasping Theodore's hand, utterly would, of course, have to be a slow and costly • regardless of lookers-on; and, indeed,the lookers- piece of work, but in the meantime eve effort r 3 on were not then troubling themselves very much was made to repair .the injuries caused to streets and cottages. It is only just to say that Mr. about the ways of two young lovers. Ronalds shewed himself not merely generous Theodore returned the. clasp with interest. but even lavish in his contributions towards the "Now," he said "I must go back to Rockshore. repairs of the damage. Mr. Ronalds had been 1 ' a • I shall have lots of work to o inlending a profoundly impressed by the efficiency his son helping hand." had displayed during the night of storm, and in the work of restoration which followed it, and "your‘`But lifey o u,,rilliiff,,,, yinoudraltiigfeei!..':, Cynthia ploaded— he began to feel for the first time a confidence "Lehall keep out of daoger,' Theodore said, in his son's capacity to make a career for firmly. "I understand all this sort of thing, and himself. the people will want cool heads and strong arms There was to be an especially gladsome Christ- mas at Rockahore if only to mark the gratitude viloltliii,iiidteettheativ,,.l ICeonidne,mtehcamh,elspoiliirg of you u Imvni felt by the settlement for the manner in which s!iew you what to do." the threatened ravages of the flood had been All Lady Annabel's men-servants, inspired by happily mitigated. Most of the inhabitants were Theodore's energetic manner and several of Mr. amazed to hear that the Christmas was to be Ronald's household, promptly offered to follow spent by Lady Annabel, her daughter, and her in Theodore's footsteps and do whatever work he immediate friends the young patrician already might command them to do. mentioned among 'the rest, under the hospitable "These ladies and I," Cynthia declared, with roof of Mr. Ronalds. Lady Annabel's house was ouiet euer..-y-looidne, at the nurses who had in full process of internal restoration but it</p>
        <p>I-IVAICA naraly oe in a condition to receive the Christmas guests for that year. Mr. Ronalds had boldly invited Lady Annabel to make his house the scene of her Christmas entertainment, and Lady Annabel had not only graciously but even gratefully accepted his invitation. Every- body in Rockshore saw what was coming. The readers of this tale can already see as much for themselves, and can see also that the story is coining to an end. Lady Annabel had recognised at last that the reciprocal love of her daughter and Theodore was too strong and too deep for parental interference, and she had come, moreover, to entertain a warm and genuine admiration for the courage, the capacity, and the noble nature of the tradesman's son. Mr. Ronalds, for his part, had become satisfied that in the career which he now felt certain his sou was destined to make he could not find a better helpmate than Cynthia Marston. When the great Christmas reception took place in Mr. Ronalds's house it was quietly made known among the guests that Theodore Ronalds and Cynthia Marston were engaged, with the full consent of their parents, and that the marriage was only to be delayed until Theodore should have seen his way to the opening of some definite career in which he might have a chance of proving himself worthy of his happy fortune. There would be no want of money, Mr. Ronalds frankly declared to every inquiring friend; but both Theodore and Cynthia were alike resolved that Theodore was not to lead a life of more happy idleness. Lord Edmond Gervase was one of the guests, and had learnt even before his arrival that there was no inducement left to him for the making of any attempt to become a suitor for Cynthia's love. Lord Edmond, how- ever, had sot set his own heart greatly on the enterprise, and had been made hopeful mainly by the gentle encouragement of Lady Annabel, so that he bore his disappointment with perfect good humour, and did his best to promote the general happiness of the Christmas celebration. The crowning point of the Christmas evening came when Mr. Ilonalds the elder suddenly drew Cynthia under the mistletoe at the door of the supper-room and kissed her on the forehead, ramediatelv after Lady Annabel draw Theodore itomeds under the same auspicious branch and offel•ed her forehead to his respectful lips. We may glance far enough into the future to say that the wedding of the young lovers came off in due time, and that Theodore Ronalds and Cynthia Ronalds, his wife, seem to have a lutppy life before them.</p>
        <p>[TILE END.]</p>
