      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>Saturday January 4,</head>
        <p>Saturday January 4,</p>
        <p>come, may Annanm, come, I implore you Miss Marston is safe. I met her in Roclishore just now. She is in no danger. Trust yourself to me, and come at Once." There was something of command in the attitude, the manner, and the voice of the young man which overawed the aristocratic lady. If ever any man could have come with an authorita- tive and inspired warning of danger and claim to confidence, it seemed to lie the tradesman's son, who just now stood before her. Without uttering a word of remonstrance or demand for explanation, she followed Theodore down the hall door steps. Even in that short time the servants had begun to form a little crowd in front of the house, for some of the elders remembered stories of former inundations, and even the youngest among them had some con- ception as to the calamity which such ,an event might bring. "Come with me at once," Theodore called again. "Do not think of anything else—there is not a moment to be lest. Come with me—come!"</p>
        <p>Theodore gave his arm to Lady Annabel, and they made their way in rapid flight through the garden gate and inland along the broad road lead- ing up to the slope of the hill high on the side of which stood the large modern house built for the London tradesman. Lady Annabel endeavoured to halt for s moment while she entreated Theodore in panic-stricken accents to tell her about the safety of Cynthia, and Theodore, still compelling her to move on, assured her that she need have no fear,that he knew where Cynthia was going to when he met her, and that they would call for her on their way and take her with them. He also told Lady Annabel that he.,liad not said anything to Cynthia about the threatening danger, because he knew that she was going out of its way and that there would be ample time to see to her safety; and meanwhile there would be no use in giving her a needless alarm. Already the alarm seemed to be spreading, for the crash of the waves was becoming more and more tumultuous, and Theodore saw numbers of persons hurrying from the streets of Rockshore and making towards the higher ground. Before they had gone very far they came to an old- fashioned school-house on the side of the road. Here Theodore, leaving Lady Annabel, ran and knocked at the door, and in a moment Cynthia, with two women dressed as hospital nurses, and some delicate-looking children, came out. Theodore, in the calmest tone he could assume, told them of the threatening danger, and declared that they must all come with him. He hurried them along, giving them no time for questions or for listening to explanation, until they came to a sudden turn in the road high river the village</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>RESULTS OF SATURDAY'S MATCHES.</p>
        <p>G. T. P. New Zealand THE NORTHERN LEAGUE. *Hunslet *Ebbw Vale 0 3 9 Leeds 1 0 2 *Hull 2 0 10 \Varrington *Widnes Halifax 3 6 24 *Dewsbury *Oldham *Keighley *St. Helens 2 4 16 Bramley 0 1 3 *Runcorn 3 7 27 Huddersfield 0 0 0 *Hull Kingston R. 2 1 7 *Leigh *Wakeileld T 9 2 10 Rochdale H. 0 2 6</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>Warrington Beaten at Hull. Injury to Lees.</head>
        <p>Warrington Beaten at Hull. Injury to Lees.</p>
        <p>Warrington made the long journey to Hull on Saturday with a strong team. Fish was the only notable absentee, his place being taken by Jordan. Fine cold weather prevailed and a crowd of about 7,000 assembled on the Boulevard ground. Play was of a very even character up to half-time, but shortly after the interval Lees, the Warrington captain, had to leave the field with a damaged collar-bone and the borne team then won comfortably. Teams : Hull—Taylor, back ; Parry, Cotterell, Bruce, and Rogers, three-quarter backs ; Anderson and Walla 3e, half backs ; Herridge, Holder, Owen, Major, Fulton, and Carroll, forwards. Warrington—Tilley, back ; Jordan, Dickenson,Taylor and Brooks ; three quarter backs ; Boardman, Thomas' Shugars, Belton, Harmer, and I'. Boardman, forwards. Referee Mr. W. McCutcheon, Oldham. Touch judges, Messrs. d. Fowler (Leeds), and A. Sarith (Halifax). . .</p>
        <p>Early in the game, Wariington were pulled up, and Bruce landed a capital goal. The visitors, however, got the lead a few minutes afterwards, when, from a sc. Jruniage on the home line, Lees scored in a clever fashion. Thomas failed with the goal kick. Before half time a pretty bit of passiug- in which Anderson, Wallace, and Bruce took part, resulted in a fine try by Rogers. He ran over in the corner, and from a difficult point Bruce inadea good shot for goal, the ball only just passing outside. Hull crossed over with a lead of 5 points to 3, and the game had only just started when Lees injured his colic-- bone through tackling Wallace, and he was unable to take any further part in the play. The home team managed to double their figure, Cotterell scoring after the ball had been nicely handled by Wallace, Rogers, and Bruce, pod Rogers after failing to improve the try, kicked a splendid penalty goal. Result :—Hull 2 tries, 2 goals (10 points), Warrington I try (3 points). ST. HELENS BEAT ELEVEN OF BRAMLEY. Played at St. Helens, in boisterous weather. Flramley started the game two men short—Webster, the half back, and one or the forwards having missed the train. Briers was the first to score for St. Helens from Matt Creevey's pass.but Pennington missed the goal. Bramley were penalised for off-side, and C. Pennington lauded a goal. The visitors rarely got over the half way, and Matt Creevey raced over the line, Pennington failing to improve. Pope put on the third try for the " Saints "and Matt Crosvey landed a good goal. The visitors' forwards played a good game in the loose, and Goodwin was prominent in the centre. Trenwith near the interval tricked the opposition, and scored, the goal kick failing. Half-time score—St. Helens 2 goals, 4 tries (16 points), Bramley nil. In the second half the visitors irld the help of a strong wind, but St. Helens were quickly attacking. Bramley put more spirit into their play, and from a cross-kick by Ross, Goodwin ran over at the corner. Padgett failed to land a goal. Result—St. Helens 2 goals, 4 tries (16 points), Braruley 1 try (3 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>RUNCORN v. HUDDERSFIELD.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN v. HUDDERSFIELD.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn. before 1,000 spectators, the ground being in a frozen condition. After play had been contested in the home half for a short period, Unsworth cleverly f, o t away, and passed to Moran, who scored at the corner, Jolley failing at goal. Runcorn attacked again. Sykes chsirged a kick down, but Richardson kicked across to Padbury, who rushed over and scored at the corner. Richardson failing to augment. Jagger and Burchell got away, but a rush by the home forwards ended in a scramble on the H,uddersfleld line, where Deakin got over, Richardson again failing at goal. Just before the interval Padhury added another try, which Richardson failed to improve upon. Half-time score : Runcorn 1 tries (12 points), Huddersfield nil. Alter the interval Runcorn were soon attacking. Jolley sent the ball to Whitwatu, and his kick being charged by Padbury, the latter had no difficulty in scoring. Jolley added a goal. Bartholomew made a grand run into the home half, but a dribble by the Run- corn forwards ended in Unsworth scoring a try, Jolley adding a goal. Burchell, Kitchen, Sykes, and Charlas- worth gained ground for Huddersfield, and the visitors at this point were within au ace of scoring. Walker, however, got away, and passed to Moran, who ran more than half the length of the field, completing the effort with a grand try, Jolley adding a goal. Result : Runcorn 3 goals.l.tr.i,e.l (27 p.v!l?ts), Huddersfield nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>WIDNE4</head>
        <p>WIDNE4</p>
        <p>Played at Widnes, before 1,000 spectators. The home backs got going, but Hughes intercepted, and after running strongly he passel to Heath, who scored behind the posts. Plimmer placed it goal. After some scrambling play Cara got possession and scored. G. Aspey failed at goal. Just before half-time Widnes were penalised, and Plimrner placed a goal. Half-time score :—Vork, 2 goals 1 try (7 points) ; Widnes, I try (3 points). Ea:ly in the second half Widnes attacked, and York were penalised near the line. G. Aspey had difficulty "I placing it goal. The home teara kept up the attack, rid passing by Noon, Lloyd, and Barber ended in the latter scoring at the corner. Ni) goal followed. This gave Widnes the lead by one point, but it was not for long, for Plimmer placed a penalty goal from a difficult position. Some exciting play followed in the last few minutes. Just before the whistle sounded faylor marked, and G. Aspey placed a goal. Result :—Widnes 2 goals 2 tries (10 points) ; York, 3 goals and I try (0 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCH.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCH.</p>
        <p>WIRRIN4;TON " A " v. CADISIIE.III.—This match at Wilderpool on Solurday ended in a runaway victory for Warrington. The " Wirepullers " should have Met Leigh Shamrocks in a Combination match, but that team was engaged in a qualifying cup-tie of the No them n Union Challenge Cop. Cadishead proved but poor oppo,ition for the locals. whos• forces were weakened by the demands of the seniors at Hull and sickness. The %Warrington men soon opened the scoring for Tinckler crossed after fine passing. Cadishead rAied, and Daintith, having a kick charged, the ball ors taken over and the scores equalized. From this point the hone side pursued an aggressive career, and after Lloyd had scored, the result of good passing, Tinckler added his second try, rind this time Forster added the coal. Cadishetd made several ineffectual attempts to cross, and the attack being beaten off, Tinckler raped over for a third try, Warrington leading</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>NEW METHOD of creating Nerve Strength.</head>
        <p>A NEW METHOD of creating Nerve Strength.</p>
        <p>ADVOCATED BY DOCTORS.</p>
        <p>Without doubt the advent of Dr. Cassell's Tablets marks a new era in medical research and discovery. This great medicine, lately evolved from the prescription of a noted specialist, is absolutely the last word in body-building and invigorating specifics, and the hundreds of cures of so-called hopeless cases of nervous prostration, anemia, loss of flesh, debility, premature decay, indigestion, kidney and stomach troubles, every day being effected through its medium, bear witness to its efficacy and undoubted value as a household medicine, and are causing doctors to advocate its use. For safety, reliability, and power of restoring the jaded faculties and building up flesh, health, strength, and vitality in young and old, Dr. Cassell's Tablets have never been equalled, and it says much for progressive medical science that a remedy so eminently safe and effective has been brought to light and perfected. A box of Dr. Cassell's Tablets only costs bid., obtainable at all chemists.</p>
        <p>at half-time by 14 points to three. Turning round, Warrington, with the assistance of the breeze. early paid a visit to the Cadishead quarters, and Lloyd soon crossed. Jolley scored twice in quick succession, one of which was converted. Ashton showed an able defence for the visitors, but he could not cope with such deter- mined work as that of the " Wirepullers,". and a round of passinr, lei up to Duckworth scoring, and Forster adding th goal. Daintith next lowered the Cadishead goal with a clear drop, and near the close the visitors took advantage of some loose play of the home backs, and an unconverted try, was recorded. Final : War- rington, four goals eight tries (32 points) ; Cadishead, two tries (six points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>MEW YEAR'S DAY MATCHES. ALL BLACKS' TOUR.</head>
        <p>MEW YEAR'S DAY MATCHES. ALL BLACKS' TOUR.</p>
        <p>Wales 0 New Zealand THE LEAGUE. _ _</p>
        <p>-- .--..—... Salford lB Wakefield l2 Broughton If Rochdale H 6 Swinton Runcorn Oldham l2 Leeds 7 Halifax 32 Hull Kingston It I3arrow Bradford Hunslet 24 St. Helens Wigan</p>
