      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>FIRST INTERNATIONAL.</head>
        <p>THE FIRST INTERNATIONAL.</p>
        <p>Wales Beat New Zealand. The All Blacks were particularly anxious to will their matches against the representative teams, and they would be keenly disappointed at th, result of their first international at Aberdare on Wednesday. It must be admitted, though, that Wales were the superior team, and ought to have won by more than a single point. The New Zealanders opened the scoring, for Kelly gathered a en -s-kick from Messenger and got over, but the goal kick failed. Dai Thomas neutralised this with a brilliant try, but W. Wynyard scored for New Zealand, and with Messenger kicking a goal they led at the interval by 'OA points to three. In the second half, though, the Welsh pack was seen to great advantage and especially in the loose, some of their dribbling being particularly fine. Francis scored from one dribble, and just before the finish Jones obtained the winninn, try, neither of them being von- verted. The All decks were thus beaten by nine points to eight. There were 15,000 spectators. Warrington Fall at Runcorn.</p>
        <p>There was a nu"; crowd at Runcorn on Wednesday for the match with Warrington, and the weather being dry live or six hundred spectators accompanied the visitors. Runcorn were at full strength, but IVarrington had to find substitutes for l'illey, Taylor, Lees and Thomas, the first three being injured and the latt.r playing for Wales at Aberdare. Mr. Edmondson of Bradford, was the referee and the teams were as follows :</p>
        <p>Runcorn : Harrison, full back; Deakin, Butterworth, Unsworth and Moran, threequarters ; Jolley and : Watton half-backs •, Walker, Richardson, Padbury, ' Dixon, Woods and Macdonald, forwards. 1 Warrington : Jordan, full back ; Fish, Isherwood, 1 Dickenson and Lander, thi oe-quarters ; Brooks and ' Jenkins, half-backs ; Shugars, A. S.Board man, P. Board- man, Harmer, Belton and Naylor, forwards. Warrington won the the toss and had the assistance of a strong wind in the first half, and after some exchange punting Jenkins marked in front of the Runcorn goal. Fish tried a long shot, but made a poor attempt. Aided by the b. .e Warrington attacked strongly, Jenkins missing apa when well placed. The backs got going again but this time Isherwood was at fault and Watten cleared to centre. P. Boardman took play back to the home quarter with a strong run and Isherwood had a drop at goal which passed outside. Determined efforts to score were made by Warrington and after Dickenson bad been tax ed close in, Brooks marked in front of goal. Fish tried again but kickcel badly for the object. Runcorn could not clear their line and in a scramble clese to the li, ' I'. Boardman forced his way over with the ball at his toes amid loud cheers from the Warrington supporters. This time Brooks took tl a goal-kick but was wide of the mark. The reverse roused the home team to grea t efforts. Unsworth passed to Moran who took play to the Warrington quarter with a ape. ly run down touch. Smart passing by the whole of the home three-quarters looked promising, but Harmer intercepted at the critical moment and kicked down the field. Runcorn tried bard to get level, but a tine passing round by Brookes, A. S. Boardman, Naylor and Lender put Warrington on the attack again. Li Idor h ' a chance a moment later,but was tackled at the corner. Then Fish picked up smartly and seemed a certain scorer, when Harrison got across and push I him in touch. Smart play by Jolley and Walker nearly brought a score, Brooks relieving with a flying kick. Interval : Warrington 1 try, Runcorn nil. .</p>
        <p>ii-lch-arilson re-started, and the Warrington forwards led by Stingers rushiag to the home line. Runcorn wele in a tight corner till Jolley cl: :red with a huge kick past centre. The home ttarn rmde good use of the wind and before five minutes I d elapsed Dickenson made his first blunder in the match whic'l put Jolley in possession near the line and Runcorn were on level terms. The scorer failed at goal. There was some desperate work after this and Fish made a fine run into the home half where Harrison saved by a deliberate knock-on which went unpunished. Warrington attacked with vigour but a penalty kick gavf3 Runcorn relief. After this Runcorn had the advantage and by lofty punting kept Warrington in their own half. Jordan made some plucky saves, but about three-quarter time Jolley dropped a grand goal from the base of the scrum and gave them the lead. Warrington made one more effort, but Landor lacked dash near the line and was tackled. Final : Runcorn 1 goal I try (5 points), Warrington I try (3 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>WHOLE FAMILY WITH RINGWORM.</head>
        <p>A WHOLE FAMILY WITH RINGWORM.</p>
        <p>Another Marvellous Zam-Buk Cure.</p>
        <p>Not only is ringworm one of the commonest of children's ailments, but it is also one of the most distressing and " catching." It is well, therefore, that parents should know of a sure and certain remedy that lies at their hands wall within the</p>
        <p>reach of every purse. Mrs. Bedworth, of G, Star Alley, High-street, West Bromwich, has had practical experience of this unique remedy, And is, therefore, in a position to give advice to other parents still in ignorance of it, whose children may even now be suffering acutely from some painful skin disease. To the " West Bromwich Chronicle," Mrs. Bedworth told the following story : in June last," said Mrs. Bedworth, " ruy eldest, a boy of nine, was noticed to be suffering from a bad head. In spite of every attention the sores got worse, and the other children, even the little baby, were also attacked. The disease developed into ringworm and became very acute. The elder girl, five years of age, suffered most. Her bend became an awful sight, and the hair began to fell off, as did also that of the other children before I tried Zam-Bulc. Then in a short time a wonderful change was noticed. It was some time before I heard of Zatu-Buk. and I tried several things in vain. For a time I spent Is, a week on ordinary ointments, but instead of the cchßf ohialurdii kll children rroobeernonne otswhkagssele.. rl tat?itrln,h.heg better eerraneavtdlebrecyloar bs nif b:stlsedoaadn i got crysre,doesacesn w decided tddr) ene:ei,hooti le aso'fxnttaw.hr yee:szTzt aahtrnme. oh yn.e Bilk. I sent for a box, and the first applications did a lot of good. The disuse was arrested, and the children's heeds began to get better. I used another box, and thp improvement still continued. Baby's head soon got all right, and the scurf was all cleared away. I than got another box, but before I had used it all the children were perfectly well. Their hair was growing again, and the eldar girl, who had the disease worst, has now got a splendid head of hair. Thep 11,1 re had Ito trout)? e Fciace, an the en me vas satire!!! !lite to Zant 8u7,•."</p>
        <p>Don't experinlent with cheap salves which are useless in skin disease, and are not even recom- mended as being of the le ist good for skin disease. Zam-Buk has peculiar medicinal properties which distinguish it from all else.</p>
