        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>:111 to be 7</p>
        <p>A Popular Winter Resort.—Egypt is every year becomine more favoured as a fashionable wia ter resort, and the Empress EnOnie will spend ROMP part of the winter there. It mist recall to the Empress the occa- sion of her first visit to Egypt., when, accompanied by the Emperor, ehe went, there for the openine of the Suez Canal in 18(11. It was the Empress, rather than the Emperor. who Was the figure head in that great opening ceremony, and which was naturally made notch of in France. Incidentally it gave to the world of fashion a new colour, or at least one with a new name —" ean de Nil." After the return of the Emperor and Empress, a great ball sees given at the Tuileries, at which the Empress wore a gown resembling in colour the waters of the Nile by moonlight, and for long after- wards the semi-green and grey we know as " eau de Nil " Was the one and only fashionable colour in Paris. Fashions for the Little Ones.—Children's parties are in full swing and one notes with satisfaction the dis- appearance of the poodle fashion in tying little girls' hair. The latest, and very much morebecorning notion, is a straight band across the top of the head, ancle rosettes above the ears. When the dress is of white or cream, the idea is for this ribbon band and rosettes to match the shoes and stockings,which are usually carried out in pale blue or ride pink. Black shoes aad stock- ings are quite out of favour for the little ones, so also are white, which at one time were' almost universal. The January Sales.—At•the sales, remarkable bereains . are being offered, especially in fors.also in fur-lined,and fur-trimmed garments. This is no doubt owing to the mild weather we experienced up to Christmas the majority 'of women's putting off purchases until the January sales. Next after furs, in the matter of bargains, come Paris model gowns in velvet, which so far have not " gone off well." The simply of evening coats too, appears tin have far exceeded the demand, and vast, numbers of charming specimens are marked at just half the price lasked for them before the sales. The great demand of ; the moment. however, seems to he for furs, and when making stich purchases especially when the costly varieties are concerned, we shonld see to it that they are clearly described in the bill. Then should the ' "bargain " torn out to be other than what we bought , it for, in the hurry, Marne, and commotion of sale time, ' we have the bill at our beck to help us to set things ' straight. - The Lace Coat.— The sleeveless and unlined lace ennts in black, white and cream, which with Perisiennes have for some time past taken the place of the lace bolero, for indoor wear over smart afternoon and evening gowns, are being offered in shopland at exceedingly low prices. The wise will refuse these cheap and nasty imitations of a mode only suitable for the wealthy ; those who can command the best kinds of lace, such as ' Chantilly, Allenzon, Maltese and Spanish leeee, or the .-o:sch desired Trish crochet, and who can afford suitable nes, over which to wear them.' Up-to-date Umbrella Handles.—The craze for rock, Crystal, silver and ' jewelled handles, is past. and carved ivory. handles are now considered correct. Of course the more wonderful and beautiful the carving, the more such handle is ti', be desired and sought after, and certainly it has a consider- able advantage in being in far better taste than the flashy jewelled ones end the showy silver handles with their very undesirable propensity for soiling dainty gloves. "Acquired " Hair.—Nowadnys we think no more of wearing hair culled from some other person's head, than a feather culled from a fowl's tail, moreover we are quite candid about it, and make no secret of the fact that all the curls, loops, and coils that cote make pp our elnhorate coiffures, are not home grown. And in shopland this Christmastide, this state of affairs seems to have been fully recognised, for it has been no uncommon event to SPA clusters of cerls, or loops, marked up. consnicnously as " Suitable Presents " or " Popular Gifts." Cholmondelev Castle and Eaton Hall.—Practical cokes as a rule, are of very elementary form of humour, and the " burgling " of Cholmondeley Castle, by nparty of guests from Estrin Hall, would certainly not have .been approved of be the late Duke of Westminster. . The Cholmondeleys, like the Grosvenors, are of very *fees! old standing in Cheshire, but the Castle—near Melee,'— " ' is not modern built, like Eaton Hall. The late Duke , of Westminster commenced the building of Eaton Hall , in 18(17, and made it one of the chief undertakings of i --- fi his life. It took nearly fifteen veers to complete, and ! Warrington Man Sent to Gaol. is without exception the most palatial conntry mansion i of modern times. It war whilst Eaton Hall was in 1 At the Borough Police Court on Monday, before the building that the Marquies of Westminster, whose first wife wars a dreighter of Harriett. Duchess ' of Snther- Mayor are other magistrates. William Smith, 31, Plumptomstreet was charged with stealing from the lann—the great friend of Queen Victoria—was created a duke. - works of Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co., some time The Premier Trish Duke.—The Duke of T einster it between December 21st and 22nd, eight new brass steps is understood will sitcceed Captain Walter 'Warine' as. Master Irish Viceregal of the horse te the Lord Lieutenant at the d T • and various ,ether articles of brass valued at RI 155., ' ^ . the property of Monks. Hall and Co.--Nir. F. G. Taylor age on March lsCt,ortunrdt" •H e will "1°1)11" his coming of (I(lessrs. Henry Greenall and Co.), appeared to prosecute rank in the s the youngest nobleman of his on behalf of the company and Mr. 'P. S. Steel repre- only six peerage. He'. cane' into his inheritance when sented the prisoner. Years of 'age and h e h •ne been' ell Mr. Taylor said the man was an assistant electrician managed during his m, inorittve thsetatDesukenvuilll be on