      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <p>I</p>
        <p>V</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>1/4.</p>
        <p>TEN'S</p>
        <p>. , '</p>
        <p>A COLD ON THE CHEST.</p>
        <p>I Take Peps Before Worse ills Arise</p>
        <p>There is never any telling where a cold on the chest may end, Pneumonia, pleurisy, lung,inflamma- tion. or even consumption itself may be the penalty of a day's neglect. In Peps, the wonderful medicine in handy tablet form, we have a Sure preventative of evil nftPr chill has gripped you, and a remedy for the cough and tightness on the chest. Mr. A. Green, 65, St. George's Road, Aldershot, for 22 years in the Military Police, used Peps successfully for a frightful cold in the head and chest. He writes—" It was nothing but, cough, conch, onngh, night and day. Ikty head was com- pletely stuffed up, so that I could scarcely breathe. My friends advised runny different remedies, but none of them did me any good, and I did not know what to do to get rid of it. A chemist to whom I explained my symptoms strongly advised me to try Peps. Well, before I had half finished thnt box my cold was completely broken up. What is more, I feel in better health bodily than I have done for a long time. I could not wish to feel better than I do to-day. it is Pens that has done this for me. I -have since given Peps to my wife and children, and to several friends." .</p>
        <p>Miss E. Malawi, the Links, Ilook Heath, Woking, tried Peps for a weak chest after a had attack of infinenn. She writes :—" I cannot speak too highly of Peps which have made my chest quite strong again ; also I do not take cold so rprulily now." After returning home after wet, fog, or frost, let Peps attack that shininess shout the chest, and soreness of the throat, which are often the first signals of chest trouble. Place n Pep on the tongue on going to bed, and so avoid the coughing that has previously broken your rest. rake with you a few Peps in their sealed silver jackets when ont-of-doors in bad weather. There is nothing "just as good as." or even like Peps—ls. lid. and 2s. gd., from all chemists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>THEFT OF BRASS.</head>
        <p>THEFT OF BRASS.</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, January 4, l9OB</p>
        <p>WOMAN'S REALM.</p>
        <p>DRESS-FASIIION-HOME.</p>
        <p>SOME BIIIDAL FASHIONZ.</p>
        <p>We are always in search of some new thing, and we generally sue:4d in finding it, says the Queen. Satin remains the one fabric which no bride can do wrong in choosing for her wedding gown. It is often used to fashion a long train coming from the back of the shoulders in a point on each side, and in that case the gown is of flimsy chiffon, and even sometimes of net or tulle, mingled with beautiful lace. A wedding is more or less a pageant, and great attention is consequently paid to the general effect of tho procession as it marches up the church. The bridesmaids arc sometimes each drc,ssed in a dif- ferent colour, but this is not usual, though as a novelty it appeals to some. Very lovely embnai. 'clerics are introduced on the bridal dresses, in which pearls and silver threads often mingle with mother-o'-pearl sequins. French bridal gowns are generally much more simple than English ones, but in both countries the Empire mode has a certain following. Very ,pretty is the effect of the fulness of the skirt wlen of some • light fabric, coming frorn between the shoulders. There is a quaintnels-In these short waists which is both girlish and elegtnt. How TO RENOVATE A DP:ESS. , - ! We are giving this week a suggestion for •,renovating an evening gown of soft satin or silk, • making a dainty little dress that could be worn for a small dance or party. A soft while silk or satin, the, skirt of which is most probably soiled at the bottom, might be treated in this way. The skirt is cut up in deep vandykes, which are bordered with silver tissue ribbon and very small silver ball fringe, the skirt being finished with a deep flounce of white silver-spangled chiffon.</p>
        <p>.re remuced, and tilt very kw and trimmed with a fancifully shaped yoke- piece of the material, outlined with narrower silver tissue and the ball fringe. A chemisette of spangled chiffon, drawn up with silver ribbon, fills in the low-cut bodice, and the quaint sleeves of chiffon are caught up into a small puff at the top, the long flowing ends being finidied with a silver tassel. Nitw BONNETS FOR THE BABIES.</p>
        <p>Picturesque designs in bonnets for the babies are among the many attractions in the shops, and the new shapes are quaint, yet practical. The tiny baby still has the traditional little cap, but for the very small girl there are bewitching little bonnets of many kinds, and the simplest designs are, perhaps, the most charming. The lines are, as a rule, closer than they were a little while ago, with a rather close-fitting cap for foundation, and sonic soft, becoming trimming around the face, but without the large poke brims or the wide frill effects. 'Possibly tho quaintest and prettiest shapes, says the Tribune,. are those finished around" the face with a rather flat ruche, but trimmed over the ears with soft bows or chola, or with little clusters of some dainty flowers. Plain bonnets of heavily-corded silk are bor- dered by bands of fur and softened by choux and strings of chiffon and lace, and in the more ex- pensive varieties there are delightful little bon- nets of ermine, with narrow upstanding bands of the ermine turning back around the face, and lined with lace, and with side-bows and strings of Liberty satin. Bright little coats of red or Royal blue-faced cloth, braided simply in black, are being worn with roll brim hats of smooth, soft kit match- ing the coat in colour, and trimmed in soft blatk satin scarves. White caraeul coats, with caps to match, are modish both for small girls and small boys, and for the very little boys are hats of very soft white felt, with round bowl crown, sharply rolling brim, a little ruche of lace set next the hair, cholla of lace over the oars, and satin strings.</p>
