pt the title." I I'll stop that, Betty." "I know; and now he'll get all back without She murmured something incoherently, and marrying me, for, of course, I shall release him. then. a.s she stood with downcast eyes, he said, in But perhaps he won't accept his freedom." ' quite a new tone: Something in the girl's face and tone revealed "How pretty you are, Betty. I don't wonder to the man the secret she had hitherto guarded at Ned's infatuation." so zealously. 1 "It's the money he wants." she said, bitterly. "Why. ,you love him!" he exclaimed. ! " l'm a secondary consideration." "I do,' cried the girl, with crimson cheeks, ' • "You want to be loved for yourself alone. Ah! half ashamed, half glorying in the confession, that's a crumpled rose-leaf in every heiress's "I think I've been in love with him from the pillow. You must marry someone. And apropos first day we met. I think there's no one like of marriage, will you marry me? Everybody him in the world." I wants us to marry. Let us bow to the inevi- " But does he think the same of you? He table." , didn't ask you to marry him as the future Mar- "I don't are to marry for that reason, and quis of Fortingham, but as Arthur Oulton, the especially a man who must really hate me." clever but poor politician. He proposed to his "Oh. no. / don't. I confess I was annoyed cousin the richest heiress in England—not to an when I first heard of your existence. But a man obscure nobody. The Marquis wanted the mar- ca.n't hate a pretty, girl who's always nice to bins. triage. He's been urging him to propose to you, I know you don't love me, Betty, but you'll have so have all his friends; it was such a beautifully to marry. So shall I." simple way out of the difficulty. I daresay he'll "But you don't love me, Arthur." settle a small annuity on you if you tell bun the "Oh. yes. Come, I know I'm a sort of fortune truth, but he'll do no more." 1 hunter; hut I shall be in the next Cabinet if all "I must tell him, of course." I goes well