 she should do.ranted it, did she guess its import. hard to give it all up, she told herself, and yet ' - And now she was lying in bed wondering who!, was hard to riA: losina I Hold her tongue. nay Edward to keep silence, marry Arthur, and live happily ever after? Was . happiness possible while masquerading in a title to which she had no right, and allowing her hus- band to spend his own money under the impres- sion it was hers? On- the other hand, tell Arthur—and lose' him. He had nothing snore than a tolerant liking' for her; the manner of his proposal left no doubt on that point. Ile was in love with Lady Maud Tintern: Sybil had told her so. He was marrying her because he was ambitious. Politics absorbed him; a rich wife would help him in his career. I "But I Would make him a good wife— at leftist, I would try to," she told herself. " I 'wonder if it would be very wicked to bold my tongue. Oh. how hard it is to do right!" . ' She fell asleep at last, worn out with thinking, at'; awoke unrpfreshed and tired. Ilut she shook off the sick feeling of horror that came to her when she - remembered the-events al the previous day. and when she quitted the room looked much as usual. It was the custem aof the ladies to breakfast together in Lady ' Oulten's sitting-room. • Neither 'of her companions noticed anything amiss with Betty. the more as she threw herself heartily into the subjecf of the night's ball. Come what may, that at least she would' en- joy. It was given in her honour.. and was to outvie anything of the kind done in the county hr a generation. to wakcn memories of , the iaieties of fifty years ago, when the hospitality of the Marquis and his young wife made Fon. tingham Castle famous. Betty resolutely pot the decdsion out of her head, and devoted herself to her dress. Some slight alferation was, after all, needed, and with that , excuse she shut herself up with her maid and answered a note from Arthur asking to see her with a verbal message that she could not