      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <p>sne mass es, aim aon get tu enty.rour hours' respite; then ,she meant to enjoy herself. History repeated itself. She found him again sitting over the fire with a cigarette, in modern dress, too, .for he did not mean to dance. Again he rose, and. tcrsing aside his cigarette, placed a chair for her. " Have you decided?" he queried. At that moment the opposite door opened, and Arthur Oulton entered. He was dressed as a cavalier, a costume which became his handsome face and figure well. His eyes lit up as they encountered hers. "Betty,"" he cried. "here you are, then; I haven't seen you all day. By Jove! how, regal you look!" In his presence, at the sound of his voice, the question the girl had been debating all day de- cided itself. Arthur must know. The burden of the secret was more than she could bear. There would be no happiness for her unless he knew the truth. "Arthur," she said, and the firmness of her voice surprised herself, " Edward has made a new discovery about me, one which deeply con- cerns you." - She turned to Mr. Hanlan. "Tell him."</p>
        <p>So unexpected was her speech, that _Hanka reeled under it. and went white to the lips, lie had never doubted Betty would accede to his terms; she herself he had lost, but the fat rent roll of the Surrey estates would be hts, and after all there were other women in the world. By her decision he had lost everything. _</p>
        <p>"I will leave Betty to tell it," he said at 14t, breaking a long-drawn silence, and with one lo6k of baffled hate bestowed impartially upon them both, he stumbled out of the room.</p>
        <p>Arthur watched his departure with puzzled eyes. "What's up with Ned?" he demanded. "He doesn't look happy. Has he been propos. ;Dr again? " Yes; but a different kind of proposal. Arthur, you will not have to marry me to reign here. Yon interloping cousin, no longer a relative. ii going to vanish into the obscurity out of which she came"</p>
        <p>"My dear child, what are you talking about?" " I've just discovered that I'm an impostor. A female Arthur Orton. I'm not an Oulton; not your cousin; not the future Marchioness!" "What makes you imagine that?" In a few brief sentences she told the story Edward Hanlan had narrated the previous night. Arthur listened in silence. "Do you care for me?" he asked, with seeming irrelevance, when she _concluded.</p>
        <p>The girl raised her head proudly. "I care too well to act a lie to you for the rest of my life." He came closer and drew her to him. "You love me, and I never guessed it. I thought you were only making a marriage of convenience. But, touching this story, have you investigated it?"</p>
        <p>Betty shook her head. "I have," was the unexpected reply. "That rascal Scanlan came to me with that same storz, directly you were heard of. Said I was still tho riehtful heir, and if I would promise him £20,000 he would prove it. I promised. Then he told the tale, and I believed it; but, to assure myself, I went down to Brighton, and I found—" "You found?"</p>
        <p>"The whole story was a fraud—there was no death, no substitution, and the documents were clumsy forgeries. When I taxed the scoundrel he frankly confessed it, and had the impudence to suggest that, more artistically presented, the story might yet deceive the world and he earn the money. I told him to go to the deuce, and he seems to have gone to my dear cousin, and either deceived him or got him to conspire, pro- bably the latter." "Then—l am an Oulton. My father—"</p>
        <p>"Was your father. You, and you alone, are heir to title and estates. Betty, you've been very brave and frank, I'll be the same. I love you. I wouldn't confess it before, even to myself; but I do. If I were King Cophetua, and you the beg- gar maid—" . . _</p>
        <p>" Alice!" The startled cry broke from the old Marquis, who had entered and was gazing at his rranddaughter. "Alice, have you come back to me, Alice?"</p>
        <p>He took- her in his trembling arms. "Poor old man." whispered Arthur "he takes you for his wife. You're her image in that dress. I've seen a miniature of her in it. Do you doubt now you're his qranddaughter and the greatest heiress in England?" "I'm soething better," said Miss Chilton, softly. "I'mm the happiest girl in the world."</p>
        <p>[Tai Esp.)</p>
      </div>
