        <p>FOOTBALL FIXTURES, INVOICES RULED TPATOTERN. PROSPECTUSES. PROGRAMMES. JOHN WALKER &amp; CO., BEWSEY STREET, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The belated Christmas</p>
        <p>LORD CURZON cracker fired off the other AND A PEERAGE, day by Lord CURZON of Kedleston on the doorstep of the PRIME MINISTER has not fizzled Out SO soon as might have been anticipated. It, has, on the contrary, continued to fizzle from the old year into the new. The BE-VICEROY professes to be aggrieved because Sir HENRY CAMPBELL- BANNERMAN has not offered him a Peerage of the United Kingdom—has, indeed, even declined to grant this distinction when it was asked for. The casual reader of Lord Coßzom's letter would be led to suppose that the PRIME MINISTER has been discourteous to a political opponent, denying to him something that would at once have been con- ferred on another person in the same position. This, in fact, is the impression that the letter was intended to convey. The discourtesy is that of Lord CURZON. He suggested that the PRIME MnrisTEß is keeping him out of a position to which it has been customary to raise ex-Viceroy, that his own case is one of exceptional and unfair treatment, and that it is something which justifies him in reverting to conduct resembling that rude- ness of manner which was characteristic of him when he sat in the House of Commons and was expected to answer questions on foreign affairs.</p>
        <p>We believe we have the right to assume that Lord CuazoN's letter cannot have been written under a misapprehension. It is impossible for him not to have known that the ex-Viceroys who have seats in the House of Lords were members of that body before their appointment to Viceregal position. Their case might have been his own; but, needing only a title, and having a Peerage in prospect, he chose to be created an Irish Peer with the express intention of coming back from India, I not to the House of Lords, bat to the House of Commons. Much water has flowed under the bridges since those days, and more still since the earlier times when Lord CUR- ZON was amongst three sons of Peers, expeztant heirs of Peerages, who made a demonstration against being compelled to go to the House of Lords. Among the expectations that have arisen, and withered, in the meantime, was one of peculiar interest in its relation to Lord Cuazores anomalous attack on Sir HENRY CAmPBELL-BANNERMAN. The late VICEROY OF INDIA was to come back from India to lead the Conservative Party. He has come back to find that, in the old sense, there is no party to lead, and that if any party existed he would be, so long as he remains a Free Trader, as far outside it as is Lord Huall CECIL, who is a man of much more original genius than himself. But why does Loan CURZON blame Sir HENRY CAmPBELL-Bslimituls for keeping him out of the House of Lords? We can easily understand the conduct of the newspapers which assail the PRIME MINISTER for not giving an English Peerage to the Ex-VICEROY. They would assail the PRIME MINISTER for any reason whatever if the slightest excuse or provocation were offered. But when they say that Sir IifENRY CAMPBELL-BANNERMAN churlishly refuses to bestow, in this instance, an honour which has been customary under like circumstances, Lord CURZCN knows that they are speaking for the sake of party, and not on the side of truth. If every Indian Viceroy has a right to advancement in the Peerage when he comes home, Lord CURZON ought to have been made a Peer by Mr. BALFOUR, who was Prime Minister when he resigned. It was not until nine months afterwards that Sir HENRY CAmrnEra.-BANNEnmAx took office. What reason can there be that Sir HENRY CAMPBELL-BANNER. MAN should delight to honour the ilx-Vicrcaov who was passed over by Mr. BALFOUR ? He is not at present engaged in making Peers. He is not making Peers from his own party. Why should he make Peers from the party opposite? Why, in particular, should he select for special and unnecessary distinction an ex Viceroy who went out to a contented India and left an India which was on the point of revolt?</p>
        <p>A scheme is being =- ENLARGING THE sidered by which there will Comicin. • be a rearrangement ot the wards of the town and a possible increase in the number of members of the Town Cotincil. There are a number of electoral anomalies in connection with the wards as at present atranged. All the nine wards return three councillors each, and yet some wards have twice as many voters as others. Far instance H3wsey Ward has 855 electers against L ttchford's 1,751; Town Hall Ward, 962, againAt S. John's 1,752. Altogether there are 11,720 municipal voters. Whether the number of the wards or the representation per ward shall be increased is one of the main cpiestions to consider. Seeing the rapid growth of the town and the anomalies now existing a reconsideration of the whole scheme of dividing the borough into wards is essential. Before any changes can be made, however, the assistance and authority of the Home Office will have to be obtained.</p>
        <p>There are at least two THE TowN CouNcir. things whick act like the AND &amp;maim red rag to the bull in their effect on the Town Council. One is the question of taxing land values—which is always shelved and docketed under the heading " " and therefore beyond consideration— and the other is Socialism. The latter crops up rather oftener, and more unexpectedly, than the former; probably because we have two Socialist Councillors. They occasionally advance some pro- position which seriously perturbs the city fathers and it is at once dubbed and damned as Socialism.</p>
        <p>louncillor Stott has a proposal ready for Tuesday's Council meeting which will probably receive scant courtesy. It is nothing more nor less than that the supply of household coal should be municipalised just as gas and water are. Councillor Stott launched it upon the Council once before on a side-issue, arid was promptly ' ruled out of order, though not betore he had argued that if the Corporation could buy coal at 49. per ton below the retail price they might buy enough to supply the town, and thus save 14,000 householders about £ls,oooannually. Oa Tuesday Councilllor Stott will advance the proposition in correct form and endeavour to get the Council to recommend it to the Associ- tion of Municipal Corporations and through them to the Government. On the face of things, the municipaliz ition of the coal-supply seems as reasonable as the municipalization of other neces- sary coin,nodities such as gas and water. If the Corporation could buy coal at an advantageous price why should it not sell to the townspeople, even if it were taking another step towards the nationalisation of all moans of production and distribution advocated by Socialists F Opposition to the scheme of the most strenuous nature can be readily foretold. But will it not be largely opposition derived from self-interest The ques- tion is certainly a very wide one, but that is no reason why it should not be discussed within the narrow confines of a local Council. We look forward to an interesting, if not an illuminating discussion, on Tuesday evening.</p>
        <p>No gathering is more . in</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>OLD FOUL</head>
        <p>THE OLD FOUL</p>
        <p>keeping with- the spirit of this festive season than the Old Folk's Treat. and the scene presented at the Parr Hall on Thursday would have delightei the heart of a lover of humanity like Charles Dickens. Christmas generosity could not find a more fitting outlet than in providing good fare for those who, in the evening of their days, do not find themselves well blessed with this world's goods, and we hope the treat—which be it said to Warrington's credit is handsomely supported—will long occupy a prominent place amongst our beneficent institu- tions. The Mayor, with his usual kindliness, , inaugurated a new departure this year in , personally inviting the local army veterans to partake of the feast. Tnat the action was appreciated was evident from the ready. response of those who in the hey•day of life helped to fight their country's battles. The Mayor voiced what must be in almost everyone's mind when he expressed the hope that 'ere long dequate pro- vision will be made for our old soldiers to spend their closing years in comfort, instead of leaving them to the tender mercies of the world. It is a significant fact that several of the veterans present on Thursday have their abode in the Workhouse. Medals are something to be proud of, but they are ridiculously inadequate as a reward for those who have borne the burden and heat of battle. Public sentiment is awakening to a keener sense of duty towards these:men, and we haven a instance of it in the projected establishment of Regimental Cottage Homes—an object which Warringtonians in particular have good opportunities of supporting. This was perhaps the dominant reflection wiaich a visit to the treat called forth. Another was that appreciation of the bounty is as great as ever, the number of guests baiog an advance upon last year.</p>
        <p>In the preface to the 1908 PROTECTION STILL edition of the "Financial A DANGER. Moroi Almanack," the editor remarks that a year ago "we pointed out that, although the General Election showed that the majority of the electors were opposed to any tinkering with our fiscal system, the campaign was not over, since the restoration of Protection was supported by the Conservative Party. The events of the year have confirmed this view. The Tariff Raforrners have been most active, both on the platform and in the Press. Enormous sums must have been Ert their disposal to enable then) to tarry on this agitation during the year, while Free Traders, who do not see any immediate prospect of the success of their opponents, have boon apathetic. Individual interest always excites more activity than the general interest of the community, and, if the fallacies of Tariff Reformers are not continually exposed, the public, at the next General Election may give a majority to a party which has accepted policy of Protection, merely because some of the sections of the Liberal or Labour parties are dissatisfied with the G wernment for not having pressed forward their own particular hobby. Free Traders must continue to repeat that Protection and Preference mean dearer food and dearer raw material, and, as a consequence, increased restric- tion of production and commerce, which means'an increase of the unemployed. Last year we pointed out how the action of Britain in refusing to claim any preferential treatment from her colonies and dependencies had materially assisted in building up the Empire, by disarming or counteracting the jealousies of foreign nations. We are glad to note that our argument has been emphasised by, amongst others, Lord Cromer and Mr. Lloyd George."</p>
        <p>About a year since the Tint Cup OF Salvation Army founded an MISERY. anti-suicide bureau, with its</p>
        <p>headquarters in London,and branches in some of the chief cities both of the Old and the New World. By this means it is said to have saved over 1,500 lives. The statement is surely rather extravagant. A drop of 1,500 in what may be called the suicide rate would have been notable eniugh to attract wide attention. But we have heard nothing of any conspicuous drop in the Tate of suicide, and instead of saying' that the Salvation Army has saved over 1,500 lives it would probably be truer t say that it has administered comfort and good advice in more than 1,500 cases. Of those who even attempt suicide, a large proportion do not suCceed in carry- ing their object into effect, and the number of those who convince themselves that this is the only possible solution of life's problems for them, but who do not actually attempt self-destruction, most be very large indeed. Evidently, it is some of these last who have sought the consolations of the Salvation Army. It is noted that they do not belong to " the submerged tenth." Teose who I have got down to the very bedrock of poverty and misery either do not commit suicide or they do not pause to ask advice about it. Among those who have sought the Anti-Suicide Bureau the professions were strongly represented. Of what ' sort were the troubles that made them des- perate? Over 50 per cent, were distressed by financial worries or hopeless poverty. Eleven per cent, were victims of drink, drugs, 'or disease ; which seems a rather low pro- portion. There was 9 per cent, of melancholia, , generally produced by loneliness, and 5 per cent. of crime. Tragic, no doubt, were the stories that were told. An °sample is given. A man I ruined himself to save his son from prison. Then he took to drink, lost his employment, and broke his wife's heart. " Four times," he said, " I have walked across the bridge, looked into the water, thought of my wife, and felt that I would rather join her than live on as I had been doing." There must be thousands of cases not dissimilar ; but looking at the water is the nearest approach to ! self-destruction. Suicide is the exception, after all. There are no conditions under which life is not endurable, and there is generally a way out lof the gravest difficulty without approaching the tiates of Death. If the Bureau only shows the way to a modest proportion of its frenzied callers it has done something which perhaps no other human agency is capable of doing so well. S) long as there is trouble in the world, just so long will the Salvation Army perform a great service by holding out a helping hand to the self-condemned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>A woman in need is a devil indeed. —Pulitzer. Eove of success kills love of the good. —De Pressetzse.</p>
        <p>Forty is the old age of youth and the youth of old age. —Victor Ilugo. Money is like manure—of very little use except it. be spread.—Bacon. . . _</p>
        <p>Tile reward of one duty is the power to fulfil another.—Ben Azai.</p>
        <p>Labour prevents or cures all the maladies of the mind. —Emile Deschanel.</p>
        <p>Selfishness is the one safe receipt for a contended mind. —Costa° Hamilton. It is motive, it is the great puspose, that consecrates life.—Tennyson, The impelling forces of human activity are women and wealth. —Stuart Young. What is the use of health, or of life, if not to do some work therein ?—Carlyle. Humour, instead of lashing, laughs men out of their follies and vices.— Swift. Ouly very dangerous people tell the truth about themselves ; the wise try to tell it about other people; the discreet avoid it altogether. —3frs. Craigie.</p>
        <p>* * The frost has come at last and there seems to be every prospect of its staying with us for some time. Skating has already commenced, and lovers of the sport are looking forward to an excellent time this week-end. * ,„ *</p>
        <p>Remarkable activity has been shown by the Educa- tion Committee of the Warrington Co-operative Society this season in the provision of elevating entertainment for the members. So successful have the efforts in this direction proved, that the Committee have engaged the Parr Hall for to-day (Saturday), and there will be attractions for both children and adults. * *</p>
        <p>" There is an evil practice in this part of North Wales ""( a correspondent to Country Life writes) " which threatens to destroy one of its characteristic beauties. Anyone who has fished about here knows the extraordinary beauty of the mosses that cover the racks in the beds of streams and in the woods. This moss, is being stolen in great quantities and sent off by rail, presumably to serve as packing. It is no uncommon thing for as much as twenty sacks to leave one of our little stations, Arthog, in one day." *</p>
        <p>Under wills reported during the last two years a sum of about £10,500,000 has been left upon trust for charitable, religious, and other public uses. The bequests under wills not reported would probably make up the total for the two years to more than 11,000,000. The annual average for the twenty years before 1006 had not exceeded £2000,000. The total for 1001 has exceeded £5,500,000. Those who leave considerable sums for public uses are, for the most part, childless persons.</p>
        <p>* * * The health of the town ought to improve rapidly as a result of the active measures now being taken. The Health Committee has captured two samples of potted lobster which, unlike a certain lady of old, were not above suspicion. The matter—or maybe the lobster—has been left in the hands of the Town Clerk ! The committee has also given notice that water from Appleton Reservoir is not fit to drink. The clever fellows found this out by analysis. Ordinary folk would not have looked twice</p>
        <p>* * * It was an army recruit's first appearance at the rifle range. The range officer tried him first at 500 yards, and the recruit did not, as the saying runs, come within a mile of the target. Next the officer tried him at 300 yards ; then at 200 yards ; and finally at 100 yards. His lar4 shot was even worse than the first. The officer looked at him disgustedly, and, losing his temper, walked up to him and shouted the command in his face : "Attention ! Fix bayonet! Charge the target ! Its your only chance."— X.Y.Z. in the" Daily News." * * * The "Manchester Guardian " relates the following tram story :—lt was between nine and ten on Sunday morning, when trams to town are few and far between. A stout 1 gentleman carrying a portmanteau in one hand anda hatbox in the other stepped into the road- &lt; way at the gate of the University as a car came along&lt; Palatine-road. " Does this car go to the Central ?" he asked the small trolley-boy on the platform. "I' don't know. I have not yet inspected the indicator," was the boy's calm reply. And as the stout gentle- man tried to recover from the shock the tram sped on its way, leaving him gasping in the middle of Oxford- street.</p>
        <p>o The veterans' table was a centre of special interest at the Old Folk's Treat on Thursday. The old soldiers, proudly wearing their medals, seemed to thoroughly enjoy themselves and the martial songs given for their edification must have called up memories of more warlike scenes. Several of the men had been through the Crimean War and Indian Mutiny and another had served with General Gordon in Egypt. One white-haired and wrinkled veteran could hardly restrain his delight whilst the Mayor was saying flattering things about them, and when his name was read out he drew himself erect and saluted_ A recitation descriptive of the horrors of Cawnpore by one who had actually formed one of " Havelock's little band" was a dramatic feature which touched every,- one. * *</p>
        <p>Dr. Wardlaw Thompson on Wednesday entered on his year of office as chairman of the Congregational Union. He has been foreign secretary of the London Missionary Society since 1881, and his birth and upbringing were not inappropriate for his missionary career. He was born at Bellary, in South India, and educated at the South African College, and he took his degree of B.A. at the University of the Cape of Good Hope. He also studied at Cheshunt College, which has lately been removed to Cambridge. His D.D. is from Glasgow, an honourable distinction awarded him this year. In a high appreciation of Dr. Wardlaw Thompson recently penned by Dr. Horton, the latter speaks of his "strange blending of prudence and enthusiasm," which is very characteristic of him. The year of his chairmanship will probably prove to be a great missionary year with the Congregationalists. Dr. Wardlaw Thompson is a disciple of Isaac Walton, and delights in trout fishing.</p>
        <p>EYESTRAIN. Arises from small unnoticed defects of focus, and causes Headache, Inflammation, Weak Eyes, Inability to stand the Light, Neuralgia, etc. GLASSES are the only cure, Glasses should only be worn after proper testing, otherwise the sight may be permanently injured. JOHNSTON WATSON, EVE- SIGHT SPECIALIST, 81, Bridge-street, Warrington. Low Charges. Satisfaction Guaranteed.</p>
