        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Coming Events. The fourth annual dance in connection with the Warrington Cathffic Young Men's Society will be held in the Parr Hall, Warrington, next Tuesday, under the patronage of the Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. J. Smethurst), Mr A. H. Crosfield, M.P., and Mrs. Crosfield. The annual ball in connection with the Warrington Butchers' Association will be held at the Parr Hall on Thursday evening next. Under the auspices of the Education Committee of the Co-operative Society a special children's entertain- ment will be given in the Parr Hall this afternoon by Mr. H. Hibbert. In the evening a lantern lecture will be given, interspersed with cinematographic views, for adults. Mr. Hibbert will also officiate in 'the evening. The annual ball in connection with the Cemetery Hotel Bowling Club will be held on Thursday February Bth, when Mr. A. Starkey's band will be engaged. The annual exchange of pulpits between the ministers of the Warrington Free Churches will take place on January 12. A public meeting of the Warrington branch of the - N S.P.C.C. will be held in the Council Chamber on Fiiday January 17. A public meeting will be held in the Council Chamber at the Town Hall, on Thursday evening next at 6.45 for the purpose of forming a Parliamentary Debating Society. The Mayor will preside. A special general meeting of governors of the Infirmary will be held at the Institution on Friday 17th inst. to consider recommendations of the Com- mittee of Management to the effect that Mr. Henry Greenall be elected a vice-president ; that one of the honorary assistant medical officers be appointed a full honorary medical officer ; that an honorary assistant medical officer be appointed, and that the rules be amended in regard to the duties of th 2 last-named</p>
        <p>officer. 'LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs, Colds, and Bronchial troubles. 9id. &amp; Mi. Of Chemists.—AdVt MORE LIGHT AT LESS COST.—" " Electric Lamps do this and more. Full details from the local agent.:—J. S. GARLAND, 51-55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. The Rev. Horace Gray. The Bishop of Liverpool has licensed the Rev. Horace Gray, headmaster of the Warrington Grammar School, to officiate in the diocese. School Inspector's Resignation. Mr. A. R. Smith, who has for some time officiated as Inspector of elementary schools of the town and neighbourhood, has been transferred to another district. His place will be taken by Mr. A. T. Kerslake, of Preston. JORDAN ALMONDS is. 6d. per lb. at ASHLEY'S.—Advt.</p>
        <p>LTN SEE D COMPOUND ' of 40 yeftrF efncacy</p>
        <p>for Coughs, Colds, and Bronchial troubles.—Advt. Adult School Movement. The meeting to be held to-night in connection with the above movement, in the Kendrick-street Schools, and at which it is hoped the Mayor will occupy the chair, has for it:, object the rousing of interest in the school to be opened to-morrow at 8.45 a.m. A well- organized adult school consists of gatherings for Bible study, with educational classes, lectnrettes on hygiene and other topics; savings banks, libraries, &amp;c., &amp;c. TOAGULINE," KLINK,"TENASITINE.'--Cements for broken and other articles.—Advt. Alderman James Fairclough's Will. Alderman James Fairclough, who died on December Ist, left estate valued by his brothers, Mr. John Fair- lough, The Grange, Latchford, Mr. Richard Taylor airclougb , Barryfield, Latchford, and Mr. Henry irclough, 37,Wilson Patton-street, Warringtou,corn merchant, the executors, at :U1,605 Is. id. of which £21,561 lls.lld. is net personalty. The will is dated May 4th, 1888, and the codicil May 22nd, 1901, under which everything passes to his wife and then to his children.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Bold-street Popular Concert. There was another large attendance in Bold-street Schoolroom on Saturday night, the chief attraction being a display of living pictures which caused much amusement. 'Perhaps one of the most successful items on the programme was an illustrated recitation by Miss Harris which reflected great credit upon her, whilst Mr. Shakeshaft created great merriment with his ventriloquial sketches. The Rev. T. Miller pre- sided, whilst Mr. W. Parkinson officiated at the lantern.</p>
        <p>POH XMAS FRUIT, ASHLEY'S, Sankey-street.—Advt. LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs and Colds. of proven efficacy. Of Chemists.—Advt. AP-</p>
        <p>lieged Poaching at Bewsey. At the Occasional Court, on Monday, Richard Mills, who 'would not give any account of himself, was charged with poaching at Bewsey at about 11.30 p.m on December 28th on land belo.iging to the Right • Hon. Lord Lilford. Mr. E. A. White prosecuted, and. said that at about 11-30 on the night stated, a game- keeper, heard a gun shot in the direction of certain woods at Bewsey. About ten minutes afterwards he , saW prisoner coming from the land with a gun.—The case was remanded to the Newton Sessions to be held on January 9th. INlaclaine Cleansed CURRANTS 3d ,t4d lb.at ASHLEY'S Advt.l PIM LIC BEtkIEFIT—IINDER TRW CLOCK FOR BEST ROOTS INLANCASHIP.7.—Advt. Voters in Lancashire. The lists for the 23 Lancashire county Parlia- mentary divisions, which came into operation on New Year's Day, show a normal increase in the number of voters in each division. The total number of electors (Parliamentary and local) in each division is as follows : —Accrington, 19,556 ; Bootle, 23,803 ; Blackpool, 28,868 ; Chorley, 17778; Clitheroe, 20,707 ; Darwen' 20,491 ; Eccles, 22,064: Gorton, 19,149 ; Heywood, 14,302 ; lace, 15,185 ; Lancaster, ' 16,029 ; Middleton 18,308; Newton. . _ _ Ormskirk,</p>
        <p>Tiossendale, 16,377 ; .- 29,137 ; Westhonghton, 20,537; and 'Widnes, 12,632. OSRAM " ELECTRIC LAMPS.—DOUhIS the light at half the cost. See Windows.—Agent : J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Burglaries In Hood-lane. Hood-lane, Sankey, was visited by burglars during Sunday night, an entrance being effected into three houses in the occupation of Mr. John Hill (150), Mr. Thomas Johnson (152), and Mr. John Blythe (154). In each instance a gimlet bad been used in boring a hole • in the sash of the lower back window in order to move +( the catch. On rising on Monday morning tbe tenants of the houses found the rooms in a state of disorder, ' and large quantities of eatables and clothing were missing. Mr. Blythe found several ornaments smashed and he also missed small amounts of money that had been left on the piano and on the mantelshelf. The ' burglars left the gas burning in the hall. At Mr. -Bill's the cat had been locked in the parlour, and among other things the owner missed his overcoat .collar and tie. There are several hencotes in the L' gardens at the rear of the houses, but they were ' -evidently overlooked. MOUNT/UN' FLAX (Lnant CkauraurrotryoPfLLS An agreeable aperient. Worth a trial. 911c1.—Advt. Wycliffe P.S.A. A well attended meeting of this Society was held on Sunday, presided over by the Secretaty (Mr. B. Farrar) , Mr J. Ewan (Sale), spoke from the words "Choose ye this day whom ye will serve," and gave advice suitable to the closing hours of another year. He also took a retrospect of the past year, with all its ; 'experiences. What shall nor choice he ? not the r many gods they saw around them, but the one, true, and only God the Father of all. (Applause.) Mr. Noden and Miss Wood sang" 0 Love Divine," and ; Mr. Noden "The Psalm " and each item was greatly appreciated. Mr. Stringer and Mr. Healey also IDA part in the meeting. The Chairman wished all Present and absent "A. happy and prosperous new year." The brethren stood while thanks were given 'to Almighty God for the blessings of the past year. 'LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs and COMB . 'Relieves Asthma and difficult breathing.—Advt,</p>
