      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>IN ALL DEPARTMENTS.</p>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>A Week of Pantomime.</p>
        <p>The Royal Court Theatre haa been busy thia week with the seasonable pantomime. Messrs.Pardoes' and Miss Lottie Holland's company has occupied the boards in the production of that old-time favourite " Cinderella," and the piece has been a good draw. There is sufficient of the old fairy tale to amuse and interest the children and to form a groundwork for the medley of gay scenes, comic songs, and fancy turns which make up the modern pantomime. The fun is of the screaming variety and waxes fast and furious under the lead of Mr. George Hyam. The ugly sisters are not played by men in this pantomime, but are very funnily represented by Miss Amy and I Miss Ada, who are unique as female humbrists. Cinderella is sweetly represented by Miss Edie Cameron, who is the possessor of a tune- ful contralto voice. Miss Lottie Holland is the dash- ing Prince Charming, who falls in love with the down-trodden over-worked Cinderella in the traditional way and comports herself with the necessary boyish- ness and vigour. A good word must be said for Miss Violet Rivers as Dandini, -her acting and singing being distinguished by grace and sprightliness. As the perturbed Baron, Mr. Syd Herman is well placed and has some good songs, while Mr. Tom Griffitt as Father Time sings with a mellow baritone voice, in a manner which earns warm applause. Of the speciality turns, the Pardoes, " the world's only hnman skipping rope and human bundle," perform with great skill. Miss Lottie Holland produces a pretty scena, " In the Shadow of the Pyramid," assisted by the Sisters D'Estrail ; the Edingale Trio are clever acrobatic speciality dancers ; the Vanoros contribute a pleasing musical act and there is some capital dancing by Miss Nellie Rose. During the play a pretty electric coach drawn by two well-trained ponies is introducted for the use of Cinderella at the ball. The story has been written by Mr. E. Clementson, and set to catchy music by Mr. Fred Dawes. There is a matinee at 2.30 to-day, and the usual evening performance.</p>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>Lays "Lucia Dußrum."</p>
        <p>For the third time within the last two and a half years Wilson Barrett's last play " Lucky Durham " will be once more amongst us next week. We cannot tell Warrington playgoers anything they do not know about this remarkable piece, which is just completing its 870th performance. The story is too well-known to need recapitulation, and the influence of the play upon all classes of society has been great. The same admirable company is again interpreting it. Edmund Kennedy plays "John Durham," Arthur Leigh " Reggie," Alice Esther Belmore " Helen," and other members of the company are T. Renaud Lockwood, A.. • T rtt,W. F. Clitherow, Rosabel Gourlay. The , ets built by the late Wilson Barrett are travelled in their entirety. Friday night promises to be a red letter night, for it is set apart for the benefit of Mr. Edmund Kennedy, who has piloted " Lucky Durham " to success, and played the. chief part, that of John Durham, for over 800 times. This is the first benefit Mr. Kennedy has ever received, and of all the towns on the present tour which he was offered, he chose Warrington, where he has always been so acceptable to the public, who recognise in him the consummate artiste. On this evening a handsome souvenir containing over 20 photographs of the scenes and characters in the play will be given to all patrons booking seats or entering by early doors (gallery excepted).</p>
