      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>FREE CHURCH COUNCIL. . Week of Special Prayer.</head>
        <p>FREE CHURCH COUNCIL. . Week of Special Prayer.</p>
        <p>The annual week of special prayer under the auspices of the Free Church Council will be held from Monday next until Saturday next. The following services have been arranaed, and it is hoped that Nonconformists will make an effort to attend. . .</p>
        <p>_ ! Monday, Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church : i Subject," Thanksgiving and Humiliation." Chairman, ' Mr. C. E. Parker ; speaker. Rev. S. Pitt. Tuesday, Bold-street; Wesleyan Church : Subiect, "Church Universal." Chairman, Mr. T. Dale ; speaker, Rev. J. B. Buglass. 1 Wednesday, Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church : Subject, " Nations and their Rulers." Chairman, Mr. E. A. Saunders ; speaker, Mr. E. Wynne Jones. Thursday, Wycliffe Congregational Church : Subject, "Foreign Missions." Chairman, Mr. J. C. Holland; speaker, Rev. J. L. Milne. Friday, Baptist. Church, Golborne-street : Subject 1 ' "Families, Educational Establishments and the Young." Chairman, Mr. A. Mounfield ; Speaker, Rev. J. S. Hughes. Saturday, Friars' Green Chapel: Subject," Home , Missions and the Jews." Chairman, Rev. F. Vaughan; 'Speaker, Rev. J. Yonge.</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, January 4, 1907.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>OLD FOLK'S TREAT.</head>
        <p>OLD FOLK'S TREAT.</p>
        <p>SUCCESSFUL FUNCTION AT THE PARR HALL.</p>
        <p>• A Gathering of Army Viterans.</p>
        <p>It is natural to venerate the old folk who form, as it were, connecting links with the past, and to make the evening of their clays as happy as possible. Especially does this feeling find expression at this festive season of the year, and it has therefore come about, very appropriately, that one of the first events of the New Year in Warrington is the treat to the aged poor. The liberal support afforded to this old- established institution is highly gratifying as betoken- ing the desire that all should share in the seasonable rejoicings. There is no doubt that the subscribers would have felt well repaid if they had chanced to look in the Parr Hall on Thursday and see the happy faces of the aged guests who assembled for this year's treat. As usual, the old men were entertained at , dinner at noon and they were feasted in right royal I fashion. There were about 600 of them, several being over SO years of age while one had attained his ' eighty-ninth year. It was a motley assembly. Some of the veterans carried their years lightly and walked erect, whilst others were bowed with infirmity. Soon all were seated at the long tables in readiness for the meal. At one table, which was specially decorated with Union Jacks, there was a particularly interesting gathering. It consisted of twenty-one army veterans who had come by invitation of the Mayor, who is the President of the Committee. Many proudly displayed the medals which they had gained for service in memorable campaigns and, despite advancing years, , the majority retained something of the o'd military bearing. The following are the names of a few of the ept eartarns i ck: Miskell, late of the 66th Regiment, nearly , eighty years of age. Served in the Crimean War and Indian Mutiny for which he holds medals. Sergeant Cummins, late of the 95th Regiment, 70 years of are. Served in the Crimea and Indian Mutiny for which be holds medals, in addition to two medals for good conduct and meritorious service. Colour-Sergeant Henry Cornev, late of the 17th Regiment, 72 years of age. Served in the Crimea and in Turkey, holding medals for each campaign and one for good conduct. John Flynn, late of the Dublin Fusiliers. 63 years of age. Served in Egypt, under Gordon and Kitchener. Henry Bond Bowers, late of the 64th Regiment, 77 years of age. Served in the Persian campaign 'in 1856-7, and took part in four engagements, holding three medals. He also served in India under General Sir Henry Havelock and assisted at the funeral of that distinguished soldier. He was one who helped to wrap the horse cloth about the corpse, prior to interment. When in India he was a member of what was known as " Havelock's little bend." He retired from the army on January 19th, 1869 and is still in very good health. Sergeant-Major William Sullivan, late of the 40th regiment, served in the army for twenty-eight years, five of which were spent in India. Sergeant Costello, late of the 88th Connaught Rangers, 70 years of age, was in the army 39 years and served in the Indian Mutiny, holding a medal. Michael Makell served in the Crimean War and Indian Mutiny. The carving at the veterans' table was undertaken by two chum majors. An excellent, dinner was served—in fact one guest enthusiastically described it as bsing "fit for the King." There was an abundance of roast beef and pork and plum pudding, with an allowance of beer. and after the meal pipes end tobacco were distributed. The food had been cooked by Mr. and Mrs. Hare. An army of workers waited upon the old men, the members of the committee in attendance being the Mayor, President (who was accompanied by the Mayoress). Mr. , R. Shackleton (vice-president), Councillor Nield (hon. , treasurer), Mr. Thomas Arthur Sutton (hon. secretary), Alderman Forshaw, Councillor Rogers, Captain A. F. G. Potter, Messrs. C. E. Bunch, S. Davies, T. Atherton, A. Jolley, W. Webster and H. Hope. After dinner the toast of the "King and Queen and the rest of the Royal Family " was enthusiastically honoured. I The MAYOR mentioned the fact that for the first I time in twenty-eight years an old lady had sat down to the dinner. She was the wife of one of the army veterans, who was rather infirm, and had asked specially to be allowed to accompany him. Continuing, his Worship said that from the bottom of his heart he wished all the guests a happy and prosperous new year. Many of them had cause for thankfulness to God that they had been spared to such a ripe old age. There I was not a member of the Committee with them now who was a member when he (the Mayor) joined it. Mr. Shackleton had, however, been associated with the institution for many years. He (the Mayor) was sure the subscribers would approve of the step the Com- mittee had tnken in inviting the brave men who had fought England's battles in the pest. (Applause.) He was happy to think there was a prospect that in the near future those who had served their country with the coheirs would, instead of being tossed about hither and thither, be comfortably housed and taken special care of. (Hear, hear). ME. HUMPHRIES then sang "The Veteran's Song " and " The old soldier" with fine spirit. At the request of the MAYOR the old soldiers were enthusiastically cheered by the other guests. The MAYOR then moved a vote of thanks to the workers, and remarked that the Warrington Old Folk's Treat was one of the oldest institutions of its kind in Lancashire. The records could be traced for thirty years, but prior to that period no minutes could be found. He did not think there was a town where the public subscribed more willingly to the treat. (Anplause.) The vote wee carried with rousing cheers. Mr. .ToTiN Dunitv, a well-known local temperance worker, proposed a vote of thanks to the Mayor and Mayoress, Committee and subscribers, and gave some interesting reminiscences of his experiences in the Army in Ireland many years ago. Mr. I. JoRNsoX seconded, end declared that. the dinner had been " fit for the King." He congratulated the Mayor upon his re-election,and expressed the hope that during the coming year the widening of Bridge-street would be completed. He wished the Mayor and Mayoress every success and happiness. The vote was enthusiastically passed. Mr. BOWERS. one of the veterans, gave a recitation " Gallant 64th "with line dramatic effect.</p>
        <p>Praise is due to Mr. W. B. Randall who gave organ seleotions whilst the dinner was in progress. There W,OI another happy gathering in the evening when over 700 women, ranging in ages from sixty to ninety-five, were entertained at tea. The meal consist- ing of ham and beef sandwiches, currant cake ece..was thoroughly appreciated. The wires of members of the Committee and other ladies presided at the tables. After tea oranges and mince pies were distributed. An excellent entertainment followed, and there Was a capital attendance of the public in the galleries. The Mayor presided and at the outset wished the guests a very happy New Year. An admirable programme was I submitted, the first item being "The old soldier " sung very effectively by Mr. Humphries. Later in the evening he was very successful in " Asleep in the deep" and Father O'Flynn." Mr. W. W. Whittle created a great deal of mirth with the humnrous songs What's the good of anything ? " and "The Serenade" and other excellent comic items were the songs " Lazy " and "Heigh up ! It's coming ! " by Mr. J. Emson, which delighted the old ladies. Mr. E.Kelly, of Cad's- head, VINO two humorous songs in character which proved very popular. The committee were indebted to Mr. F. Maonaghten for the appearance of a number of artistes from the Hippodrome whose turns added much to the enjoyment of the evening. Burlesque sketches were admirably given by the Sisters Russell and The Two Sprees, and the songs by Miss Edith St. Claire, a clever comedienne, brought down the house. Miss May Trenton. diabolist, gave a splendid exhibition in which she proved herself an expert at the game. A series of wonderful feats of club juggling by Mr. Michael I. Fitzgerald and a troupe of ladies were highly enter- taining. Saxo. the man of many instruments' played skilfully on a Japanese fidele and a saxophone. Messrs. Randall and Lathom acted as accompanists.</p>
        <p>Bcfore the guests dispersed cheers' were eiven for the workers and subscribers, at the call of the MAYOR, and a vote of thanks was accorded to big Worship on the nronnsitinn of Captain POTTER, seconded by Councillor Nield and supported by Mr. S. DA.VIES.</p>
        <p>Much troubled in mind, the Cynic reclined, His star was not in ascendant ; He deigned not to scoff, he had a bad cough, And a tear from his nose was pendant. "I'll away ! "said Diog. "To Delphi log, And consult that oracle sure." He went, and it said," For a cold in the head Take Woods' Great Peppermint Cure."—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>MUSICAL SUCCESS.</p>
        <p>Among the candidates who have successfully passed the recent examination of the Associated Board of the Royal Academy of Music and Royal College of Music in the advanced section, is Miss A. Muriel Bell, youngest daughter of the Rev. Fred R. Bell, of Widnes Wesleyan Circuit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>BAD LOT.</head>
        <p>A BAD LOT.</p>
        <p>r At the Cheshire Quarter Sessions, on Monday, Henry M`Donald, alias Ernest Holton (N), stoker, pleaded guilty to stealing an overcoat and a woollen singlet, the property of Thomas Leach, at Runcorn, on the 14th of November.—The Chairman said prisoner was a bad Jot, He started in 1895—.—Prisonec (angrily) : "I have heard quite sufficient about that lately. You keep that to yourself."—The Chairman continued to read the prisoner's record, and ended by sentencing him to three years' penal servitude.—Prisoner : Good luck to you. NO TRUE RILL.</p>
        <p>No true bill was returned in the case of Ernest Baker (38), labourer, charged with stealing clothing at Runcorn. RUNCORN INSURANCE AGENT IN TROUBLE. At Runcorn, on Monday, Hubert Finney, agent of the Refuge Assurance Society, was charged with embezzling £3 12s. 9d. Finney gave notice, and on its expiration he produced accurate accounts, but tendered short money, offering to pay by instalments, as he alleged it had been done in other cases. The com pany wished it to be known that this could not be done. The bench, in sentencing defendant to one day, said his character was not much blackened.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>'At the monthly meeting of Runcorn Urban District Council on Tuesday, Mr. Jones presiding, the annual report upon the Canal Boats Acts was presented,shovring that 1,073 boats were registered in Runcorn. During the year 302 boats had been inspected, upon which were 468 men, 141 women, and 140 children, ranging from babies in arms to thirteen years. Not one case of infectious disease had been notified during the year.— The medical officer of health (Dr. M'Dougall) reported that during December there were 26 deaths, six being due to pneumonia and five to bronchitis.—Mr. Robert Wright, superintendent of the fire brigade, tendered his resignation, and mentioned that he had been connected with the brigade since its inception in 1858.—Mr. Handley said Mr. Wright had completed fifty years service, and he would suggest the letter be referred to the committee.—The resolution was carried.—Messrs. Sweetrnan, Pringle, and Kirkham were appointed the Council's representatives on the Brunner Guildhal Management Committee.</p>
        <p>SHOCKING RAILWAY ACCIDENT AT WIDNES. About 5.30 on Wednesday evening, Samuel Knight, aged 64 years, who was said to come from St. Helens was found by a railway shunter named Richard Davies, lying on a siding known as the Vine Slip, near the Vine Paint and Oil Works, Sugsdale, Widnes. The, body was lying in the four foot way, with the head towards the works. Examination revealed shocking injuries. The head was almost severed from the body, and the right arm and shoulder were crushed to a pulp. Information was given to the police, and the body was conveyed to the mortuary at tbe Widnes Accident Hospital. The station-master at Appleton had issued a ticke to the deceased from Appleton to St. Helens at 4.45 in the afternoon, and when Knight was told that the next train to St. Helens was not until 529, he said be would" go and have a drink until train time." The stationmaster did not see him again until the body was found on the line. It is conjectured that deceased missed his way, and walked down the line, about a quarter of a mile from the station. He had the presence of mind to keep clear of the up and down line to St. Helens and Widnes, but walked on the sidings, where he was knocked down by a train of works waggons wnich were being shunted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>SEPARATION AFTER EIGHT MONTHS OF</head>
        <p>SEPARATION AFTER EIGHT MONTHS OF</p>
        <p>MARRIAGE:</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday, before Alderman A. R. Norman (in the chair) and Mr. John Littler,Stephen Rose, Gilbert-street, was summoned for persistent cruelty to his wife. She alleged that she had been obliged to leave him and applied for a separation order and alimony. Defendant pleaded guilty.—Complainant said they had been married eight months. During that time he had several times thumped and kicked her. She had been to the Police Station times without number to complain of him. 'Four months ago he was sent to gaol for 14 days for kicking her. A week last Saturday night he met her on the Top Locks bridge. He was drunk and began to kick and otherwise illuse her. She left him on Christmas Eve. He was a dock labourer and earned 18s. a week.—The Chairman : How does he behave when sober? Complainant : He is worse sober than drunk. He has it in him. It's bred in him. He cannot help it. The Magistrates Clerk (to defendant) : You plead guilty. The only question is the amount of the order. How much do you say you ought to pay? Defendant : As much as you like.—The Chairman : It's a very brutal admission on your part.—Defendant : No. It is not. It is her own fault. She doesn't give me a chance to kick her. She goes out.—A separation order with a payment of Gs. a week was granted, and defendant was also ordered to pay the costs, 10s. Gd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>RUNCORN FLATMAN DROWNED.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN FLATMAN DROWNED.</p>
        <p>At the Lymm Police Station, on Tuesday morning Mr. Thomas Ridgway, deputy coroner, held an inquest touching the death of Charles Lindberg (21), flatman, of 59, Percival-lane, Runcorn, whose body was found in the Bridgewater Canal at Lymm, on December 28th.— Matilda Lindberg, of Kensington, Liverpool, identified the body as that of her brother. She last sawhim alive on November 10th, 1906.—James Worthington, 59, Percival-lane, Runcorn, captain of the flat Tweed, said they left Manchester about 7 p.m. on Saturday. There were four flats drawn by a steamer, and his flat was the last of the four. All went well until they reached Ditchfield's Bridge, at Lymm. Witness was at the fore- end of the boat, and deceased was steering. They then collided with a float on the opposite side of the towing path. He heard deceased shout "Oh." Witness said, "Charlie, where are you ?" and deceased replied, "I am in the canal." Witness jumped ashore and ran to tho spot where deceased had fallen in, but he could not see him. Others then came to witness's assistance, and helped him to recover the body. After the collision the flat swerved, and he thought it struck the bank, thud giving the rudder a jerk, which would throw deceases into the canal. The time would be about 11.45 p.m.— The jury returned a verdict of" Accidental death."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>APPOINTMENT FOR AN EARLESTOWN DOCTOR. Dr. J. P. Walker, of Earlestown. has been appointed public vaccinator for the town of Oldham, his remuner- ation being fixed at the minimum scale of 2s. Gd. per case, with £l5 per annum for a central vaccinating station.</p>
        <p>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS. There was only a light calendar at these sessions last Thursday, before Mr. Robert Stone (presiding), Colonel Wood, Mr. Roger C. Parr and Mr. C. 5. F. Borron. Faank Dean, Haydock, was fined 2s. 6d. and costs for having been drunk and disorderly at Hayclock on I December Bth.—Alexander Ranglash was fined 58. for driving a cart without a light after prohibited hours ; and for allowing his dog to be at large without a collar I Rev. Farher O'Meara was fined ss.</p>
        <p>DIED ON CHRISTMAS MORNING, I On Christmas morning Mrs. Harriet Wood, aged 64, of 118, Blackbrook-road, Haydock, was found dead in bed. Mrs. Wood, for about four years, had suffered from chronic bronchitis. She always refused to see a doctor, and on Sunday, when she became confined to bed, still persisted in the belief that doctors could do her no good. On Christmas morning Mr. Wood got up about half-past nine, leaving his wife sleeping peace- fully in bed. He made the fire, and about an hour afterwards took her a cup of tea, when he was shocked to find his wife had been dead for some little time. g%</p>
        <p>C.Y.M.S. ANNUAL BALL. On Boxing night the members of the above society held their annual ball at Earlestown Town Hall. The attendance was 'satisfactory and the Town Hall looked very pretty and inviting, the decorative art of Mr. E. T. Evans' staff being resplendently shown on the walls and balcony, the front of the latter bearing an effective design, " Success to the C.Y.M,S." Mr. , J. Miller, of the Oak Tree Hotel, Newton, bad charge of the refreshment buffet, and this department also gave much satisfaction. A lengthy programme of 24 dances was provided and the music of Mr. J. Dennett's band, from St. Helens won the praise of all present. The masters of ceremonies were Messrs. J. Byrne, E. Fitzgerald, Kitchen, and Noonan. The Committee responsible for the very excellent arrangements made for the comfort and convenience of visitors were Messrs. J. Rice, J. Catterall, J. McGuinness,T. Cosgro ve, W. Russell, M. Kenny, J. Fleming, and R. Smith, with the energetic Secretaries, Messrs. W. F. Hunt and J. Dixon. Dancing was kept up with vigour till an early hour next morning. The ball was under the patronage of the General Secretary of C.Y.M. Societies and other prominent Catholics. BURNT TO DEATH. A shocking accident befel Ellen Prior, of 62, Regent- street, Earlestown, on Thursday of last week, which resulted in her death at the Warrington Infirmary on Sunday.—Mr. S. Brighouse conducted an inquiry at the Warrington Police Station on Tuesday afternoon. The first witness was Charles Gardner who had lived with the deceased twenty years at 62, Regent-street, Earles- town. Deceased was fifty-two years of age. On Thursday last about 6.15 she went into the pantry with a piece of lighted paper in her hand to chop some wood while the witness and two children were sitting by the fire. She had only been in the pantry about a rginute when she rushed out with her clothes on fire and screamed "Charlie, Charlie." The witness was so overcome</p>
        <p>; that be fainted. The doctor was called in and ordered the deceased's removal to the Warrington Infirmary. She could not give witness any explanation as to how it was done, and she died on Sunday.—A member of the!jury : Were there no candles in the house at the time ?—Witness : I couldn't say. I never looked.— Jane Prior, daughter of the deceased, said she was not I • in the house at the time of the accident. She was at Manchester. She was present when her mother died at 1.15 at the Warrington Infirmary onSandly and had tol her that she went into the pantry to chop some wood with a piece of lighted newspaper in her hand,when her clothing got on fire. She had a flannelette blouse on.: The Coroner said there was DO suspicion of foul play.— The jury returned a verdict of " Accidental Death."</p>
        <p>RAN AWAY. Henry Hunt, a Golborne man, was charged at the Warrington Petty Sessions on Wednesday with running away from Golborne and leaving his wife and family chargeable to the common fund of the Union. The evidence was to the effect that prisoner's neglect had cost the Guardians £lO3 2s. out of which he had paid £2B 12s. 6d. It was the third time he had run away and he was described as a" lazy man." Prisoner had been sentenced for two months for a similar offence, and was now sent to gaol for three months with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS COLLIER FOUND DEAD.</p>
        <p>William Henry Pownall, aged fifty, a colliery daywagernan, living at Silver-street, St. Helens, was found dead in bed on Sunday. It is stated that the man had never been ill, and that he was all right on Sunday morning when he got up. He later went back to bed, but, when he was called for dinner, was found to have passed peacefully away. ST. HELENS MAN'S ATTEMPTED SUICIDE.' William Williams a young collier, Wilson-street, was remanded at St. Helens on Monday on a ck,arge of attempted suicide. William Bagley, a young man, said that shortly before midnight on Saturday he was , passing by the police office at the Town Hall, when he saw Williams near the doorway with a razor open in his I hand. He drew the razor across his throat, but only inflicted a slight scratch. When witness spoke to him he dropped his hand, and said he was destitute and wanted to get rid of himself. He took the man inside the office, where, in answer to Constable Merrill, he said," lam downhearted. I have been sacked from my work at Lea Green, and I have tramped through north Wales for the week. I will do it." Prisoner's sister said he had his head hurt, and drink affected him. The bench directed that he should be examined by the prison doctor. DEATH OF MAJOR WILLCOCKS</p>
        <p>Major J. G. S. Willcocks, who for twenty-seven years was adjutant of the St. Helens Volunteers, passed away on Saturday evening at his residence Ravensdale, St. Ann's, after a long and painful illness. Major Willcocks was seventy-five years of age. For fifty-six years he had been a most respected resident of the town, and until illness prevented his attendance be was a useful member of the St. Helens Education Com- mittee. He was churchwarden of Eccleston Church for many years, and also manager of the schools of the parish. In 1861, when the St. Helens Volunteers were formed by the late Sir David Gamble and Colonel Pilkington, Major Willcocks was selected for the position of adjutant, having been trained in the Militia. He was always a most capable organiser, and under his trainin., the regiment won , a splendid reputation training marksmanship and general efficiency. In January, 1888, when the major retired from the adjutanoy, the late Sir David Gamble presented him, on behalf of the regiment, with an illuminated address and a cheque of 100 guineas. The presentation took place in the 'Volunteer Hall at the annual prize-giving, and Major Willcocks, in acknow- ledging the gift, mentioned that during the twenty- seven years no fewer than 6,000 recruits had been I enrolled in the regiment. A committee of local residents, under the chairmanship of Mr. W. J. Thomson, J.P., also presented an address and cheque for £llO to Major Willcocks. He leaves a widow and two sons and two daughters, and, though death came as a relief to prolonged suffering, the loss of the cheery and gentlemanly major will be sincerely regretted by all who knew him. The funeral took place on Tuesday afternoon at Eccleston Church. A.FATAL CHRISTMAS CAROUSE. Mr. Brighouse, county coroner, held an inquest at St. Helens on Tuesday, on Bridget Noon, sixty years of age, who lived with her husband at 4 house, 5 Court, Parr- street, and who died in the Providence Hospital on Saturday night from injuries. James Noon, chemical labourer, the husband, pre- seuted himself to give evidence, but appeared very dull.</p>
        <p>The Coroner said the difficulty in that case was that since Christmas Eve neither witness nor his wife had been sober, and be-had a very slight recollection of what had been taking place. The Coroner, later, said the best thing he could do would be to tell the jury what Detective Staveley had found out in the coarse of his inquiries. Mrs. Noon was a cripple, having lost her right leg about three years ago. She was also very infirm, and both she and her husband were addicted to drink. Noon said that neither of them had been sober since Christmas Eve. He also said that on Christmas Day he got up and he and his wife drank a gill of rum which he had obtained the previous night. That was the first thing on Christmas Day morning. They started to celebrate it with a gill of rum, and at half-past twelve he went to the Nag's Head public-house, where he had a glass of ruin and took home a noggin for his wife. At six o'clock he went again to the public-house, and got drunk. He did not remember getting home. On Boxing Day they had a lot more drink, and he did not remember clearly what happened on that day. About four o'clock on Friday morning Noon was awakened by hearing his wife call. He found her lying on the kitchen floor unable to rise. Her face was cut and bruised. He carried her to bed, and after she had been there about half an hour she fell out of bed on to the flagged floor. He lifted her back again, and later the same morning he called a Mrs. Mullins In. The latter found deceased's face cut and bruised, and the told Mrs. Mullins that she had fallen on the floor by the side of the fire. Noon aeain went out, apparently to celebrate the event. He visited several public-houses and arrived home drunk. Early on Saturday morning he again found his wife on the floor. After getting her to bed he called in a Mrs. Riley, who found her unconscious. Noon then went for Dr. Davies, who had the woman taken to the Providence Hospital. The house was situated in a very low neigh- bourhood, and on Saturday night, when the coroner's officer visited it, the husband was too drunk to tell them anything about the case, and the offices had to leave him until Sunday morning. " That is the Christmas carol, as sung by Mr. Noon," remarked the coroner. Three witnesses were called to speak to the couple drinking to excess, and to the facts of the old lady having had a great many falls. Dr. Davies detailed the wretched state of the house and the deceased when he was called in, and said that at the post-mortem he found a clot of blood on the brain opposite a large bruise on the left side of the face. There was also an effusion into the ventricles of the brain. The cause of death was coma, due to pressure. The jury returned a verdict according to medical evidence, and asked the coroner to censure the husband. The Coroner administered a rebuke to husband and advised him to turn over a new leaf. He also commented on the state of the house in which the people lived, and said that sanitary, habitable dwellings ought to be erected, and that would be the first step to get people into a better way of living.</p>
        <p>WAR OFFICE TRANSACTION. In a case heard on Monday at the Highgate Police Court a witness stated that her father sent her with the jacket to the War Office ?—Mr. Beal (Clerk) : The War Office ?—Mr. Graves (Assistant Clerk) : That is what some people call a pawnshop. (Laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>CROSS-COUNTRY RUNNING.</head>
        <p>CROSS-COUNTRY RUNNING.</p>
        <p>A pack consisting of about 20 runners from Warring- ton Wesleyans and Rylands' Workmen's Harriers started away from the Horse and Jockey. After an abFence of a little over half an hour, J. Massey was first man home. The Wesleynns won by ten points. Runcorn Harriers failed to keep their fixture with Sankey, who however, in view of their run with War-' ' rington A.C. from the Blnck Horse, on Saturday next had a strong run in which Chris Vnse showed up well. ' Warrington A.C. visited Sutton Harriers. A small pack covered about six miles in a little over 35 minutes, returning in the following order :—Sutton : J. Bailey ; . Morris. 0 ; J. Chapman, 8 ; R. Cropper, 9 ; total, 21 points. Warrington : H. Holbrook, 2; H. K. Jepson, 3 ; W. Antrobus. 4 ; J. T. Mulhall, 5; total, 14 points. A count of 4 aside excluded M. Tavlin 7th, but War- rington won by 10 points. Owing to failing light the correct times could not he taken ; however, Joe Bailey piloted the pack home, 5 yards in front of Herbert Holbrook, with Jenson 50 yards off and a similar distance in front of Antrnbus ; Mulhall was ten yards away and about six yards in front of Morris. Neither club was fully represented, Sutton being without. r Welding, J. W. Bailey and Bradshaw. Warrington had G. Watmongh, A. Fori nd S. D. Parsonage absent. 1 To-day Chester are due to run Warrington from Bridge Inn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>ACCIDENT.</head>
        <p>ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>On New Year's Eve Emma Scholes (68), Mercer- street, Newton-le-Willows, was returning home from a tea party when she either slipped or was knocked down, and sustained a broken leg. She was conveyed to the Warrington Infirmary, where she was detained.</p>
        <p>Woods' Great Peppermint Cure, for Coughs and Colds; never fails. 1/li, 2/9.—Advt.</p>
        <p>- -</p>
        <p>THE VOICE OF LABOUR. Edited by John Workman. M.S.S. must be addressed to John Workman, " Examiner " Office TVarrington and should be sent in not later than Wednesday in each week. Readers of this column are invited to assist in making it interesting and helpful to social workers by contributing themselves. Correspondents must write on one side of the paper only, and endeavour to make their letters and notes short and to the point. A nom-de-plume may be used, but the real name of the writer should be forwarded in confidence to the Editor of the column.</p>
        <p>A Happy New Year to all of us. In mounting the ladder of life may we never meet a friend. .</p>
        <p>* * There are many things worth having in this life. One we cherish most, probably, is the love of one's friends. Life's ironies are easily ignored then. And, after all, most of the ills we suffer from are of our own making—or our neighbours! Whilst we arc struggling in our own way for the betterment of the race may we not quarrel with our friends. May we always appreciate an honest endeavour even if we cannot agree with the method adopted. Mr. Parker and I have many views in common. We differ most, I think, in appearance. He believes that every man should get the whole fruit of his labour —and nothing more, nothing less. If we were all born into this world with bodies that would develop strong and healthy; if we were given minds that would guide and direct us in the strenuous life before us, then probably such a belief would readily meet with acquiescence. But we are not, and the strong must protect the weak. I refused his challenge because it was such a silly challenge. Socialism must not be I adopted by cliques but by nations. If I wanted to retaliate, I would ask Mr. Parker to select nineteen of his friends and invite them to agree to abide by the theory he advocates, viz. : " Every man to get the fruit of his labour. The whole fruit and nothing but the fruit."</p>
        <p>I wonder how long they would be able to live if they had not others to work for them. Mind there would be no interest on capital under his plan, for interest is not the fruit of one's labour—it is the fruit of somebody else's. I thank him for his good opinion of me. I hope he may think more highly of my creed before long. JOHN WORKMAN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>TRADES COUNCIL</head>
        <p>TRADES COUNCIL</p>
        <p>' Sir,—l have read the criticisms of " Oqlooker Spectator " and " Trades Unionist," which have appeared in your column, regarding the apathy of the Trades Council delegates. If your correspondents will have the courage to sign their own names, we shall then be better able to judge whether they have a' moral right to criticise either the Council or any other trades union body. The nom-de-plumes may perhaps represent one individual, who probably is not a Trades Unionist. However, if they will give us their own names in subsequent contributions, and prove to be Trades Unionists, lam prepared to reply to their criticisms, and to prove that we are not such criminals as oar critics would have your readers believe. —Yours etc.,</p>
        <p>FRED STOTT.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l note in the last issue of the " Examiner," a scurrilous letter referring to the local Trades and Labour Council by" Onlooker." Now I only wish to inform " Onlooker " that I do not choose to follow a phantom, and would ask : Who are you " Onlooker " ? Come from behind that nom-de-plume. Sign your name and let us see what sort of a friend of lab nur you are. If you have the courage to do this,l will then consider the advisability of replying to such spleenful attacks. I have no liking for men who desire to hide them. selves, pounce upon a fellow-msn from behind, stab him in the back, and retire to the hiding place. If "Onlooker " dare not disclose his identity, I do not hesitate to call him a coward, and shall treat him with the contempt which he deserves._____.</p>
        <p>THOMAS PLUMB.</p>
        <p>Padgate-lane, January Ist, 1903. _ THE FIEUTIEI COXIVITTEE.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Now that the intense interest lately evinced in local football (doe to the recent visit to Warrington of the famous All Blacks) has partially subsided, and the Christmas and New Year Festivities have once more become things of the past, I wish to call the attention of your readers to the remarkable speech delivered by the Chairman of the Health Committee (Councillor J. Evans) at the Council meeting held on Tuesday December 10th.</p>
        <p>In that particular oration statistics were quoted and other information was utilised for the purpose of comparing the records of Warrington with those of other large industrial centres on the important ques- tions of Infantile Mortality, the Death Rate, Smoke Nuisance and Slum Property.</p>
        <p>Allow me, Sir, to here give a short extract from a Press report of Councillor Evans' address : —" During 1906 out of a total of 352 deaths of children under one year of age, one hundred of these were certified as being due to that cause (meaning diarrhcea). The main factor bringing about these deaths was the wrong feeding of these infants either from ignorance or carelessness on the part of the mothers and especially the substitution of bottle." Granted that the " wrong " feeding of young children is a contributory cause of the high infantile death rate (race-suicide) prevailing in this town, the ! question then arises —ls every precaution being taken to minimise this glaring evil by those holding responsible positions, including Councillor Evans and his colleagues on the Health Committee? I ba'e ma doots sir ! Is it not a fact that every month there are scores of girls leaving the public elementary schools of this borough and entering domestic service, factory or shop-life, etc., who have received no " wise " tuition of such a nature as will safely guide them in the fulfilment of those exacting maternal duties which in adult life they may be called upon to perform. Further than this, John, most of the members of the Health Committee have not, of late,been strikingly active in propagating the humane principle that a moral minimum living wage be paid to " all " workers. Why, as a matter of fact, not a small pro- portion of them are loyal supporters of those particular political parties that are strongly opposed to this much-needed reform. Incredible as it may seem (judging by his latest public speech) even the present Chairman of that body did not support a motion,brought before the Council a couple of years ago, proposing that every able-bodied adult employed by the Warrington Corporation should be paid as wages no less a sum than 21 shillings weekly. It is extremely childish and absolutely futile to direct attention to effects, and ignore or belittle the causes that produce these effects. What useful purpose can possibly be served by merely stating that the deaths of many young children are due to "bottle feeding and the carelessness of mothers ?" Do we not all know, alas too well, that there ere in this town hundreds of working class families, who are more or less dependent, for even the bare necessaries of life, on the few shillings received as weekly wages by the mothers of those households ? If every unit in the industrial army were assured of " a fair day's wage for a fair day's work " then there would be far less justifica- tion for the mothers of this land to sub-let their maternal duties or feed their offspring artificially. "[hey are now, in numerous eases, literally forced to supplement the shamefully low wages paid to their husbands as manual workers by their own scanty earnings in factories, workshops, etc. Mr. Evans also mentioned the fact that Tuberculosis accounted for one-eighth of the deaths in the borough during 1906, and that the Health Com- mittee were endeavouring to combat the disease in various ways, one of which reads thus : By preventing as far as possible the sale of tuber- culous meat and milk." (Rear, hear, and applause.) I claim, Sir, that the only feasible method through which we could ensure the purity of the milk supply in Warrington is by the Town Council taking direct and complete control, in the interests of the consumers, of the entire distribution of this necessary food in every ward of the borough ; whilst the establishment of the suagested public abattoir, and the closing of all ! private slaughter-houses in this district would be, at l least, a useful step in the direction of ultimately obtain- ing an untainted meat supply for the inhabitants of Wireopolis. In dealing with this question I notice i that the Health Committee's recommandations are in the main directed to initiating an improved system of detecting tuberculosis in preference to attempting some great far-reaching fundamental reform which Would practically remove the principle causes of this , disease so prevalent in our midst. • Referring to the smoke nuisance, Councillor Evans , stated that he felt sure that the public-spirited manu- facturers will, when the seriousness of the case is put , before them, do all they can to abate it. The Chair- man of the Health Committee must consider the - 'burgesses of this town easily bluffed if he hopes to , persuade vern that the local manufacturers do not already know the seriousness of the smoke nuisance.</p>
        <p>Most of these " public-spirited " employers of labour take the wise precaution to reside in the country, for like Alderman Bennett" they cannot see that breath- ing an air choked with smoke is good at any rate for the, r lungs." I firmly believe that there are compara- tively few local governing bodies in the North of England who would allow (without a strong protest or possibly a prosecution) the repeated emission of such huge volumes of dense black smoke as periodic- ally belch forth from the chimneys of the firms con- trolled by these " public-spirited " manufacturers of Warrington. With regard to the question of slum property,l may say here that after carefully reading Councillor Evans' remarks on this sub)ect I toured Orford, Bewsey and St. John's Wards and from a personal knowledge of these districts I am firmly convinced that if it be not true " that the Health Committee has been asleep for years "then those gentlemen who have recently occupied seats on that body have either been grossly incapable of fulfilling the responsibilities of public life or are shamefully indifferent to the crying need for some drastic housing reforms in Warrington. In each of these three wards there are scores of houses inhabited by human beings that are really "not fit for a pig to live in," whilst a number of the "slum" dwellings are in a mest disgraceful insanitary state. Here, sir, do we find ample justification for the agitator's taunt : "That in this England of ours it were far better to be born a rich man's dog than the poor man's child." But the saddest phase of this tragic drama is that. the only practical remedies (suggested by Councillor Evans) for these deplorable conditions are—(Lord help us, John) "The enforcing of through ventilation in all houses and of increased ventilation and light in. many others." Shame on us,aye thrice shame on us,say I, if we stand idly by and allow these timid reformers to continue to thus " tinker," aimlessly with these grave social diseases. It is our bounden duty, Sir, (yours no less than mine) to strenuously insist on our public representatives promptly systematically and intelligently studying this very intricate problem of " housing reform." We must not permit them to continue any longer (like Nero) to fiddle while Rome is burning.</p>
        <p>DEMOS.</p>
      </div>
