      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>WATCHNIGHT SERVICES.</head>
        <p>WATCHNIGHT SERVICES.</p>
        <p>GOLBORNE-STICZET BAPTIST CHURCH. At the Golborne-street Tiantis, Church. a watchnight service was conda-red hv the Rev. loseph Ellison, of Vnrsobnl, South Wales, son of Alderman Ellison. of Latchford. There was a plod congregation. Mr. Ellison gl”(1 an addr,ss on "The sours oblective,'' based on Teh. xxi. 2. " Looking unto Jesus "end spoke, very impressively of the solemnity of the occasion. A consecration service followed. BOLD-STREET WESLEY CM:7RM. There was a large attendance at the watchnight ser- - I virP. The usual hymns were sung. The ninetieth Psalm was read as lesson, and its opening verse was the sllbieel. of the address by the Rev. T. \Tiller. God's , immutability was contrrsted with man's changeableness. The evidences of chance es seen in nature and harnan life wore dwelt upon as reasons for trust in One whose years fail not. Our Father's God is our God, From . everlasting to evorlas*inT thon sr. God." Geology and astronomy have familiarised US with long stretches of time and slow progressive movements, behind and beyond all these movements is God.—Mr. Prank Hick- r; man ably °resided at the organ, and his rendering of the " Hallelujah Chorus " was most inspiring. ST. PAUL'S CHURCH'. . The preacher at St. Paul's Chnr•.h was the Vicar, ". the Rev. NV. Br 'coracle) who said it MIS a great • pleasure to him to meet the congregation at another watchnight service, although in some quarters these ; services were being condemned. He was one of those who believed they were a great blesFing to many people and he would never he the one to stop them. The past year had been a year of great progress and blessing in St. Paul's parish, end he looked forward to the future with great hope. He asked all nresent to realize the ‘• full solemnity of the service, and if any present felt .2 that the past rear had been ono of neglected oppor- tunities. ther would make a solemn resolve, with God's •, blessing.. to devote themselves tn the Master's service. After the servics the church bells rang out a merry peal.</p>
