      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>BEWSEY-ROAD WESLEYAN CHURCH.</head>
        <p>BEWSEY-ROAD WESLEYAN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>There was a large congregation at this Church on New Year's Eve, the pulpit being ocennitvl by the Rev. Sidney Pitt. who took for his test Job xiv. 14. "All the days of my anpointed time will T wait till my change come." The preacher spoke of the inevitable changes both seen and nnforseen which must take place during the year and of the great change by vrhich all work suc- ceeded and for which there was an appointed time. The rev. gentleman made sympathetic reference to the change which had come over the life of Mr. Lloyd George in the loss of his daughter end said that no doubt the father would have sacrificed even his important position if his child could have been spared. A solo "The star of Bethlehem" was rendered by Mr. Tynan with great _ _ . . spent</p>
        <p>effect. Tho last few moments of the year were in silence, after which an nil Methodist hymn " Co let ns anew" was sling with great heartiness. collnetion which was in aid of Dr. Stevenson Children's Homes, realised n. substantial amount. WYCLIFFE CHURCIFT. ' was</p>
        <p>A watchnight service was held at this church and largely attended. The Pastor, the Rev. J. Yonge in Ft brief address, said it was well in such moments fasten their thought upon their own !ices, and upon t, state of their hearts before God. They needed oppo' timities for careful reflection. Life now-n-days was busy, so crowded with toil, or care, or excitement, ' they were robbed of times of quiet., earnest thou, Hence it we needful to pull themselves up, end mt themselves think about their life, their habits, tht experiences, and their characters. Presence at such service as that was a sign that they recognized thei. relation to God and His to us. Let them he real in such an hour. Did they realize their dependence upon God, and rheir indebtedness to Hie mercy ? Did they . . -ived</p>
        <p>take' time to think of their mercies? Had they receiN, any anqcial blessings, any naw joys or successes thi: year ? Lrt them thank God for them. But anothe thought was that of their manifold shortcomings at wrongdoinam What were the feelings which a car review of 1907 awakened_ in them ? God wished sight.</p>
        <p>candid</p>
        <p>them</p>
        <p>to search their hearts and their lives, ns in His When ther did an. end the conviction of wrong came them, what were they to do? Must they not pray confess, and forsake the sin ?—forseke the secret the sinful habit, the sinful quarrel ? God was set ins their hearts. What did He find there? They not continue in 1908 the wrongs of 1907, but give no, resolutely, in the strength of God. He abundantly pardon, if they would abundantly and forsake. 7oods</p>
