      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>After doing so well at Christmas, Warrington finished up the old year and started the New Year in a very disappointing fashion. For the last Saturday of 1907 they were at Hull, and failed to repeat the victory recorded there last season. On New Year's Day they played the important return match with Runcorn which counted In three League, ad again bad to submit to defeat. There were extenuating circumstances in both matches, but the narrow defeat at Runcorn was Tent galling for it practically robbed Warrington of all chance of securing the South West Lancashire Cup.</p>
        <p>'Misfortune dogged the footsteps of the Warrington men at Hull. They played a fine game and deserved a better fate. In the first half their cleverness in all • departments was pronounced and the homesters looked like being beaten. Lees was the first to cross the line and give Warrington a lead of three points to two Then Tilley was injured and while he was absent Hull got a try and obtained the lead. Almost immediately on resumino. Lees put his shoulder out in attempting a tackle, and took no further part in the game. Then Taylor was hurt and absent for a time. In fact at one period Warrington had only ten men in the field. Yet Hull scored only another five points and the defeated Warrington side came very near getting another try. No doubt ample revenge will be taken out of Hull when they visit Wilderspool on March 7th.</p>
        <p>For the Runcorn match Warrington were decidedly handicapped by the iniuries to Tilley,Taylor and Lees, and the absence of Thomas through the Welsh match at Aberdare. Fish and Isherwood returned, so the team was by no means a weak one and ought to have beaten the Linnets. There were several reasons why they failed. brit the principal one was that with the exception of Dickenson none of the backs placed up to form Still they were quite as good as Dunccrn. and the forwards held an advantage in the scrums which should have been good for more than a solitary try. Runcorn could only cross the line once hut the irrepressible Jolley won the match with a fine dropped goal.</p>
        <p>Warrington had the pleasure of winning the toss and thus obtaining a theoretical advantage Personally I should have preferred that Runcorn had named the exposed side of the coin, even though there was a strong wind blowing down the field. It always seems to me that the team which has the wind in its favour for the concluding stages of the game has an excellent chance of wiping out the deficit of points, if the points are not too many. Thus, on changing ends, it was guineas to gooseberries on Runcorn scoring more than the three points which represented Warrington's total. They knew exactly what they had to do and they did it.. Supposing Warrington had lost the toss and Faced the wind, they would have been equally certain of victory in the second half with such a modest score against them.</p>
        <p>Warrington's failure was in only scoring three points before the interval. It was not so much due to Runcorn's defence as Warrington's weak attack, and poor judgrnent in using the wind. Neither Brooks nor Jenkins made much headway from the scrum, hut early on they sent out passes which might have led to tries. But on the left Isherwood was not as reliable as usual, and en the right Landor seemed wanting in determination nod finish. The combina- tion never once worked with a swing, and the only try was scored by that promising young forward, P. Boardman, who dribbled over when near the line. Fish had two fairly easy shots at goal. hnt never got near the target. Thomas was sadly missed !</p>
        <p>In the second half Runcorn made much better use of the wind. They were always a better punting team than Warrington, and the latter made the fatal mistake of kicking a great deal too much, with the consequence that they rarely entered the Runcorn half. Surely Warrington have learnt by bitter experience in the past that it dreg not pay to kick against a team like Runcorn. And worse still, they kicked with the same stupidity against the wind, only to see the ball come back about double the distance. There was not balf the resource exhibited that one would expect from a team like Werring.ton. They sadly lacked a captain on Wednesday.</p>
        <p>Runcorn played a characteristic game, and played it well. At times they did some smart passing but it was not very dangerous against such a defence as Warrington's—which is second only to Oldham's. Jolley and Watton were very good at half, and the younger man has developed into quite a class player. The Warrington lad, linsworth, did some neat things at centre, while Harrison was brilliant at full back. The forwards worked hard and followed up with speed and untiring persistence. Walker is as good as Padbury notwithstanding the difference in years.</p>
        <p>The referee, Mr. Edmondson, had one had fault and that was bis laxity with regard to obstruction. I never saw more deliberate obstruction of the kicker, and it was mostly by the Runcorn men, yet Warring- ton never got a single penalty for it. In faet Warrington only had about a couple of penalty kicks during the game, whereas Runcorn had a dozen at 'east.</p>
