        <p>Merthyr -- Warrington may as well ivenp all hone of winning the South Lancashire Cup after the defeat at Runcorn on Wednesday. Five points had now been dropped out of a possible fourteen, whereas Wtgan have gained every point hut one, and are not likely to lose any more except perhaps at Wilderspool. The Competition seems a moral certainty for them now. Last season Runcorn were the winners, and Wigan in the two previous years. Wnrrington secured the Cup in 1902, and 1904. If they had been consistent in their play at St. Helens, Widnes, and Rnncorn they need not now have been lamenting.</p>
        <p>The South West Lancashire Competition up-to-date is as follows : _ _ _ Points.</p>
        <p>'. 7 !,. P. W. L. D. For Apt. Pte, Wigan " 5 . 4 2... 1 ... 65... 14 ... 9 Warrington 7 ... 4 ... 2... 1 ...100 ... 21 ... 9 Leigh Itnneorn S . 4 . 4 . 0 61 : 55 8 St. Helena 6 0 4 . . 0 . 36... 81 ... 4 Widnes</p>
        <p>Warrington will have another stiff match to-day if the ground is in playing condition. Salford have always been doughty opponents and just now they are performing strongly. All their men are reported fit and the team is expected to he at full strength. On the contrary Warrington are severely handicapped by injuries to players. Unfortunately since Wednesday Jenkins has had to be added to the list of incapacitated. Kenyon returns at opportune moment, but the com- position of the team will be rather uncertain till the men step on the field.</p>
        <p>Warrington's selection is as follows. Tilley, full back, Fish, Teherwood, Dickenson and Kenyon three- quarters ; Lloyd and Brooks half backs ; Thomas, Shurgars. Heath, A. S. Boardman, P. Boardnyin, and Belton forwards. The reserves are Jordan and Landon</p>
        <p>Salford's team will be : A. Mason; 3. 11. Cook, W. Thomas, J. Lomas, W.</p>
