      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Local Appointments.</head>
        <p>Local Appointments.</p>
        <p>Th 9 following local gentlemen have been appointed magistrates for the county of Cheshire :-- James Hepherd, Appleton, maltster. Henry Roberts, Appleton, merchant. F. W. Monks, Appleton, iron and steel manufacturer. L. Greening, Grappenhall, wire manufacturer. C. T. Whitley, Stretton, architect. G. Dakin, Latchford. R. H. Posnett, Runcorn, tanner. Dr. J. M. Frith, Runcorn. Dr. F. M`Dougall, Runcorn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>Biographical Sketches.</head>
        <p>Biographical Sketches.</p>
        <p>Mr. Henry Roberts, of Kingsley, Hill Cliffe, has played a prominent part in the public and com- mercial life of Warrington. The eldest son of the late ID-. Richard Roberts, he was born in Edgeworth- street in the year 1853. He was educated at People's College (then known as Newton-street School), and subsequently at the Grammar School. In April, 1873, he was apoonated secretary of the Liberal AQsociation and in 1883 his services to the party were, recognized, by a very handsome presentation valued at 120 guineas. He ultimately became agent to the party, succeeding Mr. Ridgway, solicitor, and held the post of secretary and agent for 15 years, and also represented his party in the Revising Barrister's Court. His commercial career has been very successful, his connection with the Warrington Slate Company being well known. He was first elected one of the representatives in the Town Council of the old North-West Ward, but upon the re-arrangement of the wards he was allocated to the Whitezross Ward. He was elected an alderman of the borough in May, 1898, as successor to the late Alderman Edward Greenall, and held the office of Mayor of Warrington in 1899-1900-1. He retired from the Council a few years ago. He was appointed a justice of the peace for the borough by the Chan- cellor of the Duchy of Lancaster in 1892. Mr. Roberts has been president of the Chamber of Commerce, is deputy-chairman of the Education Committee, and has filled numerous public offices. He is now a manag- ing.director of Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons,Ltd.,</p>
        <p>I 'Mr. F. W. Monks, iron and steel manufacturer, Stonecroftt Appleton, Cheshire, has also been long I identified with the commercial progress of Warring- ton. He is the son of Mr. F. Monks, J.P., formerly of Walton Old Hall, and received his early education at the Friends' School, Penketh, from 1868 to 1870, and at Castle Howell School, Lancaster, from 1870 to 1872, ; taking lessons at both in wood-carving and carpenter- ling. At Otven's College, Manchester, from 1872 to I 1874 he took the science course, continuing his studies lin chemistry and the manufacture of iron and steel at the science classes, and geometry, drawing, &amp;c., at the ; School of Art in Warrington, up to 1878. In 1874 he I entered the works of Messrs. F. Monks and Co. (now I Monks Hall and Co., Ltd.), iron manufacturers, &amp;c., ! Liverpool-road, and is now the managing director. The concern is one of the largest and most prosperous of its kind in the district, finding employment for a large number of workpeople. Dir. Monks was a member of the Corporation for a good number of years, his connection terminating during the Non- trading agitatinn. He was chairman of the Sanitary I Works Committee for ten years. during which time j the whole of the plant for producing dried fertilizing , manures was re-Modelled on new lines and re-organized. He was chairman of the Electricity Committee for three years, chairman of the Technical : Instruction Committee for six years, and deputy- chairman of the Tramways Committee at its formation I in 1900 and during the coustruction of the worxe at Howley. He has taken a great interest in People's j College, and among other offices he filled he was for 18 years treasurer and secretary of Cairo-street chapel and school, and for two years chairman of the Field Club. Mr. James Hepherd of the Knoll, Appleton, Cheshire, maltster, Winwick-street, is also one of Warrington's , best known townsmen.He is a son of the late Alderman James Hepherd, who was mayor of the borough in 1889-3, and who died on the 12th December, 1879. Mr. Hepherd was app minted a justice of the peace for the borough by the Chancellor of the Duchy of Lancaster in 1897, and is very tegular in his attend- ance at the Court. HA is greatly interested in horses and agricultural matters generally. Mr. Hepherd was a member of the "Town Council from 1889 to 1896, THE CHRISTMAS OF A SHIPWRECKED CREW. being the successful candidate for the representation The Crozets are a group of rooky islands in thel of St. Austin's Ward. He was defeated at the election southern Indian Ocean, far off the track of the great in 1898, when are-arrangement of the ward was matte Australian liners, and a favourite ground for whaling and three Liberals (Messrs. D. Tinnion, T. Burton and expeditions. The Christmas Double Number of the John Crosfield) were returned. In Pall Mall Magazine " contains a long and faithful Mr. Linn:ens Greening, who resides at Grappenhall record, with many fine illustrations, of the hardships of a Norwegian crew which was wrecked there in is a prominent and active business man, being ; December last, and endured the depth and rig ours of an managing director of Greening and Sons, Ltd., the : almost Antarctic winter. Not the least interesting well-known firm of wire manufacturers. He served portion is the account of such Christmas rejoicings as the town for some years in the Council Chamber as a they managed to improvise out of the wretched fare representative of Latchford Ward, buttof late years he that remained from the wreck. After untold sufferings ! has not taken any part in municipal politics. He is, from exposure, they contrived to collect driftwood from the ship House" build a hut, which they called however, a member of the Museum Committee, and in that institutian he takes a very keen interest. bringing i Dhurriirtiginathelif'irstefewindahosnoit,er foofretheestetitnsoninto shape his well-known expert knowledge to bear. He is (says the narrator, Captain Bull, %e leader of the recognized as an authority on natural history, and expedition) we had a variety of food, the different some time ago he presented a valuable collection to ingredients of which would no doubt astonish any man the natural history section. He is a Unionist, but so with a sense of gastronomy. I would here mention that we had on board a fair quantity of kerosene for strong are his Free Trade meonvictions thal he worked our motor-engine; this oil of course ran out when the bard for Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P., at the last election. ! is a vveesssseeLsettfiftee, and all tbeaes,prt;ievuirsioannsdsaavederfrom Mr. George Dakin, of Arlington, Latchfot d, the all retired merchant, nnd is an untiring worker in the quantitly of l':read—got taint of kerosene': byesi:i3el9 a cause of charity. Be has rendered splendid services thorough soaking of the sea-water. Well, a peculiar to the Infirmary and Dispensary as hon. treasurer dish was prepared by our cook, the taste of which 1 ever since 1801, while he is chairman of the House don't think we shall forget to our dying day; and here Visitors and Finance Committee, a vice-president and is the recipe, in case some of you might like to try it •, life governor of the institution. He is a trustee of bread soaked in sea-water, flavoured with kerosene and % he asd with a fair quantity of fine sand from the beach both the Blue Coat School and the Savings Bank, and and s from the open fireplact, all jumbled up with was formerly a churchwarden at Christ Church plentye of corned beef and made into a heavy thick stew. Latchford. Eaten hot, it grew in your mouth every time you moved Mr. C. T. Whitley, architect, of Strettnn, served your tongue, and the peculiar feeling of sand between his time with Mr. Joseph Clarke, Stratford-place, your teeth and the strong taste of kerosene made you Lor don, nod afterwards went into partnership with fear for the moment that you would never be able to Messrs. E. W. Fry, architects and surveyors, of Dover. swallow it. t the hut eount wasbo Dari(iu.iTtaseaved, and contributed largely, He came to Strettnn in the year 1877, where he has ep rmcaormr a fno retd and for the e I lh-ibteeihneg. t)r oouo since resided. He takes a great interest in Church There was a special small matters in Strettnn, and is at the present time treasurer one of the big oil-kettles found on gthe Island being n and manager of the schools, used as a fireplace, and as we settled down more and MAGISTRATES SWORN IN. more to our new' and strange life, everything began to take a fairer shape and form. The chow-chow was no Mr. Greening, Mr. Heplie;d, Mr. Roberts and Mr. longer in requisition, and, properly fried or boiled, Whitley were sworn in as county magiatrates at the penguin, with soup, was now one of our most common Cheshire Quarter Sessions, at Chester, on Monday. dishes. At a later stage of our insular life, our cook invented a kind of mincemeat cakes of albatross meat. which, fried in whale blubber, were really delicious. I had brought with me from home a small pine-tree, which we had carefully nursed on board and kept green all the time. Loving bands had sent with us heavy parcels of presents for the crew and all kinds of orna- ments for the tree, and Captain ITee and I were on the passage always hopeful of spending a merry Christmas in the small but comfortable cabin on board the schooner. Well, these hopes were not fulfilled, and the tree of course disappeared in the wreck- age ; but the sea gave back Captain Bee's Christmas parcel, containing a nice lot of things for himself and a pair of woollen mittens knitted by his sisters for each of the crew. One of the small coloured Christmas candles was picked up on the beach, and, together with a few inches of stearine candle, enabled us to place two candles on our table, besides a , couple of bouquets of native flowers planted in empty milk-tins to say nothing of the ornamental display of a small table-cloth and two miniature Norwegian flags ; so that the table, to us, looked almost dazzling with light and glittet. Captain Bee has boasted as his t , private property four bottles of brandy, the only spirits on board. Two bottles bad been served out on special occasions during the passage, the third was given out, when Hog Island was sighted through the mist, and strangely enough, the last bottle was saved and carefully guarded for Christmas Eve. At six o'clock supper, consisting of a splendid so-called butter-1 porridge with milk, was served, and we all had our very 1 liberal share of the substantial meal. Tn the course of the evening, the Gospel of " Pea 3 on earth and good- will towards men " was read, a few words said about Peace on earth," some Christmas hymns were sune, and finally a few tales re-told from a hook. At nine o'clock our Christmas Eve at the island ended, everyone pleased and satisfied with the event of the day. The account goes ou to relate many adventures on the island,the sending of messages by all the albatrosses j they managed to capture, and the rescue of the crew by 1 a passin Australian steamer. The chief heroes have I since been decorated by the King of Norway.</p>
        <p>The following is an account of how Mlozi, the Arab chief, was captured by a native named Bandawe, who was a sergeant-major in the native army with the expedition : Somebov or other Bandawe had learnt that in a corner of the square of Mlozi's house was a trap-door, and below this an underground cellar. When our party had ceased investigating Mlozi's house. Bandawe alone and unaided found this trap-door, conceMed under a large bed ; and pulling it open and cautiously descend- ing a few steps, had seen Mlozi crouching in a corner of the cellar. Threatening him with instant death with his revolver if he made the least resistance, he had captured the Arab, tied him up with strips of his own cotton garments, and eventually, with the aid of a few soldiers, had brought him into our camp. "That night we enjoyed several hours' sleep, but not much, as we bad to be astir early in morning in case the forces of Mlozi's allies should be on as. As the day grew and they did not appear rumour spread that they had heard through fugitives of the fall of Mlozi's town,and bad decided to call and consider whether further hostilities were advisable. Eventually they broke up their camp and retreated to a safer distance, from which their I.3aders opened negotiations, receiving promises that their lives would be spared and their private property respected if they came in and surrendered. Some of these Arabs were exiled to the coast, but not a few of the younger men, less concerned with the old slave- raiding practices, were allowed to settle down on tracts of land assigned to them, and for aught T know are now tax-paying, law-abiding inhabitants of British Central Africa. Mlozi, the Arab chief, was tried for the murder of the hostages, found guilty, and hanged. Within a week from our arrival at Karonga the Arab power at the north end of Lake Nyasa was for ever broken, and all the Arab fortresses were either being demolished or were held by British troops. From December, 1895, it map he said with truth that the slave-trade, and slave- raiding to feed the slave-trade, came to an end in the British Central Africa Protectorate."</p>
        <p>"A CHURCHYARD COUGH,,</p>
        <p>Declared Hopel.sA, but Cured by VENO S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE. Mr. J. Churchill, of Dowlish Wake, Ilminster, Somerset, says : Nearly 30 years ago I contracted inflammation of the lungs which left me with Bronchial Asthma of a very severe type, my cough was so had that people said if ever they heard a churchyard cough, T had it, and at times the paroxysms were so bad that I could not lie in bed. Three doctors said that would never get better, but at last I was introduced to try VeTIO'S Lightning Cough Cure, and soon found T VC ns on the right track at last, and now thanks to your extraordinary remedy my asthma is entirely gone, and I am at work as hard as ever." _</p>
        <p>Veno's Lightning Cough Core is the purest, safest, and most reliable remedy extant for coughs and colds, bronchitis, asthma, catarrh, sore throat. hoarseness, old-age coughs. children's coughs, and and all chest and lung troubles. Price OM., is: 1,1 d., and 2s. 9d., of all chemists and drug stores every- where. •</p>
