      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>80-van OR BOV-BIL.</head>
        <p>80-van OR BOV-BIL.</p>
        <p>IThe question that has been agitaring the minds of the public lately as to the pronunciation of the famous word Bovril has been decided in no uncertain manner by the recent competition. Notwithstanding the large number who voted for 80-vril, the verdict has been given. by a majority of 04,840, in favour of 130v-ril, and Mrs. Brain, of Shirehampton, near Bristol, by giving the exact number forming the majority, has been made the fortunate recipient of £lOO. The second ' prize of £25 was, with one of the .£5 prizes, divided equally between Mr. E. Chatel, of the Isle of Man, and Mr. Tizard, of Uxbridge-road, London, both thesel gentlemen tieing for second place.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>MAGAZINE CUTTINGS.</head>
        <p>MAGAZINE CUTTINGS.</p>
        <p>THE LAST STRUGGLE WITH THE SLAVE-</p>
        <p>TRADERS IN BRITISH CENTRAL AFRICA. I "This is to be the concluding episode in the long eight years' struggle which Great Britain has waged, first unofficially through a trading company, and through such gallant volunteers as Captain (now General Sir Frederick) Intgard, and then through tho forces of the British Central Africa Protectorate," writes Sir Harry H. Johnston, G.C.M.G., K.C.8., in the January " Windsor," in an interesting article entitled, "The Last Struggle with the Slave-traders in British Central Africa." • For some six years actual fighting has been laid aside. After the first crusade, negotiation with the Arabs was tried, in the hope that a remedy might be found by guaranteeing to them the territory they occupied, by exemption from taxation—in short, by granting them nearly every privilege and facility except that of continuing to raid any longer the surrounding country for the supply of slaves to the Zanibar markets. At first tne European used in this war no disciplined men, merely two or three thousands Negroes whom tho cruelties of the Arabs bad rendered desperate. But the fortresses built by the Arabs, fourteen miles inland from Karonga, on the road between Lakes Nyasa and Tanganyika, had easily withstood undisciplined assault, which usually ended by the Negroes reckless flight at the first loss from the guns or rifles of the beseiged. For six years there had been an armed truce on both sides, and during those six years the British power had been firmly established on the west and south of Lake Nyasa and on the coffee-growing Shire Highlands. Here we had had enough to do to repel the assaults of the Muhammadan Yaos, led or inspired by Zanzibar Arabs. The fortune of war bad sometimes wavered ; British officers had been trapped and killed in ambush, and their forces— Indian and Negro—cut up, annihilated. But, chiefly with the aid of artillery (new to the wars of these lands), mountain fortress after mountain fortress had been assulted and taken by handfuls of brave men against thousands of Muhammadan Negroes. At last there was not an Arab dan left on the waters of Lake Nyasa (except such as sailed under. the British flag). Gunboats—British and German —patrolled the Lake, the River Shire had become a safe channel for peaceful commerce, and there only remained as the opponents of the white man's will in all Nyasa- land that group of North Nyasa Arabs under the leadership of Mlozi. Mlozi was not only the accepted , sultan or leader of these Maskati Arabs who had been settled for about twenty years on the north-west shores ' of Lake Nyasa (commanding the route to Tanganyika, and, still more, the route to Bangweulu, Mweru, the ! Luangwa River, and the Upper Congo), but he had also become the recognized leader of all the Arab settlers, I conquerors, slave-traders and merchants between Nyasa I on the east and the boundaries of the Congo Free State on the west.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS</p>
        <p>THE SANSE OF HUMOUR. He was a college student who Was brawny and untamed; He had a sense of humour, too, Or so, at least, he claimed. He helped to haze a boy to death— For fun, though, be it known— He wouldn't rob a fly of breath, Except for sport alone.</p>
        <p>On Hallowe'en he sauntered out To smash things where be might; He tore off gates and roamed about And all the chairs in ight He pounded into kindling wood— In fun, of course, you know— He shattered windows where he could And burned a shed or so.</p>
        <p>An ill-bred man whose fence was wrecked Jumped on the student's frame, His way was far from circumspect, His language was the same. "He lacked the sense of humour," thus The boy spake wretchedly,— :When he revived—which- teaches us How, ill this lack may be. Daughter: "You told me that when I wanted a favour of my husband I should ask him after dinner. The first time I tried it he not only refused, but he was just as cross and ugly as could be. He never refused me anything before —boo, hoo, boo!" Mother: "Bless_ me, my dear, you shouldn't have asked him after a dinner that you had cooked yourself." Miss Million: "So you know Miss Tiptop, the belle of the season?" Mr. Sharpwit (after Miss Million's heart, band, and bank account): "Oh, yes; she and I move in—in—ah—much the same set." Miss Million: "By the way, here comes Miss Tiptop now. We will meet her face to face. * * Why, she did not recognise you." Mr. Sharpwit: "She always acts that way when I'm with a prettier girl than sha :e." Great Editor: "Here is 10,000 dollars. Go to Madam Highart, the famous actress, now at the St. Gabriel Hotel, shake the money in her face, and tell her—before witnesses, remember —that we will give her that if she will write us a short story." Trusted Employee : "I think she will accept." Editor: ',Tee Whittaker! Then On% go." seeeteGiri (in photographic gallery): "I wish you wouldn't put that big camera so close to me." Photographer: "Don't you want the picture cabinet size?" Sweet Girl: "Yes, but I don't want the freckles cabinet size." Young Wife (time, midnight): "Quick! Quick! Wit% up! I hear someone down- stairs." Husband (sleepily): "What do they seem to be doing?" Wife: "Hark! Hear that! They're in the pantry. I heard my cake box rattle.' Husband (wearily): "Tell them to please not to die in the house." American Father: "How is this? Before your marriage I asked for some proof that you had credit abroad. You immediately drew a cheque on a Paris bank for a large sum, and it was honoured. Now you tell me you have not a. cent to your name." French Count (his son- in-law) : "Not a centime." "How did that cheque come to be paid, then? Explain that." "3 previously had informed zee Paree bankaire zat I Vas engaged to your daughtaire." Guest: "Who is this Miss Highmind that all the ladies are so enthusiastic over? I never before knew of a woman whom every other woman spoke well of." Host: "She is a very ' fine old lady, now in her ninety-ninth year." Patent Office Clerk: "Your machine is so com- plicated that some part or other will be sure to infringe on some other invention, and make trouble, with no end of court costs." Poor In- ventor: "I can't afford such expenses." "Hadn't you better try something simpler at first. What is that in your other hand?" "This? A bit of wire I picked up in the street. A button is off .and I'm going to we it to fasten up my sus- penders." "Well, we'll give you a patent on that." Mrs. Literati: "My little son has the most re- markable powers of imagination I ever knew in one so young. He will surely be a writer, and a successful one. Western Guest: "Successful! He kin jist roll in wealth. Quick es he's old enough you take him out West and start him in real estate." Wife: "My dear,. I need a little more of this stuff, and some trimming to match. I wish you would drop into Bigg, Sale, and Co.'s, and gel it." Husband' (a smart fellow): "Let me see. Oh, I know. That's the stone where they have so many pretty girls, isn't it?" "Y -e-s." "Yes, I remember. That blond girl at the trimming counter knows your tastes and will doubtless select just the sort of trimming you want—l mean the girl with the golden hair, alabaster skin, blue eyes end sweet little " "There are a number of things I want down town. Never mind, dear. I'll go and get them myself." Mrs. De Ruffle: "If von ever did any good in this wide world, I'd like to know what it is.. Mr. De Ruffle: "Well, for one thing, I saved you from dying an old maid." Hurried Editor : "I must have some one to help on this at once. Where is Binks? Where's Scribbler? Where!s; Leader?" Boy: "They is all downstairs gettin' up a list of folks killed last year by electric cars, cabs, wcggons, subway ex- ploeions, gas, an' lectric light wires." Editor (despondently): "They won't get through for a month." It was on the old camp ground. "Pass de hat," suggested Bruddah Whe.atly. But the parson raised his hand. "No, eali," he shouted. "dere'll be no hate about it. Pass a tin box wid a chain to it. De last time a hat was passed around hea-h it nevah came back, and I had tc go home bareheaded," "He says he has more money than he known what to do with." "Ah ! then he isn!t. married. A man may have more money than he knowe what to do vvith, but if he has a wife she'll know a thine or two." Little Harold approached his mother and v./1 ji\oe;ki.t,tss: asked, "Mamma, haven't I been a good boy since I began going to Sunday school?" "Yes, dear, you have," answered the fond mother. "And you trust me now, don't you, ma?" "Why, Of course I do." "Then why do you keep the pies locked up in the pantry just the same as ever?' Congressman Perkins was in the office of s friend, a justice of the peace, when a couple came in to be married. After the ceremony the justice accepted a modest fee and handed the bride an umbrella as she went out. Mr. Perkins looked on gravely, and asked: "Do you always do that, Charles?" "Do what? Marry them! Oh, yes." "No. I mean bestow a present on the bride." "A present? Why. WRATI't that her umbrella?" gasped the justice. "No; it was mine," replied the congressman, sadly. Friend: "Do you never take a vacation, doctor?" Doctor: "Very seldom. I'm kept busy all summer doctoring people who have been taking vacations." "There goes a woman with a hitteyv," re- marked the clerk in a bookstore, as -a lady passed out at the front door. "How do you know?" queried the proprietor. "Because," explained the clerk, "I just sold it to her." Cigar Manufacturer: "Yes, sir, it's an actual fact cigar-boxes are not made out of cedar at all; they are made out • of paper and coloured with cedar extract." Friend: "Well! well! Now won't you please tell me 11.11:I:itekdesoigina`g7A:ailoirlaeyt;:c1:aodlyevoiaocufk:s;p4Ose my wife has "I don't kaow." see went all over the town the week before my birthday, trying to buy me some post- that you had joinedn'c' prandial cigars. She said that she had read them Iwtsiv:-aapsenrssatttr'i'polniaseld guard.'' to a heari d , a • . * ate u elpp.sgeo3t t be sa? Cholly: "Ah! 3''know, I always said manckrell now that tint, 17,4513(14;1'r, r'e' a_seldier, getpecralnaatnsbyeaoruloletors,t. tibley,re' p‘ripnilryet:h.eshe told me last night that Miss p , so eon- ceiSthecle:! "But I'm an they're and you don't think I'm conceited, do a you?" She * e a man. 'n(socte!kilnginteoanretchoeyebrigheoai.eleelst;): the little ones 0 course don't count."</p>
