      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>PIANOS &amp; ORGANS</head>
        <p>PIANOS &amp; ORGANS</p>
        <p>All the best Makers.</p>
        <p>PHONOGRAPHS, DISC MACHINES &amp; RECORDS. STOCKTAKING SALE THEW UGHOU C j A NUARY GREAT REDUCTIONS.</p>
        <p>RICHMOND JONES &amp; SONS, 11 &amp; 13 BERRY STREET, Top of Bold Street, LIVERPO L.</p>
        <p>WRITE OR CALL FOR ILLUSTRATED CATALOGUE</p>
        <p>In 1/- &amp; 216 Tins; also 4d. Packets.</p>
        <p>'RIZE—CONUNDRU3S,. I ha,ve not head, and a tail I lack, But oft have arms, and legs, and back; I inhabit the palace, the tavern, the cot, 'Tis a beggarly residence where I am not. If a monarch were present (I tell you no fable), I still should be placed at the head of the table. Answer: Chair.</p>
        <p>• 3rd PRIZE—CONUNDRUM. Two legs sat on three legs, With one leg in his lap; In comes four legs And runs away with one leg. Up jumps two legs, Catches up three legs, Throws it after four legs' And makes him bring Lack one leg. Answer: One leg is a leg of mutton; two legs, a man; three legs, a stool; four legs, a dog. ERNEST GOODALL.</p>
        <p>2nd PRIZE—JOKE COMPETITION. DIABOLO.</p>
        <p>Two small boys were gazing at a shop window in which was placed a set of Diabolo. "Ha. does ta play at that?" said the first boy. " Why. thA. goas into backyard," says the other, "'wi' them two sticks, and tha throws up thi bobbin and catches it on that theer string as many times as tha can."•</p>
        <p>" replied the other, "How if it goas throo the winder?"</p>
        <p>"Oh," says the narrater, "tha catches it wi' thi mother this time!" HERBEIk RHYMES.</p>
        <p>3rd PRIZE—JOKE COMPETITION. A CURIOUS REQUEST.</p>
        <p>A mother once sent her little boy to a shop for some dye.. But he forgot his errand by playing on the way. When he reached the shop he asked, "Please what is it that people have when they die .(dye)?" " Some have influenza.," said the astonished grocer. "Well," said the boy, "I suppose that is right. Please give me a pennyworth." CISSY FLETCHER.</p>
        <p>2nd PRIZE—CHRISTMAS POETRY. CHRISTMAS TREE FAIRY. I know a little fairy, A kind, good fairy she, Who comes to us at Christmas-time, And brings the Christmas tree. She is the sweetest fairy In all the fairy-books, Although I've never seen her, And don't know how she looks. ; For mother says she dare not Face a little human child, "Though I've seen her very often,". Mother said, and smiled. But if the —children see them, The fairies disappear, And that's the reason, mother says, I've never seen one here. You never hear her coming, She brings the tree at night; Foc fairies are such dainty folk, They cannot bear the light. It's hung all o'er with candles, I should think that there must be, At the very least a million Little candles on our tree. She chooses toys that children like, And hangs them within reach, And ties a little ticket, With some one's name on each. , A wooden horse for Harry, A drum for little Dot; A new silk dress for Dolly, With a nice blue bow for Spot. FLORENCE COITLING. 3rd PRIZE—CHRISTMAS POETRY. WINTER. I'm glad the winter-time is come, I like the frosty weather; We made a slide as we came home, Dolly and I together; And now we've made the house quite gay. In readiness for Christmas Day. We've hung the hall with evergreen, We've decked the stairs with holly, With sprays of ivy in between, I like them, so does Dolly; But, best of all, we think, you know, Is this big bough of mistletoe. JOHN LISTER.</p>
        <p>Ag 04140,,</p>
