      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <p>....-4......-..--.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle INed,—l have got a pet kitten. It hi gray in colour. When it can it gets upstairs on the beds and pulls the clothes off. It walks up my head to pull my hair. It gets up mother's shoulder when she is making the pudding. Then it got on the table. When I took it off it scratched my face. Sometimes it gets on the table and under the fire, and burns its, fur. One morning it got into the tub of flour. When it came out it looked so funny, its mother ran it out of the house. It goes in Mrs. Faulkner's house. When she went up- stairs it went after her. It stayed up when she came down. The window was open, and it fell out. I was sewing last night, and it got in my work-box, and begun to pill my cotton and pins out. When it is naughty I call it naughty Topsy. On Sunday it got on the boiler top. It gets in the garden to pull the flowers up. KATE WILSON.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l have a dear little kitten called Topsy, with blue eyes, that follows me. wherever I go. I sometimes harness it to a little light cart, and it will goy full speed round the room. I dress it upf in a little bormet and jacket, and sit it in a chair. It lco::s ever so wise. Then I let it have a drink of milk, and we make pretend it is a. visitor to tea. . LIZZIE .I.lAwzn.</p>
        <p>A Norwich Schoolboy. when asked to define "lawsuit," replied, "The things a policeman wears."</p>
        <p>People who wonder why men's hair turns gray before their whiskers should reflect that there is about twenty years difference in their re- sneetive ages.</p>
