      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>MY DEAR CHILDREN,</head>
        <p>MY DEAR CHILDREN,</p>
        <p>I hope the prize-winners have all received their boxes of chocolates, etc. I sent them all off before Christmas, so that they should be at their destinations before the holidays. I can only say how sorry I am that I could not send chocolate to every contributor; of course, that would not have been possible, as so many sent in for competition. I hope to be able to pub- lish a good many of the contributions as time goes on. Regarding the poor children's clothes, I most gratefully acknowledge. articles from many of my members, received during the last week. Don't forget there are some nice boxes of Fry's chocolates for the senders of the best articles during the present month. Many thanks to Elsie Niblett for her letter. I shall be very pleased to receive anything, Elsie,. in the way of warm clothing. Welcome to you and Edith Edinboro as new members. I trust that you all have had a most happy Christmas.</p>
        <p>The followirtg members have sent in clothing for the children during the past week. Very many thanks indeed:— Gertie Fisher, Annie Winter, Nellie Holings- worth, Gladys Starbuck. Ada Starbuck, Jennie Rushby, Lily Lunn, Ada Conling, Katie Wilson, Maggie Faulkner, Lilian Coites, and Gladys Grover.</p>
        <p>A family of seven have benefited this week. Extreme want of clothing is their claim, and I have been most glad to give them help through my kind-hearted little members. With love UNCLE NED.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>NEW MEMBERS.</head>
        <p>NEW MEMBERS.</p>
        <p>Winnie Harvey; Elsie Kate Bird, Cyril Grange, May Grange, Lily Rose, Herbert Rhymes, Clara Whitmore, Tames Edward Lister, eissy Fletcher, Robert Ellison, Mabel Blake, Eve Bennett, Lizzie Sutcliffe.</p>
        <p>3rd PRIZE—STORY COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>HOME FOR THE CHRISTMAS HOLIDAYS.</p>
        <p>"Look here, Hilda," cried Rupert Chester to his sister, as he ran upstairs two steps at a time; "father is going out shopping, and he says that we may go with him. Oh, isn't it jolly to be at home once more, and done with school, at least for a time?" "Yes, indeed," said the girl; "but what does father want to buy?" "A Christmas turkey, of course," said her brother; "and evergreens to deck the house with, and, oh! twenty other things. Now, get on your hat, Hilda, and see that you wrap up warm, for it is terribly cold, and snow is begin- ning to fall." "Oh, how I wish dear mother could have come, too!" said the little girl, softly. "Oh, Rupert, don't you think she looks weak' and ill? When she put her arms about me and kissed me this morning, I really felt ready to cry." "Take care you don't do that." said the boy. "Father says she needs cheering up, and he looks to us to help him in keeping her bright. I mean to try my best. I am going to buy her a Christmas present—a white mouse, or some- thing of that kind." " Oh, not a. mouse!" said the girl; "it would scare poor mother out of her senses if it ran about her bed. No, Rupert; but nurse says she seeds a velvet work-bag, and a new pair of bedroom slippers; these would be good presents, don't you think?" "All right," said the boy; "but, see, father is beckoning to us to go to him—l suppose to help to choose the turkey." Rupert was right Mr. Chester wished his little daughter to select a nice, plump bird, which was speedily done; and a splendid Norfolk turkey was sent home, with as many evergreens as would dress up the whole house.</p>
        <p>Then mother's presents were bought—a pretty work-bag from Rupert. and a pair of blue bed- room slippers from Hilda, after which the children found that they had each a sixpence left over. "Now, what shall we do with our sixpences?" said Rupert. "I think I should like some Everton toffee; what do you say to that. Hilda?"</p>
        <p>"No. I don't care for toffee," said his sister; "I think I will 'give my sixpence to nurse's little nephew, who has been so ill. He is coming to spend the day with her. What a treat a whole sixpence would be to him, Rupert!" _</p>
        <p>"So it would." said he. "Well, here goes." and he handed his sixpence to Hilda. "You take care of it; perhaps I might lose it, or change my mind." So Hilda gladly took care of it.</p>
        <p>• The young Chesters' Christmas party was a very happy one. Dear mother was able to sit up at table; some young cousins were also there, while in the servants' hall there sat a pale-faced, but happy, little boy, with two silver sixpences in his hand—the very first silver money which he had ever had in his young life. Everybody will enjoy Christmas a great. deal more, if they have made sonic poor, forlorn child happy with an unexpected present. MARGERY HOCKLEY. '</p>
        <p>A country curate complained to old Dr. South that he received only five pounds for preaching a certain sermon at Oxford. "Fire pounds!" said the doctor; "I wouldn't have preached that sermon for fifty!"</p>
        <p>• MY PICTURE. On my wall there hangs a picture, In a setting old and quaint; Such a group of childish faces. As Sir Joshua loved to paint. One a rosy, laughing baby, One with wistful, tender eves, Dreaming still, perchance, of glory Left behind in Paradise. One with curly tresses falling O'er a brow undimmed by care; While the fourth smiles bravely on me From a mist of golden hair. Ah ye children, many a sorrow Your glad faces have beguiled, Still to-day, as once aforetime, - In our midst Christ sets a child. • NELLIE PAYNE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
