        <p>CONSTIPATION AND PILES</p>
        <p>A Police. Officer s 20 Years' Agony.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Chas. Fordes Bile Beans Effect a Complete and Lasting Cure.</head>
        <p>Chas. Fordes Bile Beans Effect a Complete and Lasting Cure.</p>
        <p>Scores of people suffer at this period from con- stipation, due to the effect on the liver of rapid changes of temperature. Chas. Forde's Bile Beans cure both cmistipation and its consequent ailtnent, piles. Mr. Thomas Quinev, of 79, Ash road, Saltley, Birmingham,says : 'rTwenty years ago, when in the Warwickshire Consts.bulary, I had to walk from Henley-in-Arden to Preston Baggt, to meet the Superintendent. I was hot and tired when I got there, and sat down on the stone parapet of a bridge. A few days later. I began to have pains in the lower part of the body, and to suffer with piles: That was the beginning of twenty years' suffering. " Every few weeks from then I had attacks of piles and constipation. The agony I suffered was terrible. The ailments disordeted my whole system, robbed me of appetite, of rest, of strength, of all enjoymeut in life. In twenty years I tried . scores of medicines, hurt they all failed to per- manently cure me, I tried Chas. Forde's Bile Beans and felt better after a few boxes. 1 continued to take Chas. Forde's Bile Beans until I was entirely free from piles and constipation, and I was quite cured."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>FACTS CONFIRMED ON OATH,</head>
        <p>FACTS CONFIRMED ON OATH,</p>
        <p>- Before a Commissioner for Oaths, Ur. Quincy made the following Sworn Declaration of the facts of his cure : _ .</p>
        <p>" I, Thomas Quiney, of 334, Bordesley Green •Road, Saltley, Birmingham, do hereby solemnly declare that for twenty years I was a great sufferer from Hmnaorrhoids (piles). Also that after trying many patent medicines and pills, many of which only afforded temporary relief, I have been cured by undergoing a course of Chas. Forde's Bile Beans for Biliousneggs. Further, I give full permission to the Bile Bean Co. to make use of this Sworn Declaration, in any way they may choose, to make known that it was solely by Chas. Forde's Bile Beans for Biliousness that my cure was effected.</p>
        <p>(Signed') Tnoaus QUINEY. " Declared at Birmingham this 20th day of April, 1904, before me, Philip Baker, a Com- missioner for Oaths." Writing to-day, Mr. Quiney says : —" It is now four since Chas. Farde's Bile Beans cured me of piles, and I have not had the slightest return of the complaint. My health has remained splendid." 'Chas. Forde's Bile Beans convey the gift of robust health and renewed vitality which are impossible from any other medicine. Every home should keep handy a box of the gentiine Chas. Forde's. Of all chemists, in sealed boxes only, at Is. 111 or 2s. Oct. Refuse substitutes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>LEGISLATION IN 1907.</head>
        <p>LEGISLATION IN 1907.</p>
        <p>Some Acts that Have Just Come into Operation.</p>
        <p>The legislative output for the year 1907 comprises no fewer than fifty-six public statutes, many of which are of considerable importance, and make various useful improvements in the existing law. Some are already in force, whilst others will come into operation during the early part of this year. An Act of great importance to all engaged in trade is the Limited Partnerships Act, which operates from the first of January. It introduces here a system of partnerships after the Continental type. A limited partner under the Act is a person who on entering into partnership contributes a stated amount as his share of the capital, beyond which he is not liable for the debts or obligations of the firm. Such a partner has no power to bind the firm, and is not permitted to take part in the management of the business, though he may examine into the state of the business and advise with the other partners tnereon, If a limited partner draws out or receives back any part of his contribution, he will be liable for the obliga- tions of the firm up to the amount so received back. THE PUBLIC TRUSTEE.</p>
