        <p>PROBATION OF OFFENDERS.</p>
        <p>tion as to the drift of the ice across the un-</p>
        <p>The Probation of Offenders Act, which came into operation on the let of January, is characteristic of the 1 humanitarian tendencies of the period, and of a corn- mendable desire to prevent the making of criminals. The Act permits the release on probation of offenders against the law, where it appears inexpedient to the 1 Court to convict the accused or inflict any punishment 1 other than a nominal punishment. 1 The character, antecedents, age, health, or mental condition of the person charged will each or all be taken into account. So, too, notice will be taken of the tact that the offence is of a trivial nature, or that It was committed under extenuating circumstances. The offender may he discharged conditionally on his entering into a recognizance to he of good behaviour, and to appear for conviction and sentence when called upon, during such period (which is not to exceed three , years) as may be specified in the Order. Probation officers are to be appointed, and it will be their duty to visit offenders dealt with under the ACE, and to assist and befriend them, and, when necessary, endeavour to dui them suitable employment.— (" Evening Chronicle.")</p>
        <p>known north, says Dr. Fridtjof Nansen in the Geograplaral Journal. We have, how- ever, to distinguish between two kinds of driftwood, namely, modern driftwood, which is found on the drifting ice, or floating in the sea, or lying on the shores near the present shore-line, and which has evidently been car- ried thither recently, and what we may call post-glacial or semi-fossil driftwood, which is found on land at levels generally above the present shore-line, and often high above it. Great accumulations of the latter kind of driftwood are found on many places of the Siberian coast, where it has been called Noah wood. I have there seen thick layers of it in the lake-beds and river-beds. The Nares expedition found great quantities of post- glacial driftwood on the northern coasts of Ellesmere Land; it has also been observed on Prince Patrick Island. on .rlanks &amp;c. The occurrence of this wood on northern coasts, which are now closely ice-bound all COMMERCIAL FACLURES.—According to Kern p's the year round, Mercantile Gazette, the total number of Commercial I4DICATES A MILDER CLIMATE, Failures recorded in England and Vales during the week with a more open sea during the period when ending Friday, December 27th, was 106, viz. :—New woo was depos driftd -ited on t he coasts, Bankruptcy Proceedings published in the London this Gazette, 55 ; Deeds of Arrangement registered, 51. The which at that time were more submerged respective numbers in the corresponding week of last than they are now. For on an ice-hound coast year were ; Bankruptcies, 50 : Deeds or Arrangement, much driftwood cannot possibly be carried .10—Total 100 : being an increase of 6. The total ashore and remain there. The wood is car- number of Commercial Failures recorded during the ried along with the ice, generally more or 52 weeks of the p4st seer is 7,828 ; the total number less embedded in it, and where the iCa does recorded in the corresponding 52 weeks of the previous not melt away, or there is no open water of year wae 8,329, showing a decrease of :101. importance, the driftwood gets very little op- portunity of being thrown on to the beach. It can only happen by very rare accidents. We must, therefore, expect to find extremely little driftwood on the ice-bound coasts at present. As an example, it may be mentioned that only very little driftwood is found on the northern coasts of Franz Josef Land, al- though this group of islands are not very far distant from the great rivers of Siberia, and although a fairly continuous transport ,f driftwood is carried with the ice past the islands. But the fact that modern driftwood, either Siberian or American, is found on the northern coasts of Greenland, Ellesmere Land, and the Parry archipelago, and even on an ice-floe a few miles off the northern coast of Ellesmere , Land, proves with all de- sirable certainty that this ice is not sta- tionary. but has been drifted across the .North Polar Sea, and that there is a con- tinual ice-drift across the unknown region. But this fact hardly speaks in fairpur, much 'and in the Unknown North,</p>
        <p>LIFE'S LITTLE PLEASURES. Life has no pleasures when the head is aching. That is Nature's revenge for allowing constipation to develop into a settled obstinate disorder. If the system is not cleansed daily every organ rebels and becomes derange The liver is sluggish, the blood is impoverished, the nerves are "on edge," there is a ,stabbing heartburn, you are bilious and feverish. Do 'not fly to purgatives of the drastic kind : they alleviate but they inevitably enfeeble the system. Try the following natural and soothing rmnedy, which your chemist will dispense, or he will supply the ingredients and you can mix them at home by shaking well in a bottle :—Compound Tincture of Pernvion Bark 1 oz., Nitrax Compound 1_ es, Syrup of Orange Peel 2 ozs. Take a teaspoonful in a wineglass of water three times daily after meals and before retiring. With plenty of walking exercise, the result cannot fail to be satis- acLOry.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday January 4, 1908. SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS. THE FREE CHURCHES. r</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday January 4, 1908. SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS. THE FREE CHURCHES. r</p>
        <p>COLOUR AND TASTE.</p>
        <p>A Pittsburg doctor has, it is stated, pre- scribed -variously coloured foods for a patient who is losing his sense of taste. The doctor asserts that certain colours produce in the brain sensations resembling those conveyed through the Medium of the palate, and that it will thus be possible to make the eye super- sede the tongue.</p>
        <p>- (Front Our London Correspondegzt.)</p>
        <p>IN THE NEW YEAR.</p>
        <p>1908 has ken anticipated with eager Inte- rest by the Free Churches as much as 'by the world in general. What it holds in its hand no one can see, but the New Year should offer the sOlution of many problems • that have puzzled us, and afford fresh opportunities for .varied work. Shall we at last see a settle- ment of the tducation trouble? Will the Government make a really substantial con- tribution- to Licensing legislation during the year? What new forms of service will be introduced to the churches, and what new openings shall we lv.tve during the coming twelve months? Th and similar questions Free Churchmen are asking at. this teasou.</p>
        <p>missio.NAßY ACTIVITY.</p>
        <p>The corning year ought to show a revival in missionary activity. To ,point to only one . of our great denominations, .on January 1 Dr. , Wardlaw Thompson succeeded Sir J. Comp- ton Rickett, M.P., as Chairman of the Con- gregaticnal Union. Dr. Thompson is one of the secretaries of the London Missionary Society, and his term of Office should be a Missionary Presidency. Most of the great Isocieties are 'face to face with deficits, and it seems uphill work to keep the attention of !. the ChUrehes on foreign missions. Cannot some ecclesiastical genius devise during this Iyear some new _plan for arousing missionary ' enthusiasm? Perhaps we Shall have the i answer to this question in the great Mis- sionary Exhibition which is to be held shortly I in the Agricultural Hall, London: I hear that the plans are being mad most systemat;- cally, with a view to not only .achieving suc- cess, but deserving it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>STUDY OF EARTHQUAKES.</head>
        <p>THE STUDY OF EARTHQUAKES.</p>
        <p>The object of the International Association of Seismology is the promotion and en- couragement of the study of earthquakes, and one 'of the principal means of attaining this is the publication of catalogues of large and small seismic disturbances for the use of stu- dents. The practical value of the study of earthquakes has been shewn in that country of earthquakes, Japan. From a careful and detailed investigation they have learnt in that country to build earthquake-proof chim- neys, hotels, and other edifices. The theo- retical value of the study, again, is indicated by a paper read by ' Professor Wiechert, in which it was shewn how seismological re- cords could be used 14 the investigation of the physical properties of the earth. peismo- logy thus 'becomes an e*tremely important ad- junct to geology, in which science it may perhaps be said to have had its birth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>ABOUT COMETS.</head>
        <p>ABOUT COMETS.</p>
        <p>Comets are the bulkiest members of the solar system;, but so far as we know, writes a scientific contributor, they are also pro- bably the least massive. This has • been proved in many cases by the fact that when a comet has passed very ,near a planet its at- traction has not been great enough to influ- ence the planet's motion to any appreciable degree. While the, action of the planet on the cemet may have altered the period of the latter by several weeks, the planet's own period has not been changed by so much as a single second; and this proves that the planet's mass must have been at least 100,000 times as great as the comet's. We have, therefore, in a comet a quantity of material very much less than enough to make a planet, spread through a volume far greater than the planet occupies. It is there- fore clear that it must consist either of gases of small density, or of solid particles, sepa- rated by empty spaces much larger than themselves. It is probable that, both these hypotheses are correct, for the spectra of comets shew that part of their light is re- flected sunlight, such as solid bodies would aend us, and part comes from luminous gas —hydro-carbons--giving a series of bright bands identical with those sheivn by the light from the base of a candle flame or a Bunsen burner. We may therefore regard a comet as a swarm of loose particles, carrying with them more br less gas, which move to- gather through empty space, simply because there is no force acting to pull them apakt, strong enough to overbalance their own very feeble gravitation.</p>
        <p>I RESUSCITATION AFTER ASPHYXIA. Professor G. Poe, of South Norfolk, Vir- ginia—a relative, by the way, of Edgar Allan Poe—has been experimenting for more than thirty years on the resuscitation of the vic- tims of asphyxia and the like. He began by restoring to life a rat which was to all appear- ance dead, by filling its lungs with oxygen. The usual method by means of artificial re- spiration in many cases does not succeed be- cause it is hardly energetic enough to restore the lungs and the heart to their normal ac- tivity. Moreover, it demands a vast store of patience on the part of the operator, and great physical effort, continued perhaps for hours. If unsuccessful the mind of the opera- tor must ever be haunted by the feeling that perhaps if his efforts had been more energetic and more prolonged they might have been crowned with success. Dr. Gradenwitz has described SOME STRIKING EXPERIMENTS made before an assembly of medical and scientific men with Professor Poe's patent. To a rabbit were administered 013 grammes of morphine and 113'4 grammes of ether, and the usual tests all went to shew beyond a doubt that the animal was really dead. The apparatus was applied, and after the lapse of three minutes the patient began to breathe naturally, and in six minutes it was hopping about the room. There were no signs of nausea, so that the whole of the ether must have been extracted from the body of he rabbit. A dog was next exposed to acetylene gas for forty minutes, and in its turn was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>SOCIAL SERVICE.</head>
        <p>SOCIAL SERVICE.</p>
        <p>In another 'direction we ought to hear more and more about Social work. There can be no doubt that, a great change has come over the outlook of the Christian Church during the past few decades, and this change has affected not only theological opinions, but practical matters. It is a commonplace ,to say that we now recognise that man has not only a soul, but a body, to be saved., • The present generation feels that the Housing question and the Temperance question are more important than the relative merits of Calvinism and Arminism, and that a man's environment counts for more than his intel- lectual beliefs. This change of view has not. alone affected the Chutch ; the State has awoke in an eminently practical frame of mind. Legislators at Westminster feel im- pat'ent unless they are attacking some great problem affecting the real welfare of the people, and on all sides it. is admitted that Labour, with all that it means of "unin- teresting" economies, has to .be faced and reckoned with. The State has to deal with Social Problems, and so has the Church, though Of course from a different standpoint. Our outlook must. be broadened, for condi- tions of life and service are more complex.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>FREE CHURCHES AND SOCIAL PROBLEMS.</head>
        <p>FREE CHURCHES AND SOCIAL PROBLEMS.</p>
        <p>In order to show what the Free Churches are doing in regard to Social work, the Free Church Ch ron icle, the organ of the National Free Church Council, has made its January issue a special Social Service num- ber, and in a series of short articles it detds with the various efforts associated with the Free Church Councils. In an introductory article, the Rev. J. Wilson, M.A., secre- tary of the Social Service union ot tne rres- byterian Church of England, makes "A Plea for the Study of Social Problems." "We shall never," he says, " have that effective social application of Chr:stianity until we ourselves make an adequate study of social conditions. Mere sentiment will create no potent public opinion until it is reinforced by knowledge. So that if anyone feels inclined to repeat, regarding social,' problems, the foolish remark made by someone to John Wesley, 'God does not want our knowledge,' it is sufficient to quote Wesley's reply, 'Much less, my friend, does He want your igno- rance.' "</p>
        <p>CO-ORDINATION IN SOCIAL WORE.</p>
        <p>The Social Questions Committee of the National Free Church Council has for some time past been considering how best to pro- mete the work for • which it was appointed, and various schemes, some of them of a very extensive nature, have received the careful consideration of the Committee. The desire has been expressed in many quarters for a closer union between Free Church societies dealing with social affairs, audit was felt that if such Kcieties could meet better under- standing and co-operation would result. Act- ing on this idea, the Social Questions Com- mittee issued- invitations ~to all the Free Church Vnions of Soc:al Service to send repre- sentatives to a Conference, which waS held at the Memorial Hall, under the nresideacy of the Rev. F. B. Meyer, B.A. It was agreed to appoint a committee to prepare a scheme for the Co-ordination of Social Work in con- nection with the Free Churches,- and it Is. expected that this Committee will report. to the Free Church Council in the course of the next. few weeks.. Concerted action should be very useful. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>LIVERPOOL ITER CHURCH INVESTIGATION.</head>
        <p>LIVERPOOL ITER CHURCH INVESTIGATION.</p>
        <p>An interesting development of Free Church work is being undertaken by the Liverpool (~oncil with the New Year. The idea is to nz-ke a complete Survey of the city from the Free Church standpoint, with a view to seeing •how far the present religious needs of Liver- pool are being met by the Churches. Already an outline plan has been approved by the 'Council, and a special Committee has been appointed/4) carry on the work. An under- taking of this kind must, of nec,wsity, occupy several months, and a special organiser has therefore been appointed. Mr. -Wilfrid J. Rowland has been chosen for the work, which has just started. Mr. Rowland, who was for several years financial secretary at Mansfield House University -Settlement, - has been serving the National Free Church Council for about the last four yeers, first in charge of the electoral work, and latterly of the Social Questions Department.</p>
        <p>sounirouT, MS.</p>
        <p>I hear that the forthcoming me9tinas of the National Free Church Council at South- port in March next are to be of the most interesting character. Practically all the leading Free Church leaders are to take part in some way or other at the gatherings. In addition to the sessions of the Council in the daytime,'l understand that some most impor- tant evening demonstratiom: are being ar- ranged, at one of ‘which the President of the Board of Trade is to speak. Mr. Lloyd George has not been among his Free Church people so frequently of late—owing, of eouri,.e, to the onerous duties of his office. He is sure to receive a most hearty welcome. Delegates are now being appointed to these meetings by Free Church Councils all over the lane It is expected that a limited number of personal members' tickets will be issued this year, of which particulars can be obtained of the Roy. Thomas Law, Memorial Hall, London E.C. E.</p>
        <p>Sitop Thefts. Isaac Smith, Elizabeth-street, was sent to gaol for onemouth, at the Warrington Police Court, on Tues- day, on a charge of stealing a pair ri boy's beats, value -is. lid., from the shop of Messrs. Stead and Simpson, Florsemarket-stt eet.— Isabella Lane pleaded guilty to siealing a pair of trousers from the shop of Messrs. Jackson, pawnbrokers, and also a pair of boots, value :Is. lid., from the pawnshop of Thomas Doraville.— She was sent to gaol for fourteen days.</p>
      </div>
