      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith. Matter intended for publication must he written on one side only of the paper and sent in, if possible, not later than Thursday.</p>
        <p>Parliamentary Debating Society. Sir,—The replies to my letter re the formation of a Parliamentary Debating Society for Warrington have been so uniformly in favour of the project that I have arranged for a preliminary meeting. His Worship the Mayor of Warrington has kindly granted the use of the Council Chamber and promised to preside at the meeting to be held next Thursday. The chair will be taken at 6.45 p.m.—Yours, Stu., Padgate,</p>
        <p>EDMUND OSBORNE. Warrington, January 3, 1908.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>New Vaccination Act.</head>
        <p>The New Vaccination Act.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l should be glad if you would allow me to draw ttention to the fact that from the Ist January, 1903, it will be quite easy for pirents to avoid having their children vaccinated. All that is necessary is for the father to fill op a form which will be supplied by the registrar when the birth of the child is registered. The father must then take the form to any Magistrate (either at the courts, or at his office or residence), or to any Solicitor who is a Commissioner for oaths. The Magistrate or Solicitor will sign the form without any demur, and the father will not have to " satisfy " them that his objection is a conscientious one. If the form is signed by a Magistrate, at the Courts, the Magistrates' Clerk can claim a fee of Is. If it is signed by a Solicitor who is a Commissioner for Oaths, the fee will be Is. lid. Even if this fee is paid it will be cheaper to avoid vaccination than to submit to it ! The form must be signed within four months after the birth of the child, and when it has been signed it must be sent to the Vaccination Officer, within seven days, otherwise the parent will be liable to be fined for not having his child vaccinated. The Act applies to all children born after the Ist September, 1907.—Yours &amp;c., JOSEPH P. SWAN.</p>
        <p>11, Scarisbrick St., Southport, December 30th, 1907.</p>
        <p>Is Interest Robbery? Sir,-1 note Mr. C. E. Parker's reply to me in your last week's issue. He recognises my courtesy, I return the compliment. In my letter of the 21st ult. I tried to answer Mr. Parker's query : is capital the enemy of labour? He says he cannot follow me, and cannot see how society is divided into two great classes. "It must have evolved out of Mr. Reeve's imagination," Mr. Parker says" Let us clear the ground." That was my intention, but some debris has since fallen in the form of the Warrington Savings Bank report and Mr. Parker cannot climb over it to reach my ethical and economic analysis of the operations of capital in society to-day. Mr. Parker has dropped the query" Is capital the enemy of labour ?'" as there is no reply to my state- ments of last week. Any way," I don't believe in top storey arguments "as Mr. Parker says Socialists would have him discuss, so I must go to the root of the question again. 15,465 depositors own £4o7,sB7—roughly speaking £27 each. The population of Warrington is about 72,000. Does Mr. Parker think the other 56,535 of the people in Warrington are comfortably well off because 15,465 have £27 each? Divide the amount invested between the whole population and they will have just over £5 10s. each. One month's unemploy- ment, result, bankruptcy ! Mr. Parker asks are not these investors by getting interest on their savings " robbers "? To be strictly ethical they are but I acknowledge at the same time one man's right with another, be he workingman or idler under our present system, to place his money in a bank, but my desire is to see the system changed. Where does the division in wealth and society come in ? Mr. Chiozza Money says in 1904 the wage earners numbered 15,000,000 (and of these 1,000,000 were camp followers of the industrial army), and the average wage of the above class was 20s. 6d. per week. Taking the average family of five persons they have 4s. 1 2-sd. to exist upon for a week. The cost to maintain them in the Workhouse would be about Os. per week. Mr. Money gives other figures. Under Socialism every human being will have for use or consumption everything his labour has created or its equivalent in some other product. In my reply to Mr. Parker's problem of the horse and cart he does not think my first sentence (" the value of an article is its cost of production ") is strictly true. Mr. Parker submits another economic problem for a solution, but there is a difference in the two problems. In the first Mr. Parker asks would it be robbery to charge interest for lending the horse and cart ? In the last one, "has not the inventor of the sewing machine to be paid for his labour ?" Certainly he should be, but the present system does not decide where the payments for invention shall end, and the cost of the invention is placed in the form of a tax on the article produced. The productive capacity of the labourer plus machinery has not guaranteed to him or her a living wage, but on the other hand has brought about a system of horrible sweating in the form of over-work and under-pay. And now I most say I have not sufficienttime at my disposal to continue the correspondence with strict attention to the economic details of how to fix value. So far as I am able to judge, I think the whole of the correspon- dence will be an endless display of intellectual gymnastics and the logical outcome " we agree to differ." Mr. Parker says he has read Socialistic literature that has come within his reach for the last 30 years. If he has read John Ruskin's " Unto this last,"and Karl Marx "Capital," I don't know what the light is he is feeling for. If he has not read them I iespectfully submit the above works for Mr. Parker study.—Yours very truly' GEORGE REEVES. Stockton Heath, January Ist, 1008.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>TIIE RED LION IN.—At the Warrington County Petty Sessions, on Wednesday, before Messrs. John White (in the chair), Charles White and F. A. Frost, William Davies was granted permission to sell at the Red Lion Inn, Penketh,. in the place of his brother George Davies.—Mr. T. S. Steel said that temporary authority had been granted by that Court on December 18. There were no objections on the part of the police, and he had very satisfactory raferences. GUILD Scat.—The second social of the session was hold on Tuesday evening in the Wesleyan school- room, when 130 were present. A good entertainment was provided, the chair being taken by Mrs. James Dodds. Games were indulged in until ten o'clock when a hot-pot supper, provided by the ladies of the social committee, was much enjoyed. The room had been beautifully illuminated and decorated by Mr. John Dunabirt. At eleven o'clock most of the party made their way to the chapel for the watchnight service which was conducted by the Rev. E. J. Gawne. LIBERAL CLUB.—The final of the billiard handicap was played off the other evening at the above club. Over 30 members had entered the handicap, which is the second of the season. The }ballots were W. Lightfoot and S. Tunstall. W. Lightfoot proved the winner and received the first prize, cue and case given by Messrs. Orme and Sons, Manchester. Four other prizes given by the Club were won by 2, S. Tanstall ; ,F. Sampson ; 4,D. Mather; 5, E. Darlington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>NEW SYMPHONY ORCHESTRA (London). _ Forthcoming Concerts in Liverpool.</head>
        <p>NEW SYMPHONY ORCHESTRA (London). _ Forthcoming Concerts in Liverpool.</p>
        <p>Interest in the concerts of the London New Symphony Orchestra to be given in the Philharmonic Hall, in the afternoon and evening of Satin day next, the 11th of January, is fast maturing in all parts of the city and district. The appetite of the musical com- munity for orchestral concerts has presumably been whetted by the recent visit of the Richter Orchestra. This phase of Liverpool's musical taste is too valuable a quantity to be allowed naturally to languish for any length of time. Hence it is good to find Messrs. Rush- worth and Dreaper stimulating the aspirations in this direction by providing the public of the city with such rare opportunities of gratifying the desire. In the London New Symphony Orchestra the public of Liverpool will have a chance of estimating what the opinion of the metropolis is worth upon a matter of this kind. At the perform- ances of the Orchestra in London the audiences were rapturously enthusiastic, and moreover the press of the capital waxed nnrestrainedly eulogistic over the superb effects of which this splendid combination of musicians is capable. The personality of the conductor, Mr. Thomas Beecham, is one of outstanding repute in the best musical circles of both this country and the conti- nent. His orchestra numbers nearly 100 players. With such wonderful power and artistic acumen does be lead and inspire his forces that their interpretations are considered to be par excel/race beyond criticism. On the soundness of this opinion Liverpool people will on the 11th of January have an opporturnity of judging. Mr. Beecham is not only a conductor, hut he is also a com- poser of distinction. Besides two complete operas he has written many important works. He and his orchestra in the revival of rich musical gems of the Eighteenth Century have rendered good service to the cause of musical art. The programmes for the concerts on the 11th of January are certain to be of the most absorbing interest, intrinsically as well as historically. At the afternoon concert an auxiliary attraction of surpassing enjoyment will be the performance of Backhaus, the celebrated pianist. A highly congenial element of variety at the evening concert will be the appearance of Mdme. Ella Russell, the favourite prima donna, and Mr. Jocks Szigeti, the brilliant Hungarian violinist. The plans are now open, and seats may ho booked at the office of the Concert Direction, Messrs. Rushworth and Dreaper, 21, Basnett-street, Liverpool.</p>
        <p>For Chronic Chest Complaint, Woods' Great Peppermint Cure.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
