      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>NEW YEAR. HOW IT WAS WELGOMED AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>THE NEW YEAR. HOW IT WAS WELGOMED AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The dawn of the year 1903 was welcomed at Warrington with the usual rejoicings. On the stroke of twelve o'clock the " buzzers " at the various works were sounded, and the church bells rang forth a merry peal. In accordance with a long-standing custom, watchnight services were held at many places of worship, and the last few moments of the old year were spent in silent prayer. When 1003 was ushered in, hearty good wishes were exchanged and people wended their way homewards to " let the .New Year in." New Year's Day was practically a general holiday, and numerous festive gatherings were held. The weather was as seasonable as could be desired, for there was a frosty touch in the atmosphere and a penetrating east wind. The spell of bright sunshine experienced in the afternoon made it pleasant to be out of doors. At the theatres there were special holiday performances, and large houses were the order</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>NEW YEAR TEA PARTIES.</head>
        <p>NEW YEAR TEA PARTIES.</p>
        <p>POOR CHILDREN'S TREAT..</p>
        <p>A free tea was given on New Year's Day by the members of the Wycliffe women's meeting to 600 poor children. Bags were given to each child containing a mince pie, a thick slice of currant and seed cake and bread and butter, together with cans of tea. After an hour spent in various ways each child received an apple and an orange on going home. The Committee desire to thank publicly all who have so generously subscribed to the treat. NEWSBOYS' TREA I'. _</p>
        <p>The annual tre-aigiv-e-ii-tOChe-ne.,Wsb-o-ys of Warrington by Mr. Samuel Wakefield, of the Central Hotel, took place on Thursday afternoon. The boys to the number of 200, were each presented with a meat pie and a threepenny piece, and the generosity of Mr. Wakefield, who unfortunately was not able to be present, was greatly appreciated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>SOCf AL AT GOLBORNE-STREET,</head>
        <p>SOCf AL AT GOLBORNE-STREET,</p>
        <p>The annual social in connection with the Golborne- street Baptist Bible Class was held in the schoolroom on New Year's Day. About 60 members and friends were present and the affair proved one of the most suc- cessful on record. The evening was spent in games and musical items. During an interval refreshments were served, after which a short musical programme was given. Miss Bella Lee tastefully played a pianoforte solo " Jerusalem the Golden " with variations. Miss Jessie Woodland's sweet voice was heard to advantage in "The Gift," for which she received a well-deserved encore and sang very prettily " Songs of the children." Mr. W. Tynan gave an artistic rendering of" Evening Song," and in response to a hearty encore he sang " Mary." The remainder of the evening was spent in games, &amp;c. Mr. T. Barnes proposed, and Mr. S. Fernley seconded, a vote of thanks to all who had helped to make the evening such a success.</p>
        <p>CHILDREN'S TRET AT COCKHEDGE MISSION. On New Year's Day over 300 of the poorest children in the district were entertained at the Wycliffe Cock- hedge Mission, Brick-street. Through the generosity of a number of prominent townspeople, a splendid tea was provided, to which the little ones did full justice. After tea a most interesting lime-light exhibition was given by the Rev. H. Wall, assisted by Mr. A. Ashton which delighted the audience, whose behaviour was excellent and interest well maintained. At the close of I the entertainment each child was given a small cob of bread, an orange and a packet of sweets. The delighted faces of the children (many of whom appeared to be sadly neglected) were sufficient reward to the workers, and justified the gifts of the subscribers. The only regret of the committee is that the work cannot be carried out on a much larger scale, as hundreds had to be refused. The arrangements were in the hands of Mr. J. Phcenix, Miss Clayton, and Mr. Fred Morrall (hon. sec.) WYCLIFFE SUNDAY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The senior -saia-ar--; "Tai-WyCliffe--SLi-cia-y—school had their annukl tea party on Thursday evening and spent an exceedingly pleasant time. About 100 sat down to an excellent tea and afterwards a concert was held. The Pastor (Rev. John Yonge) presided and distributed prizes for regular attendance, the number being an advance on last year. He expressed the hope that next year the number would be much larger, and suggested to the teachers that prizes should be given to scholars who were " half-timers," as in many cases it was impos- sible for children to attend in the morning. An enter- tainment was then given, the programme being varied and interesting. It included songs by Miss Armstrong, violin solo by Stanley Poole, pianoforte duet by Bertha and Elsie Weir, two dialogues by members of IMr. W. Gauld's class and Mr. W. Beswick's class, a conjuring exhibition by " Professor" Lloyd, and a song " Caller Herrin' " by members of MiS3 Maybury's class. Mr. T. F. Wallington was the accompanist. At the close a vote of thanks was accorded to all who had assisted on the proposition of Mr. W. Brown (superintendent). ST. CLEMENT'S CONGREGATIONAL TEA. PRESENTATION TO THE REV. A. A. MARKHAM. The annual congregational tea in connectioa with St. Clement's Mission, Bank-street, was held in the school- room on Wednesday evening. About 130 sat down to a substantial tea, the tables being presided over by the ladies of the congregation.—After full justice had been done to the good things provided, an interesting presentation was made to the Rev. A. A.Markham, vicar of St. Jude's, Liverpool, and formerly curate-in-charge of St. Clement's, who is about to be married, and is leaving the diocese of Liverpool to take charge of the important living of Grimsby. After complimentary speeches from the Rev. T. W. Barber and the Rector, Mrs. Barber presented Mr. Mailtham with a handsome silver rose bowl. The Rev. A. A. Markham expressed his thanks in warm terms. The tables were then cleared and dancing was indulged in, interspersed with songs by Misses L. Ross and N. Martin, and Messrs. Smith, A. Berry, Jesse Gray. and C. J. Sheath. Miss G. Ross and Mr. T. W. Davis acted as accompanists and Mr. Jesse Gray proved an efficient M.G. The arrangements for the presenta- tion were very ably carried out by Miss Boardman at the request of the curate-in-charge and sidesmen of the mission.</p>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S SUNDAY SCHOOL. The New Year parties in connection with this solicit&gt; have proved a great SUCCeSs. The senior scholars were entertained in the Kendrick-street school on New Year's Day, the attendance numbering about 100. An inter- esting feature of the proceedings was the distribution of prizes by the Mayoress (Mrs. Smethurst) to fifty of the scholars for regular attendance. The Rev. W. Bracecamp presided and amongst others present were ; Mrs. Bracecamp, Rev. E. E. Peters, Mr. J. H. Bankes, Mr. J. H. Smethurst, Mr. W. T. Flood, and the Sunday School teachers. The Vicar urged all present to be regular in their attendance at Sunday school and to do their utmost to induce others to attend. In distributing the prizes, the Mayoress expressed the special pleasure which it afforded her to assist at a gathering in con- nection with St. Paul's Church with which she had been so closely identified. A vote of thanks was accorded to the Mayoress on the proposition of the Rev. E. E. Peters seconded by Mr. Flood. During the evening dancing was indulged in, Mr. 'l'. Taylor provid- ing the music. Mr. W. T. Flood acted as M.O. On Thursday evening the children between the ages of eight and twelve had their party and 150 prizes were dis- tributed. List evening the youngsters under twelve years of age were entertained and 100 prizes were given out. BRICK- iTREET INDEPENDENT METHODIST CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The annual tea pari-ri;-C'Oti-r-iection with Brick-street Independent Methodist Church was held in the handsome Church buildings on New Year's Day. The function was a most successful one and everything passed off exceed- ingly well One hundred and fifty sat down to an excellent tea the tables being presided over by the following : Mesdames E. Roberts, Hibbert, Bennett, Shaw, Barton, Ellison, Swindles, Wright, T. Gallon and Misses. M. Fletcher,Alice Taylor, Elsie Wright. After tea a concert was held, the president of the church, Mr. Edward Roberts, presiding. Mr. Warburton presented the annual church report which stated that numerically they were in the same position as last year, some having fallen away whilst others had joined their ranks. The general condition of all branches was a little below normal, with which they ought not to be satisfied. The Sunday morning service was no better attended than last year and they hoped to be able to report a bettor condition of things next year. The Christian Endeavour Society was in a very fair condition and had done some very good and useful work ends great many of the young members had been recommended for church membership and were now actively engaged with them. The Society had also been the means of raising snbstanti il contributions to the Home and Foreign Mission.. Reierenca was made to the Sectional Efforts, which resulted in .288 Bs. Id. being subscribed towards the £2OO extinction fund. With that they were looking forward to a bazaar which would be held in March, in the hope that the £2OO required would be cleared off. The church income for the year had been .2115 Bs. 2d., which was exceeded by an expenditure of £l3O 2s. 2d., leaving a deficit of £l4 14s.</p>
        <p>Mr. WILLIAM YATES then presented the annual report of the school, which was very satisfactory. The teachers numbered twenty-five, and the scholars between 450 and 500. The Band of Hope was in a very good condition, having a membership of 300. The weekly classes had been the means of raising .£9 Is. 51d. towards the Church fund.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN in the course of his address said a great deal depended upon the work of the school and church, and the church and school depended upon them- selves. If the work they did was very satisfactory they ought to thank God as He was always ready to help them. What they required vvas perseverance and love and then they would prosper. The band of hope was a very useful society and it was always able to hand over something to the church fund. He appealed to them to help to clear the debt off the church and he hoped £2OO would be raised in a very short time. He concluded by wishing them a bright, happy and prosperous New Year. During the evening songs were rendered in a pleasing</p>
        <p>manner by Misses R. Peacock, A. Griffiths and Mr. Corrin.Mr.Bates gave violin solos while Mr. A.Grounds created great merriment by his humorous contributions. A vote of thanks was accorded to the Chitirman and artistes.</p>
        <p>BOLD-STREET WESLEYAN CHURCH. MR. E. J. GUEST'S INTERESTING ' RE AINISCENCES. THE EDUCATION QUESTION. Bold-street Wesleyan Schoolroom was crowded on New Year's Day, the occasion being the annual Con- gregational gathering in connection with the Sunday school. One hundred and fifty sat down to tea and afterwards a concert was held. The Rev. Thomas Miller presided and was supported by Mr. E. J. Guest, 8.A., J.P., Mr. P.H. Harris and Mr. F. Light (hon. sec.). The Rev. THOMAS MILLER spoke of the importance of Sunday schools and compared them with the pulpit. The minister could only get at one side of the truth whereas the Sunday school teacher could make more impression because he had the same audience every week. He had preached a sermon sometime since and he got a letter which told him that he did not preach the truth. Mr. Miller thought his correspondent could not have heard distinctly or intelligently. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>_ REPORT OF THE YEAR'S WORK. Mr. F. LIGHT in his repert referred to the removal during the past twelve months of the Rev. J. Manning, Mr. Harris, one of the superintendents, and two of the teachers Mr. and Miss Sterling,paying a high tribute to their work in the Sunday school. The report showed that there were on the books 210 scholars, 27 teachers and officers, while there was room for nearly twice that number. The average morning attendance was 83 in the morning and that in the afternoon was 157. the teachers averaged nine in the morniug and 18 in the afternoon. Such figures were slightly lower than last ye ar.The school suffered from the lack of teachers and they were bound to draw attention to the meagre support given to the Sunday school work by members of the church. The year just closed contained many good things, b ut they felt that as a school they were not doing what they were capable of. The library possessed a good stock of 700 books, but they had only about 20 readers, which they could understand in view of the facilities for read- ing afforded by the free libraries. Tie missionary collectors' Secretary reported a total collection of L'lo Bs. for the year. Mr. Dan Ostle, the school registrar, had attended to his duties in a most unselfish manner, whilst thanks were also tendered to Mr. Cooper (treasurer), Mr. Doncaster (librarian), and to all who bad in any way helped them during the past year. The report concluded with reference to the Children's Sunday last October, and the Sunday school anniversary last June.</p>
        <p>11Ir. F.H.llaßras said they had every facility in their Sunday schools to teach both sides of the truth. It appeared to him that their teachers who lived the greatest distance from the school were the most regular in their attendance, and those who only had a few minutes walk before them very seldom came. He thought that if all the teachers lived a long way from school and if that circumstance would induce them to come more regularly, the further they lived away the better.</p>
        <p>MR. GUEST'S REMINISCENCES. Mr. E. J. GuEsr, in the course of an interesting address,said:—lt is now more than 40 years since I first stood on the platform of the Bold-street Sunday school anniversary tea meeting. I remember it very distinctly as it was my first attempt at a speech. My subject was "The wisdom of aiming at perfection." The meeting was held in the old Buttermarket schooLl do not know that I have ever since then addressed this annual gather- ing and when I received your invitation on Sunday I hesitated to comply but on second thoughts decided to do so knowing that not much would be required of me. As I look back upon the past my memory carries me to the year 1855 with great vividness. Permit me to indulge in a few reminiscences. My life has been greatly influenced by the Wesleyan ministers that have been in this Circuit. It was due to Mr. Payne coming in 1855 that I was sent to Wesley College in 1836. Mr. Hannah and Mr. Mellor were here from 1860 to 1863. To Mr. Hannah I owes gre tt deal of gratitude for the love he gave me of Biblical exegesis. He was a man of considerable power of interpretation of Holy Scripture. Mr. Mellor was a man given to antiquarian research but at the same time was a preacher who probed the conscience and made the terrors of the law stand out before one in great vividness. From 1863 to 1866. Mr. Morris was a diamond in the rough and preached the gospel with much rugged eloquence. His colleague Mr. Woolley was a man of great refinement but was not so forceful in the pulpit, but his prayers were a perfect liturgy and his weekly expositions of the Psalms were a great treat. These two ministers took opposite sides in the great American Civil War. Mr. Morris was a Confederate and Mr.Woolley a Federal. When Abraham Lincoln was assassinated in 1865 Mr. Morris hearing of it on the spur of the moment said very incautiously "Thank God for that." It was an unguarded and unfortunate remark and cost him a good deal of adverse criticism. It was due to Mr. Woolley that I decided to go to Cambridge in 1866.-1863-9. Mr. Little was a gentleman who drove his own carriage. His colleague IL R. Burton was the hardest working visitor we ever had in the Circuit and to him was largely due the build- ing of Silver-street Hall as it was then called.--1872-5. Mr. Haine and Mr. Berry had much to do with the erection of 1385.7. w-road Chanel.-1875. Mr. Blanch- flower was truly a man of God and died in 1877, in the Circuit. The Rev. E. Lloyd Jones you all know and his power In the pulpit. Time would fail me to speak of G. Walker, Wilkinson, Colman, Agar Beet, Rippon. MaTurk and others. Much as these later ministers influenced me, the earlier ones stand out before me as having more power to move me. I was younger and more susceptible. As far as I remember the meeting to which I referred toox place in the year 1866. January, when J. H. Parkinson, Plinston, Farnell were leading men in the school. Miss Clarke and others also worked in the school. Of local preachers, Lamplough, Ford,Clarke, Richardson Moss and others were moving spirits • of prayer leaders, Johnny Harding, Isaac Mellor, E. B. Bowser, Creighton, Robert Cook ; of officers, James Dutton, James Carter, John Guest, Mr. Sanderson, Dr. Smith and others took the lead. Those were the days when I thought no Church could compare with the Methodist Church and no Chapel in Methodism could surpass Bold-street. To-day I may be broader in my sympathies. I can see more clearly the good in other Churches but I still think that Methodism has much work to do in the world. In connection with this or any other church there is no work more important than that done in the Sunday school. As I look back on my own experience I can clearly see that I was influenced for good very early in life, and as with me so with all young people the time to get hold of them is in early life. I cannot say that any age is too early. As the years go on it eeems to Me that Sunday school work will increase in importance. How the vexed question of Elementary Education is to be settled is a problem of great diffi- culty. So difficult indeed is it that we may yet see the solution in Secular Education. If this be so the religious education of the children must be confided more and more to the parents and to the Sunday school teacher. To my mind parents are the proper teachers of their own children. Every father should be the Prophet, Priest and King of his own children. But unfortunately there are too many I parents who are incapable of such work. They them- selves need guidance and teaching. The work of the Sunday school teacher will still be needed, and must be taken up with more seriousness and thoroughness than ever. We see how carefully teachers are trained for their work in day schools. Equal if not superior train- ing is needed to fit the'Sunday school teacher for his or her work. The great essential for this work is experi- ence of Divine things in the heart. But besides this especially in these days we need a careful study of the Word of God. We have come to realise the necessity for training the local preachers, and we have Chile College for them. I think the time is drawing nigh when Sunday school teachers mast be required to show some careful preparation for their important work. I do not know bow this is to be accomplished, seeing that all such work is voluntary ; but surely our young men and women will be ready to devote some time to earnest and systematic training to qualify them for their all important duties. Allow me to conclude by enforcing the therae of my first speech "The duty and aiming at perfection." (Applause.) During the evening a concert was contributed to by the following :--Messrs. Warburton, baritone ; B. Plumpton, bass ; T. Clayton, tenor ; Bold-street Quartette Party ; Misses Taylor, Warburton and Plimpton; whilst Mr. A. Grounds, as usual, kept the audience highly amused. The Rev. T. MILLER proposed a vote of thanks to the artistes.lVlr.FßANK EfICKmaN in responding said he was very glad a vote of thanks had been tendered to the artistes because they had taken very great pains in preparing for the function and not only that but they were quite willing to help in the entertainment. A vote of thanks was accorded to the secretary (Mr. F. Light) for the able manner the report had been pre- pared and a similar compliment was accorded to the speakers. -</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening a concert was provided by the children when dialogues, songs and recitations, and a magic lantern entertainment created great amusement after which games were indulged in.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, January 4, 1908.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>WHIST DRIVE.—A highly successful whist drive was held in the Church School, Padgate, on New Year's Eve. Admirable arrangements had been made by a committee consisting of Mr. and Mrs. Osborne, Mr. and Mrs. Pendlebury, Mr. Ernest Atkinson, Mr. Percy Bennett, with Miss Short as secretary. The attendance numbered fifty, and the affair was most enjoyable. The winners of the prizes were :—Gentlernen : Ist, Mr. J. Howes, junr. 2nd, Mr. Allen : consolation, Mr. W. 5, Howard. Ladies : Ist, M. Maginn ; 2nd, Mrs. Page: consolation, Miss Fair.—After the whist drive dancing was indulged in until the early hours of the morning. PAROCHIAL TEA.—The annual parochial tea in con- nection with Christ Church was held on Thursday evening in the schools. After tea an entertainment was given by Mr. Arthur Rix, of Southport (drawing-room humorist), and Miss Doris Briggs (a charming young elocutionist). The function closed with a dance. COTTA.OE HOMES.—The officers of the above institu tion gave a party on Thursday evening and the prooead ings, which were largely attended, proved thoroughly enjoyable. Dancing was indulged in and tte function was voted a complete success.</p>
        <p>For Bronchial Coughs take Woods' Great ePppermint Cure. 2/9.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>JIM CARLYON.</head>
        <p>JIM CARLYON.</p>
        <p>significant phrases. . . . The little bed sitting. room, it appeared, which represented his home, would be wanted for another guest very shortly. • . lie owed Mrs. Fisk some hundred dollars —and he had just parted with his last two for American Beauty roses. . . . And the roses were withering in their box, what time Miss Grace , Hartmann was dining with the Secretary of the ' Navy in distant Washington. "Very well, Mrs. Fisk," said Jim, as pleasantly as he could. "Ls that a letter ? " he added. I "Looks like a bill."</p>
        <p>BY ELLA HEPWORTH DIXON, Luthor of "The Story of a Modern Woman," "The Luck of Jane Armour," "The Disenchantment of Diana," "His Sweet Disaster," &amp;c.</p>
