Then he would finish and the majestic footman had appeared with tea the elaborate "story" he had concocted for the and lamps by the time these two young persons had Eagle around the recent divorce among the Four begun to hold rational converse again. Hundred. . . . Grace essayed to assume her most motherly, It must be admitted that this was the lowest patronising air as else poured him out his tea. hour of our hero's fortunes. Try as lie would, "And now tell rue what you've been doing," she Jim Carlyon could not snake the intermittent said. "What have you been writing? You stipend which he earned on the Eagle do. Ho know," she added, laughing, "that mother won't owed small sums in many quarters ; he wanted a new dress suit, and he couldn't get it. Heaven have the I\rew York Eagle in the house. Ever since we came back from Europe she says it's so knew how much he owed for Isis board 1 Yet in spite of his recent disappointment, his fatigue, his shockingly American. It's only now and again, with good luck. that rin.able to smuggle a copy into my sense of professional failure, there was always with bedroom." him a vision of a gracious girl with radiant face "Oh, nothing much," said the young man, and upstanding figure, the new type of American "Just finishing up my engagement on the Eagle. woman, a Gibson girl without her arrogance. And 4By the way," he added, looking at the alert, eager this exquisite creature, this Grace Hartmann, who face of the girl on the sofa opposite, "I'll read you reigned supreme over her social set with a my latest article. It will appear to-marrow." beneficent sway, was, he was aware, by no means "Is it one of those ridiculous, worked-up things," indifferent to him. demanded Grace, laughing; "all head-lines and It was the landlady herself who presently scandal, with a pound of sensation and an ounce appeared, with a covered dish containing his of truth ?" dinner, and a letter in her hand. She was a “You'll see," replied Jim, pulling sonic type- person of apologetic manners, dressed always in written sheets out of his pocket. frayed glace silk, and was afflicted with weak "Startling Romance in the Peerage of Great eyelids. Britain." "Sit down, Mrs. Fisk," said Jim, springing up "That's in enormous type, I suppose ?" inquired from his note-books and gallantly placing a chair. Grace. mildly amused. But Mrs. Fisk declined ta be seated. For the "W hacking : " time young man announced, with just next seven minutes an interminable stream of talk professional pride. ''' Now, listen: 'George Sackville flowed from her lips. . . . Out of the torrent ,Dunbar, twelfth Baron Dunbar, one of whose Jim, listening fitfully, plucked one or two ancestors commanded the Parliamentary army at</p>
        <p>the battle of Naseby, while another went to Virginia, married a young American lady, and founded a family, has just died at his ranche, near Monterey, California. The deceased peer, who was sixty-nine years of age, leaves no children. He never married, and took no steps to prove or assume his take. He is understood to have amassed a considerable fortune. His favourite hobby was fruit-farming. Lord Dunbar—who was known only as Mr. Dunbar—was one of the pioneers in California. Emigrating to 'Frisco in '49, he at once saw the vast future connected with the City of the Golden Gate, and bought land, which has since been covered with palatial mansions. His relations in America are very few, for his younger brother—who would have inherited the peerage had he survived him—had only one child of his marriage. This brother practised for many years as a doctor of medicine in Easthampton, Virginia. It is understood that the two brothers, the late peer and the doctor of medicine, were not on good terms, owing to differences of political opinion during the American Civil War. Tbe late Baron Dunbar, who was a noted Democrat and Abolitionist, gave his services to the Federal cause, while Dr. Dunbar, of Easthampton, naturally sided with other English-descended families in the South, and was attached for more than a year, till exposure and hardships brought on a severe illness, to one of General Stonewall Jackson's ambulances. It is a specially romantic feature of the case that the heir to the title and the accumulated wealth of his uncle is at present in New York earning his living in a somewhat humble capacity." "In a humble capacity—that will be in big, black, leaded type, won't it, Jim?" said Grace, laughing.</p>
        <p>"-Extra big," said Jim. "The public just loves a contrast. Something exaggerated." "And who's the lucky young man ? " said Grace,j indifferently, who felt she ought to take a polite° interest in •Jim's article for the Eagle. But just as she was diving for the plate of cucumber sand- wiches on the lowest rung of the tea-table, the door opened and a stout figure, adorned with much ermine and crowned by a French bonnet, appeared. For an instant Jim and his prospective mother- in-law faced each other. Grace trembled. Such moments were terrible.</p>
        <p>"My door Lord Dunbar!" cried the lady, who was now wreathed in smiles. "My dear boy . . . My warmest congratulations. You deserve it all. So young, so clever, and, oh—you know it, you naughty boy—so attractive!" "Mother—what do you mean?" gasped the girl. "Are you all as mad as hatters ? What do you mean by calling Jim Lord Dunbatr ? And, by the bye, I may as well tell you I'm going to—in fact, we're engaged." "It's quite true, Grace," said the young man, blushing. "I dropped the Dunbar when I began to write. Canyon is my mother's name ; and, somehow, I thought it prettier. You're such a fiery little democrat that I thought you might hate the title—and that's why I didn't tell you before I asked you to marry me." Lord Dunbar's tone was so contrite and humble, and so different to anything that the girl had ewer associated with the impecunious aristocrats whom she had met in Europe, that she fairly laughed outright. Jim laughed too, and in that moment their future was settled.</p>
        <p>r Dear Jatnes," announced Mrs. Hartmann, "will stay and share our humble pot-luck. My precious child must have some opportunity of seeing her future husband." ic Well, I don't want to be Lady Dunbar, the least bit in the world," grumbled Grace in the Empire-room after dinner. "If you knew the awful little men I refused over there, just because I always said I wouldn't sell myself Mother and father haven't the least idea. I always kept it dark."</p>
        <p>"You're not selling yourself, as it happens," said Jim, loftily. "The coronet's thrown in. It's a sort of ornamental extra, on your becoming Mrs. James Canyon." "Oh, well, if we think of it like that," said Grace, with melting eyes and adorable, lifted lips. Meanwhile, downstairs, Mrs. Hermann Hartmann was haranguing her husband. "I always said," argued the lady, "that we'd spent far too much time and money running Grace round Europe." "Did you, my dear? I—l--hardly remember that."</p>
        <p>"Hermann, give me America all the time. What's the matter with a city like New York, where a young man on one of the papers turns out to be a Scotch peer? And, when I think of it, how sick at my stomach I always am on the sea What, I say, is the matter with New York ? " "What, indeed I" meekly replied her spouse.</p>
        <p>[THE END.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Important Business.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Town Council will be held on Tuesday evening when the f&amp;lowing among other business will be transacted :</p>
        <p>ELECTION OP AN ALDERMAN. An alderman will be elected to fill the vacancy caused by the death of the late Alderman Fairclough.</p>
        <p>SUGGESTED MUNICIPAL COAL SUPPLY. Councillor Stott will move : In the opinion of this Council it would be in the interests of the community for the Municipal Corpora- tions to be granted powers to undertake the supply of coal to the inhabitants of the various boroughs, and in order to further this object, the Town Clerk be hereby instructed to circularise each Town and City Council in Great Britain asking them to pass a similar resolu- tion, and to forward a copy of the same to the Associa- tion of Municipal Corporations, with a request that the Association shall urge the Government to pass legis- lation, giving powers to Municipal Corporations to undertake the sale of coal, and the purchase of coal mines.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>WILDERSPOOL-ROAD.</head>
        <p>WILDERSPOOL-ROAD.</p>
        <p>The Street Improvement Committee have resolved that the premises at the corner of Knutsford-road and Wilderspool-causeway be taken down and the widening approved by the Council carried out. LADY SANITARY INSPECTOR.</p>
        <p>The Health Committee recommends that Miss E. G. Pekes be appointed Female Sanitary Inspector, subject to three months' probation,at a salary of £BO per annum, with an allowance of £5 per annum for uniform.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-01-04_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>PROPOSED ENLARGEMENT OF THE COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>PROPOSED ENLARGEMENT OF THE COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Re-Arrangement of Wards.</p>
        <p>The General Purposes Committee has resolved- that the following representatives of each respective Ward be constituted a sub-committee to report on the re-arrangement of the Wards and the increase of the number of Councillors :—Town Hall Ward, Mr. Councillor Lord ; Whitecross Ward, Mr. Councillor Evans ; Bewsey Ward, Mr. Councillor Toole ' • Orford Ward, Mr. Councillor Wilkinson ; St. John's Ward, Mr. Councillor Fulton-Smith; Fairfield Ward, Mr. Alderman Ellison ; Howley Ward, Mr. Alderman Evans • St. Austin's Ward, Mr. Alderman Tinnion ; LatChford Ward, Mr. Councillor Pemberton. It has been resolved that St. John's Ward be divided into two polling districts, with Lytbgoes-lane as the dividing line. . . _</p>
        <p>The following information has been compiled respect- ing the present composition of the Wards:— _</p>
