        <p>• Poor Children's Treat. MR. GROSFIELD, M.P. In the Salvation Army Barracks, Academy-street, on Monday afternoon, Messrs. Bennett and Sons, of the Derby Confectionery Works Lovely-lane, gave a treat To Visit Warrington on February sth. to over a thousand poor children, each oi whom was the recipient of a packet of confectionery. The ' packets were distributed by Ensign and Mrs. Oliver After staying some weeks in Penzance, Cornwall, Clarke, Adjutant Tuffin, Ensign Gibson; Captain Mr. and Mrs. A. H. Crosfield have now gone to Kenny, and P.C. Hughes. There was at first a great Cannes in continuation of their honeymoon. deal of excitement and scrambliniT, but at length all They have definitely promised to visit Warrington were served and the children went home delighted. A. on Wednesday, February 51h, and Parr Hall has been few days ago Ensign Clarke and Mrs. Clarke gave a engaged for the reception which it is proposed the dinner of roast beef and plum pudding in the same Member and his wife should hold. Occasion will be place to 254 poor children, each of whom was also taken to make a presentation to Mr. Crosfield from the supplied with an orange. Ensign Clarke will be Liberal Party in Warrington as a token of warm pleased to undertake similar commissions to that given esteem and expressive of the best wishes for the •by Messrs. Bennett during the winter months on future happiness of himself and his wife. ' behalf of the poor. , CANDIED LEMON PEEL 41-d. per lb. at As fILEV'S.—Advt. LINSEED COMPOUND,' Trade Mark of Kay's Compound Essence of Linseed for Coughs &amp;Colds.--Advt. Ticket-of-Leave Man In Trouble. I At the Borough Police Court on Satur lay, Thomas Troy, of no settled address, was charged with being a person out on licence, and failing to report himself to the police.—lnspector Foster said on November 27th I prisoner called at the Police Station and notified his , intention of taking up his residence in Warrington. , He said he was going to the Workhouse to get into hospital. He went there, and the day afterwards he took his discharge and went to Liverpool. He did not make any notification to the police that he was , going to change his address. He came back to I Warrington on Friday, and said he was again going to ' reside in Warrington. That was the first time they , had heard of him since November 28th.- Superintend- lent M inaghan said prisoner knew well the regulations, as he had previously been sentenced to six months' ' imprisonment for failing to report himself.—The Chief ; Constable informed the Bench that prisoner had 47 Iprevious convictions against him.—Prisoner said i 1 I was " very hard for him, because he had no settled address."—The Bench sent prisoner to gaol for six months. PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. REVOLUTION INELECTRIC LIGHTING.—" " Electric Lamps reduce your current Bill one half. —Agent : J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street, War- rington.—Advt.</p>
